\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Update\b0\par\par\uc2 1,560 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2011\par 02:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 US Dollar\par \par \uc2 The yen surged to a record high against the dollar Friday, as measures devised by Japan to mitigate the effects of a strong yen prompted traders to question the country's resolve to halt its rise. On Friday, Japan's cabinet approved the expansion of a nearly Y12 trillion plan to help companies contend with the rising yen. In practice, analysts viewed the government's efforts as a tacit acknowledgment that they are increasingly resigned to managing a muscular yen instead of fighting it. Late Friday, the euro was at $1.3899 from $1.3780 late Thursday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y76.19 from Y76.81.\par \par \uc2 Euro\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to consolidate in higher range. Supported by weaker USD sentiment, hopes that Europe's leaders will unveil comprehensive plan to resolve region's debt crisis on Wednesday, even though plans to increase financial firepower of EUR440 billion EFSF not finalized at Sunday's summit and private sector lenders balk at proposed 60% write-down on their Greek bond holdings. EUR/USD also supported by sovereign demand for euro; but gains tempered by euro-zone economic growth slowdown -- German Ifo business climate index dropped to 16-month low of 106.4 in October from 107.5 in September (though slightly better than 106.3 forecast). Data focus: 0730 GMT Germany October flash PMI, 0800 GMT euro-zone October flash PMI, 0900 GMT euro-zone August new industrial orders. EUR/USD daily positive-biased as MACD bullish, stochastics revert to bullish mode at overbought. Resistance at 1.3902 (Wednesday's high), then at 1.3915 (Oct. 17 high, near 55-day moving average); breach would target 1.3936 (Sept. 15 high), then 1.4091 (confluence of 100-day and 200-day moving averages)and 1.4286 (Sept. 6 high). Support at 1.3822 (hourly chart), then at 1.3704 (Friday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, exposing downside to 1.3655-1.3652 band (Thursday's low-Tuesday's low), then 1.3582 (Oct. 12 low), 1.3565 (Oct. 11 low) and 1.3346 (Oct. 10 low).\par \par \uc2 Japanese Yen\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to consolidate after hitting record low 75.78 on EBS Friday as markets realized Japan's Y12 trillion package of measures is meant to cope with, not combat, the strong JPY. USD/JPY also undermined by Japan exporter sales, negative USD sentiment after Fed Vice Chairman Yellen offered gloomy assessment of U.S. economy and hinted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be moving toward third round of securities purchases to revive economy. But USD/JPY downside limited by Japan importer demand, heightened expectations of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Finance Minister Azumi promises "resolute action" against excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. USD/JPY also cushioned by reduced safe-haven appeal of yen and yen-funded carry trades as global risk sentiment improves on hopes that Europe's leaders will unveil comprehensive plan to resolve region's debt crisis on Wednesday. Yen crosses vulnerable to 0230 GMT October HSBC China flash manufacturing PMI data. Data focus: 2350 GMT Japan September provisional trade statistics, 1230 GMT U.S. September Chicago Fed national activity index, 1245 GMT Fed's Dudley speaks, 1300 GMT Fed's Fisher speaks. 1900 GMT Fed's Yellen speech. USD/JPY daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics in bearish mode; bearish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 76.52 Friday. Support at 75.78 (Friday's low); breach would expose downside to psychological 75.00 and 74.00 levels, then 73.13 (projected base of descending channel formed with the April 2009 high of 101.45 and Nov. 2009 low of 84.81 on weekly chart). Resistance at 76.60 (hourly chart), then at 76.92 (Friday's high) and 77.10 (Thursday's high); breach would target 77.46-77.49 (Oct. 17 high-Oct. 12 high), then 77.59 (Sept. 12 high), 77.86 (Sept. 9 high) and 78.02 (100-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 Sterling\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to trade in higher range. Supported by lower risk aversion, weaker USD sentiment; lower-than-expected UK public sector borrowing of GBP14.1 billion in September (vs GBP14.5 billion forecast); GBP demand on soft EUR/GBP cross. But GBP/USD gains tempered by loose BOE monetary policy. Data focus: 0830 GMT BOE asset purchase facility quarterly report. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish, although latter at overbought, suggesting sideways or higher GBP/USD trading near-term. Resistance at 1.5973 (Friday's high); breach would target 1.6046 (100-day moving average), then 1.6103 (61.8% Fibonacci correction of 1.6618-1.5270 Aug. 19-Oct. 6 decline) and 1.6136 (200-day moving average). Support at 1.5861 (hourly chart), then at 1.5752 (Friday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, exposing downside to 1.5679 (Thursday's low), then 1.5629 (Tuesday's low), 1.5541 (Oct. 12 low) and 1.5525 (Oct. 10 low).\par \par \uc2 Swiss Franc\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to trade in lower range. Undermined by broadly weaker USD undertone as risk aversion decreases; weaker USD sentiment. But USD/CHF downside limited by expectations of \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold, suggesting sideways or lower USD/CHF trading near-term. Support at 0.8801 (Friday's low); breach would target 0.8790 (Sept. 20 low), then 0.8751 (200-day moving average) and 0.8646 (Sept. 15 low). Resistance at 0.8894 (hourly chart), then at 0.8952 (Friday's high); breach would temper near-term negative outlook, exposing upside to 0.9081 (Thursday's high), then 0.9115 (Oct. 12 high), 0.9125 (Oct. 11 high) and 0.9290 (Oct. 10 high).\par \par \uc2 Canadian Dollar\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to trade in lower range. Undermined by lower risk aversion, weaker USD sentiment, firmer commodity & oil prices; higher-than-expected 0.5% increase in Canada September core CPI. USD/CAD daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, stochastics revert to bearish mode at oversold. Support at 1.0063 (Friday's low), then at 1.0040 (Oct. 17 low, near 55-day moving average); breach would expose downside to 0.9868 (100-day moving average), then 0.9806 (200-day moving average). Resistance at 1.0119 (hourly chart), then at 1.0187 (Friday's high); breach would temper near-term negative outlook, exposing upside to 1.0246 (Thursday's high), then 1.0264-1.0272 band (Tuesday's high-Oct. 13 high), 1.0328 (Oct. 12 high) and 1.0338 (Oct. 11 high).\par \par \uc2 Australian Dollar\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to trade in higher range. Supported by lower risk aversion, weaker USD sentiment, firmer commodity prices, AUD-USD interest-rate differential, Aussie demand on buoyant AUD/NZD cross; sovereign demand for Aussie. But AUD/USD vulnerable to 0230 GMT October HSBC China flash manufacturing PMI data. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD bullish, stochastics revert to bullish mode at overbought. Resistance at 1.0378 (Friday's high), then at 1.0388 (200-day moving average); breach would target 1.0398 (Sept. 16 high), then 1.0440 (100-day moving average). Support at 1.0267 (hourly chart), then at 1.0199 (Friday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, exposing downside to 1.0143 (Thursday's low), then 1.0114 (Tuesday's low), 1.0098 (Oct. 13 low) and 0.9863 (Oct. 12 low).\par \par \uc2 New Zealand Dollar\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to trade in higher range; NZ markets closed for holiday. Supported by lower risk aversion, weaker USD sentiment, firmer commodity prices, NZD-USD yield gap. But NZD/USD gains tempered by Kiwi sales on buoyant AUD/NZD cross. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD bullish, stochastics revert to bullish mode at overbought. Resistance at 0.8031 (Friday's high), then at 0.8068 (Oct. 17 high); breach would target 0.8099 (55-day moving average), then 0.8149 (61.8% Fibonacci correction of 0.8571-0.7466 Aug. 31-Oct. 4 decline) and 0.8212 (100-day moving average). Support at 0.7947 (hourly chart), then at 0.7897 (Friday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, exposing downside to 0.7868 (Thursday's low), then 0.7856 (Tuesday's low), 0.7739 (Oct. 12 low) and 0.7655 (Oct. 10 low).\par \par \uc2 China Yuan\par \par \uc2 China's yuan rose slightly against the U.S. dollar late Friday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  guided the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher via a daily reference rate on the back of a weaker dollar overseas. In the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.3840 around 0830 GMT, down from CNY6.3855 late Thursday. It traded between CNY6.3720 and CNY6.3935. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  fixed the dollar/yuan central parity rate at 6.3628 Friday, down from Thursday's 6.3652. Offshore, one-year dollar-yuan non-deliverable forward contracts rose to 6.4140/6.4220 from 6.4000/6.4040 late Thursday, implying a 0.5% depreciation of the yuan against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the next year. In the offshore yuan market in Hong Kong, where the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  floats freely, the dollar-yuan exchange rate was slightly lower at 6.4285 late Friday, down from 6.4355 late Thursday.\par \par \uc2 -0-\par \par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2011 \uc2 Dow Jones & Company, Inc\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020111024e7ao0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
