\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan, S Korea Agree To Expand Bilateral Swap Agreement To $70 Billion\b0\par\par\uc2 559 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2011\par 07:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Japan, South Korea expand bilateral swap to total of $70 billion from $13 billion\par \par \uc2 -- BOJ, BOK boost yen-won swap agreement tenfold\par \par \uc2 -- BOK, Japan's MOF agree on new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap agreement\par \par \uc2 (Adds background of deal, statements from two countries, further background in 2nd-3rd, 5th-7th paragraphs)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Tatsuo Ito and Se Young Lee \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan and South Korea said Wednesday they agreed to raise the size of their bilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap agreement to a total of $70 billion from the current $13 billion to help reassure \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and financial markets amid growing uncertainty stemming from the European debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The issue was on the agenda of Wednesday's summit between South Korean President Lee Myung-bak and visiting Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The two countries sought to increase the swap arrangement to a "sufficient" level so that it would act as a mutually beneficial, pre-emptive stability measure, South Korea's Ministry of Strategy and Finance said in a statement. The ministry said the arrangement will help strengthen local banks and corporations' competitive environment, as the deal will ease worries about foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  liquidity and lower funding costs. It also said the agreement will help Korea in maintaining a stable sovereign credit rating. Korea currently has an A1 sovereign debt rating from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  and an A rating from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 , both with a stable outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of the total, the central banks of both countries agreed to expand a yen-won swap contract to $30 billion from the current $3 billion. The contract will last until the end of October 2012.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This action will mitigate adverse influences of heightened uncertainty in the global market on the two sound and well-managed economies and contribute to enhancing stability in regional financial markets," the Bank of Japan said in a statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To supplement the measure, Japan's finance ministry and the Bank of Korea sealed a new bilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap agreement worth $30 billion. Under the agreement, using its external reserves worth $1.2 trillion, Japan's finance ministry can provide South Korea with dollars in exchange for the won if needed. Japan can also obtain dollars from South Korea in exchange for the yen. The pact will be in place for one year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The announcement was seen by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market as helping South Korea deal with the weakening won, which has been falling against the yen. Following the news, the yen fell to as low as KRW14.716 on EBS from around KRW14.900 earlier in the day. On Oct. 4, the yen had been as high as KRW15.759.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Japan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official said that in theory, South Korea could use raised dollars through the swap agreement with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . South Korea's external reserves are around $300 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition, both countries maintained the existing Chiang Mai Initiative deal--a multilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap agreement aimed at dealing with short-term liquidity problems--at $10 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The crisis-prevention measures, created after the Asian financial crisis in 1997-1998, have never been used.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tatsuo Ito, Dow Jones Newswires; +813-6269-2780; tatsuo.ito@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Megumi Fujikawa contributed to this article [ 19-10-11 0543GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111019e7aj0009y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
