\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-BOJ Shirakawa says global uncertainty behind yen rise\b0\par\par\uc2 378 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2011\par 18:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, details)\par \par \uc2 * Global slowdown to continue for some time-Shirakawa\par \par \uc2 * Reconstruction spending to support Japan economy\par \par \uc2 * BOJ may mull easing Oct. 27 if markets destabilise\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 PARIS, Oct 14 (Reuters) - Japan will tell its G20 counterparts that global economic uncertainty, and in Europe in particular, is pushing the yen higher and must be tackled successfully for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease, Bank of Japan Governor Masaaki Shirakawa said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa also issued the strongest warning to date of heightening risks to Japan's fragile economy, saying that while he would not judge now whether the world is headed for a recession, he expects global growth to remain weak for some time.\par \par \uc2 "Global growth is already slowing and this trend will be sustained for some time. That of course will affect Japan's economy," he told reporters ahead of a Group of 20 finance leaders' meeting from Friday.\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa added that the pain from the global slowdown will be offset somewhat by fiscal stimulus and pent-up demand in Japan for reconstruction from the March earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 Still, his bearish take on global growth adds to growing pessimism in the BOJ that Japan's economy may lose support from global demand just as it recovers from the March quake's damage, and may heighten market expectations of further monetary easing.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ may consider easing monetary policy further at its next rate review on Oct. 27 if renewed worries about Europe's debt crisis jolt financial markets and threaten Japan's recovery prospects, say analysts and sources familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and eased monetary policy in August to stem sharp yen rises that hurt its fragile, export-reliant economy.\par \par \uc2 While the yen is now off its peak against the dollar, jitters over Europe's debt woes and fears of a global recession have kept it stubbornly high against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The heightened uncertainty on the global economy, mainly in Europe, is behind yen rises," Shirakawa said. "As such, efforts to reduce the uncertainty on the global economy would be the best way to tackle yen rises."\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Mike Peacock)\par \par \uc2 G20/JAPAN-BOJ   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111017e7ae001gp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
