\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  FOCUS: A Swiss Cap Won't Fit Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 639 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2011\par 12:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Speculation over Japanese cap on yen is misplaced\par \par \uc2 -- Japan not that reliant on exports to U.S.\par \par \uc2 -- BOJ would have difficulty implementing cap\par \par \uc2 By Nicholas Hastings\par \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN\par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Switzerland may be having some success in capping the strength of the franc but that doesn't mean that Japan will follow suit.\par \par \uc2 Speculation that Japan, which is also suffering from the impact of safe-haven flows, would head into Friday's G-20 meeting with a plan to cap the yen against the dollar led to a sharp fall in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 However, while \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may yet resort to market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the yen from climbing, there is little sign that the Japanese authorities would resort to the same drastic measures as the Swiss.\par \par \uc2 For a start, while the yen may be historically high against the dollar, its overall strength is not that exceptional compared with the all-time highs of the franc against the euro.\par \par \uc2 There is also the question of why Japan would want to drive the yen down against the dollar given that only 15% of its exports and 10% of its imports are accounted for by the U.S.\par \par \uc2 In Switzerland's case, about 70% of its exports and its imports are with the euro zone so the franc's strength against the euro is of paramount importance in terms of competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 There is also the issue of the cap itself.\par \par \uc2 The SNB runs both monetary and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy so is perfectly positioned to sell all the francs it wants to keep the cap in place and introduce all the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and options it needs to ensure that the procedure succeeds.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan, however, operates in a completely different world.\par \par \uc2 Although the bank may be responsible for monetary policy, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is decided by the politicians at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In other words, the unlimited printing of money that a cap would need is in the hands of the government and the Bank of Japan's independence as a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be compromised if it got involved.\par \par \uc2 Also, as Citibank points out in a recent summary of Japanese policy, it is difficult for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps and options to be employed by the Bank of Japan under current \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, further curbing the central banks ability to manage a yen cap effectively.\par \par \uc2 Of course all this doesn't mean that Japan still wouldn't like the yen to be weaker against the dollar. Recent data suggest the 'V-shaped' recovery that occurred after the March tsunami disaster is coming to an end and Japanese companies will be even more anxious to boost exports. But given that many have now boosted production bases in other Asian countries, reducing their exposure to the dollar's rate against the yen, pressures aren't quite so intense.\par \par \uc2 Also, Japan is hardly likely to receive any international support for manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a time when most other major economies remain fragile and when the U.S. is turning up the heat on China for pursuing its version of the same thing.\par \par \uc2 So as the dollar resumes its slide under Y80, the threat of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain an issue but talk of an actual cap like Switzerland has on the franc should be short-lived.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg TNI FRX POV\par \par \uc2 Reuters USD/DJ\par \par \uc2 Thomson P/1066 or P/1074\par \par \uc2 (Nicholas Hastings is a Senior Correspondent in London for Dow Jones Newswires who has written about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more than 20 years. He previously covered a variety of markets, including equities, fixed income, commodities and energy. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com) [ 13-10-11 1043GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1066   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111013e7ad000sp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
