\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Euro Fall Vs Yen Hurts Japan Exporters; More Downside Looms\b0\par\par\uc2 580 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2011\par 12:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- The euro, at a decade low versus the yen, has more downside\par \par \uc2 -- \uc2 Sony\uc2  says the weak euro is hurting the company more than the weak dollar\par \par \uc2 -- Speculation grows that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros for yen\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Mochizuki \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--With no end in sight for the euro-zone sovereign debt crisis, the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's drop to a decade low against the yen could be a sign of more pain to come for the Japanese economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's exporters have struggled for many months with the yen near a record high against the U.S. dollar; the recent euro weakness is just compounding their woes. Many companies count on sales in Europe for a chunk of their earnings, so its steep fall has opened fresh wounds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sony Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (6758.TO) Deputy President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kazuo Hirai\f28 \uc2 \f2  said the euro's swoon has had a bigger impact on the company's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  than the weak dollar. "As a Japanese company, we would like to expect aggressive steps by the Japanese government against the yen's strength," he told reporters Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro has tumbled more than 10% against the yen since late August, as worries grow of a disorderly Greek default, regional banking crisis and contagion spreading to other weak euro-zone sovereigns.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slid to Y100.77 in early Asia trading Tuesday, its lowest since June 2001, after another indecisive meeting of European ministers on the Greek crisis. The drop was compounded by automated stop-loss selling orders by Japanese retail investors, dealers said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 They say the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has further room to fall as it is hard to imagine a quick resolution to the debt crisis. "I see the euro at Y90 by this year's end, meaning the 10-year low it marked today is just the beginning," said Hideki Amikura, a dealer at Nomura Trust and Banking.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is not clear how much yen-strength against the euro Tokyo will tolerate. Finance Minister Jun Azumi Tuesday called the yen's rise against the euro "extreme" and said the government would deal with it "with full force," a sign that Tokyo may be closer to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In their three \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since September 2010, the Japanese authorities sold yen for dollars. Speculation is growing that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may buy euros as well if it attempts to counter the yen's strength. The last time it did so was in May 2003.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist in Tokyo, Masafumi Yamamoto, said Tokyo may consider selling yen for both euros and dollars if the euro falls below Y100 and the dollar drops below Y75, in tandem with a decline in the Nikkei 225 Stock Average below 8,000.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 0941 GMT, the euro was at Y101.13 and the dollar at Y76.62. The Nikkei 225 closed down 1.1% at 8456.12.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese economy, pulling out of a slump brought by the March 11 earthquake and tsunami, could lose its momentum if the yen remains strong.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If the current yen rates were to remain, we would see firms struggling harder than now," said Hideo Kumano, chief economist at Dai-ichi Life Research Institute.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Mochizuki, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2782; takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Juro Osawa contributed to this report. [ 04-10-11 1027GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4258 5095   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111004e7a4000s8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
