\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  Cuts Targets For Australia's S&P/ASX 200\b0\par\par\uc2 1,819 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Oktober 2011\par 01:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2332 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  cuts its S&P/ASX 200 target to 4075 for December 2011, 4400 for June 2012, and 5000 for December 2012. The new targets are 8% below previous forecasts by GS, and follow downgrades to its global and Australian economic growth forecasts. The broker has also adjusted its model portfolio to reflect the deteriorating global growth and increasing uncertainty in the macro outlook expected to emerge in the next 1-2 quarters. PanAust (PNA.AU) has been cut from the portfolio and BHP (BHP.AU) reduced, while Sonic (SHL.AU) has been added and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Transurban\f28 \uc2 \f2  (TCL.AU) and Suncorp (SUN.AU) increased. The index is up 1.8% at 3943.2. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2331 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude is likely to consolidate with risks skewed to the upside after rebounding more than $3.50 from a one-year low of $74.95 Tuesday, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. The daily continuation chart is mixed as the MACD and stochastic indicators are bearish, but a bullish hammer candlestick pattern was completed Tuesday. Resistance is at $79.64 (Monday's high); a breach would expose upside to $83.23 (Friday's high), then to $83.98 (Thursday's high), $84.62-$84.77 band (Sept. 28 high-Sept. 27 high) and $85.00 (Sept. 22 high). But a drop below the $74.95 support would reinstate the near-term negative bias, exposing downside to $73.58 (Sept. 23, 2010 low), then to $72.75 (Sept. 17, 2010 reaction low). November crude is up $2.29 at $77.96/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2319 GMT [Dow Jones] LME base metals are likely to open lower, with euro-zone debt worries set to weigh further on investor sentiment after \uc2 Moody's\uc2  three-notch downgrade of Italian government bond ratings overnight. Deepening concerns over a possible default by Greece triggering a cash crunch in Europe, coupled with worries over slowing global economic growth, have pushed base metal prices sharply lower in the past few weeks. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  and Standard Bank Tuesday lowered their copper price forecasts for 2012. Goldman expects a mild recession in Europe and cut its LME copper price forecast by 14% to $9,200/ton for 2012. Standard cut its price forecast by 6% to $8,950/ton for the same year. Flagship LME three-month copper ended down 2.7% at $6,800/ton Tuesday. All the metals in the complex finished lower except tin, which ended up 2.4% at $20,990/ton. (arpan.mukherjee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2315 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs are likely to open lower as U.S. Treasurys surrendered earlier gains on a late session rally in U.S. stocks Tuesday. "JGBs' two-day rally is likely to suffer a setback," says \uc2 Tokai Tokyo Securities\uc2  chief strategist Kazuhiko Sano. But amid lingering uncertainties surrounding the global economy, banks have large piles of cash available and are ready to put them into safe-haven JGBs. "After an initial sell-off, the market will likely see bargain-hunters swoop in," adds Sano. Lead December futures are tipped in a 142.34-142.59 band vs 142.54 at last close, while the benchmark 10-year cash JGB yield is tipped in a 0.985%-0.995% range from 0.985% at Tuesday's close.(mitsuru.obe@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2312 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZX-50 is +0.1% at 3323.08 after a sharp rally on Wall Street toward the end of the session underscored market volatility and "made a lot of investors stand aside here in New Zealand," says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Bryon Burke. That volatility, coupled with the still unresolved European debt crisis, means investors are "pretty much sitting on their hands" with very little change in most of the leading stocks, he says. Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) is flat at NZ$7.57, Telecom (TEL.NZ) is down 0.4% at NZ$2.585, and Sky City (SKC.NZ) is up 0.9% at NZ$3.29. In all cases Burke says there is no news driving the stock and volumes are light. Given the volatility, Burke expects the index to remain flat over the course of the session, even if Australia performs strongly. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2308 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY falls in early Asia, though Joseph Capurso, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 , forecasts the drop may be short-lived. "Overall, you've still got a very stable (USD/JPY). On the one hand, it has pressure to fall because of safe havens but the threat of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  means it isn't going to 76," says Capurso. The USD/JPY is last at 76.798, down from a late U.S. level at 76.98. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2305 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR may resume falling in Asia trade as \uc2 Moody's\uc2  cutting Italy's government bond rating by 3 notches weighs risk appetite, says a senior dealer at a major Japanese bank in Tokyo. The euro rose Tuesday in New York on an FT report that EU financial ministers were considering coordinated fund injections for financial institutions, and also on Fed Chief Bernanke's comments which seemed to suggest additional easing. For the USD/JPY, investors will pay attention to a Nikkei report that Japan is considering additional EFSF bond buying; "the question is how Japan wants to fund it," meaning they are curious if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the EUR/JPY to raise funds, he says. The USD/JPY is at 76.76, and may trade in a 76.45-77.00 range; the EUR/USD is at 1.3317, and may trade in a 1.3250-1.3350 range; while the EUR/JPY is at 102.24, and may trade in a 101.50-102.50 range, he adds.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2305 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei Stock Average may rise on a firmer EUR/JPY, now 102.22, and a late rally on Wall Street following a Financial Times report that EU finance ministers are discussing ways to recapitalize the region's banks. Yumi Nishimura, a senior market analyst at Daiwa Securities, pegs the index resistance for Wednesday at 8600 vs Tuesday's close of 8456.12 (down 1.1%). "Although there seems to be a gradual progress in European discussions, the rebound will remain weak with investors still closely watching developments," she says, indicating a full-fledged rebound in shares is unlikely until later this month. She adds that recently beaten-down shares such as \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) may be lifted by short-term bargain-hunting. Nikkei December futures ended Chicago trading at 8530 vs the Osaka close at 8450. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Philippine inflation likely rose 4.5% on-year in September according to a Dow Jones Newswires poll, compared with August's 4.3% increase, due to supply constraints following typhoons that hit the country and a peso weakened by the impact of global economic uncertainty. Despite the projected uptick, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which expects September inflation to fall within the 3.8%-4.8% range, isn't likely to tighten monetary policy at its next review meeting on Oct. 20. "In the grander scheme of things, we do not expect the September CPI to influence the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  materially, with (policy interest) rates expected to remain frozen for rest of the year and into next," says Forecast Web economist Radhika Rao. The data are due around 0100 GMT. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2256 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 index may rise 1.5% to test the 5-day moving average at 3932 on short covering Wednesday before awaiting further developments out of Europe. The template for Wednesday's price action might be something like that of Sept. 16, when the market jumped 2.2% to 4160 on European plans for liquidity injections, before resuming its slide to fresh 2 month lows. IG Markets tips a 1% opening rise to 3910, after the S&P 500 rose 2.3% to 1123.95, generating a 4.5% intraday recovery, after The Financial Times reported that EU leaders are working on a plan to recapitalize European banks. However "the market should be cognisant that this supposed 'Bank Aid Plan' is light on details," IG Markets strategist Cameron Peacock says. "Over the last several months we've heard a lot of rhetoric about 'needing to do more' but a great reluctance for anyone to reach into their pockets. Until or unless decisive action follows, this may prove just another opportunity to close long positions or establish fresh short positions." Domestic retail sales data are due at 0030 GMT. The S&P/ASX 200 index closed at 3872.1 on Tuesday. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2255 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional equities could rebound, after recent sharp losses, as U.S. stocks staged a late session rally to close in the green on a Financial Times report that EU finance officials are mulling ways to coordinate the recapitalization of financial institutions. But any positive cheer may be dampened by news of \uc2 Moody's\uc2  lowering Italy's government bond ratings. China and Hong Kong markets are shut for holidays. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the euro is likely to tick higher on the FT report, though gains could be capped as a firm resolution to Greece's debt crisis has yet to emerge. The EUR/USD is at 1.3314 from 1.3355 late Tuesday in New York, the EUR/JPY is at 102.20 from 102.53, and the USD/JPY is at 76.75 from 76.82. On the data front, there's Australia's August retail trade at 0030 GMT, the Philippines September CPI at 0100 GMT, and Taiwan's September WPI and CPI at 0800 GMT. Elsewhere, there's the UK 2Q GDP, EU 2Q GDP - third estimate, EU August retail trade, U.S. September ADP jobs report, U.S. Treasury Secretary Geithner participates in a forum in Washington, and the BOE's October monetary policy committee meeting. (wei-zhe.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 04, 2011 19:32 ET (23:32 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111004e7a400821\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
