monetary base, 1300 GMT Fed's Raskin speech, 1400 GMT Fed Chairman Bernanke testifies before U.S. Joint Economic Committee on the economic outlook, 1400 GMT U.S. August factory orders. USD/JPY daily chart mixed as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode, but bearish-engulfing candlestick pattern completed yesterday. Support at 76.51-76.49 (yesterday's low-Friday's low); breach would target 76.41-76.33 band (Thursday's low-Wednesday's low), then 76.26-76.21 band (Sept. 27 low-Sept. 26 low), 76.15-76.10 band (Sept. 23 low-Sept. 22 low) and 75.94 (Aug. 19 record low). Resistance at 76.96 (hourly chart), then at 77.27-77.33 band (yesterday's high-Sept. 15 high); breach would target 77.59 (Sept. 12 high), then 77.86 (Sept. 9 high) and 78.48 (Aug. 8 high).\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by heightened fears of Greece default after the country's admission that it would miss its budget-deficit targets and euro-zone finance ministers delayed decision to give Greece its much-needed installment of EUR8 billion aid payment until Troika report Oct. 13; investors' lack of confidence in European leadership to resolve debt crisis; funding tensions in European interbank market as counterparty risks rose on potential writedowns to banks' holdings of Greek government debt and talk of Franco-Belgian bank \uc2 Dexia\uc2 's liquidity problems; worries over euro-zone recession as region's manufacturing PMI dropped to 25-month low of 48.5 in September from 49.0 in August (though better than 48.4 preliminary reading); ECB's rate-policy dilemma as high September inflation figures may restrain \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from lowering interest rates at Thursday's meeting to help stimulate economy. But EUR/USD losses tempered by sovereign demand for euro. Data focus: 0900 GMT EU August PPI. EUR/USD daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold; 5- & 15-day moving averages falling. Support at 1.3163 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to psychological 1.3000, then 1.2871 (Jan. 10 reaction low). Resistance at 1.3285 (hourly chart), then at 1.3360 (previous base set Sept. 26); breach would expose upside to 1.3601 (Friday's high), then 1.3679 (Thursday's high), 1.3690 (Wednesday's high) and 1.3799 (Sept. 21 high).\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to trade with bearish bias as markets await 0330 GMT RBA interest rate announcement: \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to stay pat on rates. AUD/USD undermined by inflamed investor risk aversion, weak commodity prices (CRB spot index closed down 1.77 yesterday at 296.38). But AUD/USD losses tempered by AUD-USD interest rate differential, sovereign demand for Aussie. Other data: 0030 GMT Australia August building approvals, August international trade balance, 0530 GMT September commodity price index. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics in bearish mode, though latter at oversold; 5- & 15-day moving averages falling. Support at 0.9505 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.9405 (previous cap set Nov. 16, 2009), then 0.9359 (Sept. 20, 2010 low). Resistance at 0.9605 (hourly chart), then at 0.9699 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.9808 (Friday's high), then 0.9878 (Thursday's high), 0.9955 (Wednesday's high) and 0.9985 (Sept. 27 high).\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by heightened investor risk aversion, weak commodity prices. But NZD/USD losses tempered by NZD-USD yield gap. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics in bearish mode, though latter at oversold; 5- & 15-day moving averages falling. Support at 0.7506 (yesterday's low), then at 0.7485 (March 28 low); breach would expose downside to 0.7288 (March 21 low), then 0.7113 (March 17 reaction low). Resistance at 0.7583 (hourly chart), then at 0.7641 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.7703 (Friday's high), then 0.7831 (Thursday's high), 0.7900 (Wednesday's high) and 0.7957 (Sept. 27 high, near previous base set Aug. 9 and 200-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to trade with risks skewed lower. Undermined by intensified investor risk aversion; increased expectations of further quantitative easing from Bank of England, though \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to stay pat at Thursday's meeting. But GBP sentiment soothed by surprise rise in UK manufacturing PMI to 51.1 in September from 49.4 in August (vs forecast for drop to 48.5). GBP/USD downside also limited by GBP demand on soft EUR/GBP cross. Data focus: 0830 GMT UK September CIPS construction PMI. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics turning bearish; 5- & 15-day moving averages falling. Support at 1.5419 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.5325 (Sept. 22 1-year low), then 1.5294 (Sept. 7, 2010 reaction low) and 1.5123 (July 21, 2010 low). Resistance at 1.5483 (hourly chart), then at 1.5531 (hourly chart) and 1.5587 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.5665 (Friday's high), then 1.5715 (Thursday's high), 1.5754 (Sept. 19 high) and 1.5780 (previous base of 1.5780 set on July 12).\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to trade with bullish bias. Underpinned by broadly stronger USD undertone as investor risk aversion increases; bigger-than-expected drop in Swiss manufacturing PMI to 48.2 in September from 51.7 in August (vs 50.2 forecast); expectations of \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's CHF-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain EUR/CHF floor at 1.2000. Daily chart positive-biased as stochastics in bullish mode, MACD turning bullish; 5- & 15-day moving averages rising. Resistance at 0.9218 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.9290 (April 6 high), then 0.9340 (April 1 high), 0.9370 (March 9 high) and 0.9506 (Feb. 22 high). Support at 0.9140 (hourly chart), then at 0.9078 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.8962 (Friday's low), the