\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 939 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2011\par 08:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ GOVERNOR: CAUTION OVER PROSPECTS FOR GLOBAL ECONOMY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said Friday caution over the prospects for the global economy is increasing as the U.S. economic slowdown has become more evident, while concerns about the euro-zone debt crisis persist.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO BOOST YEN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WAR CHEST \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister sent out a fresh warning against the yen's persistent strength on Friday, with a plan to expand the nation's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war chest to record levels and a vow to take "decisive measures" to limit the yen's rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: S&P, FITCH DOWNGRADE NEW ZEALAND, CITING EXTERNAL DEBT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ratings Services and Fitch Ratings both downgraded New Zealand's government debt Friday, citing its worsening external-debt position and the costs of earthquake recovery. (published 0444 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AZUMI: TO BOOST \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ARSENAL BY Y15 TLN TO Y165 TLN \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Finance Minister said Friday that the government plans to boost the size of the nation's war chest to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets by Y15 trillion to "flexibly" respond to the yen's upward trend. (published 0123 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SLOVAK PM: EURO ZONE COUNTRIES SHOULDN'T TAKE ON NEW DEBT TO COVER ANOTHER'S DEBT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Debts of one troubled euro-zone country shouldn't lead to the issuance of new debt in other countries in the region, Slovakia's Prime Minister said in an interview with CNBC Friday. (published 0538 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: CHINA SAFE REVISES DOWN 1H CURRENT ACCOUNT SURPLUS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulator on Friday revised down its estimate of China's current account surplus in the first half of the year to $87.8 billion, from a previous estimate of $98.4 billion. (published 0559 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: HSBC CHINA PMI STEADY IN SEPTEMBER VS. AUGUST AT 49.9 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The final HSBC China Manufacturing Purchasing Managers Index, a gauge of nationwide manufacturing activity, was unchanged at 49.9 in September compared with 49.9 in August, \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC Holdings PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Friday. (published 0317 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: YUAN HITS LOW END OF TRADING BAND FOR 3RD CONSECUTIVE DAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan hit the low end of its daily trading band against the U.S. dollar Friday for the third consecutive session, as the scramble for the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  intensified amid persistent demand from Chinese importers and investors wary of the growing global economic uncertainties. (published 0332 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN OUTPUT CONTINUES POST-QUAKE RECOVERY, BUT OUTLOOK MURKY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese industrial production continued to bounce back after the devastating impact of the March 11 earthquake and tsunami, rising for the fifth-straight month in August as the transportation, steel, and electronic component sectors regained their footing. (published 0109 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN'S AUTO PRODUCTION +1.8% ON YEAR IN AUG \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Production of cars, trucks and buses in Japan increased 1.8% on year in August, marking the first increase in 11 months, as car makers mostly restored their parts supply chains disrupted by the earthquake and tsunami in March. (published 0414 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: KOREA AUGUST INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT WEAKER THAN EXPECTED; MOMENTUM SLOWING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's industrial output rose at a slower-than-expected pace in August as production of semiconductors and electronics devices weakened, government data showed Friday, indicating slowing growth momentum for Asia's fourth-largest economy as global uncertainties shake markets. (published 0046 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CIC EXECUTIVE: CHINA CAN'T BUY EURO-ZONE ASSETS WITHOUT CLEAR PICTURE OF SOLUTIONS -XINHUA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China can't be expected to buy assets in the euro zone without seeing a clear picture of the solutions to the region's sovereign debt problems, the state-run Xinhua News Agency reported Friday, citing Jin Liqun, chairman of \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Investment Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s supervisory board. (published 0113 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: AUSTRALIA WARNS MARKET TURMOIL WILL HIT TAX REVENUES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's government said Friday that it expects weaker revenues due to the ongoing shakeout of global markets, but stuck by a pledge to return a balanced budget in the 2012-13 fiscal year. (published 0138 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PHILIPPINE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : INFLATION TO STAY WITHIN 2011 TARGET DESPITE TYPHOONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philippine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Friday that despite recent typhoons that have damaged large areas of farm land, inflation should remain within its target range. (published 0506 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par PREVIEW: AUSTRALIA'S RBA TO KEEP RATES STEADY TUES -ECONOMISTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to keep interest rates on hold at a policy meeting Tuesday, with market expectations of aggressive interest rate cuts over the next year likely to be challenged by the bank's accompanying statement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par HEARD ON THE STREET: EMERGING BONDS SWIM AGAINST THE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 As western governments mire themselves in debt, emerging-market sovereign bonds are coming of age. Asia's fast-growing economies and relatively strong fiscal positions have lured in ever more foreign investors. But, at least when it comes to local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bonds, investors should remember that they have two legs supporting their value. (published 0530 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: INDONESIA SEPT CPI LIKELY +4.77% ON YEAR, +0.39% ON MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's inflation rate will likely ease in September, but this will unlikely prompt the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to lower its policy rate when it next meets Oct. 11 as a cut would put further downward pressure on the rupiah. (published 0159 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: INDONESIA AUGUST TRADE SURPLUS LIKELY WIDENED TO $1.87 BLN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia likely posted a wider trade surplus in August than in July on resilient demand for its commodities globally, economists said. (published 0237 GMT) [ 30-09-11 0657GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   