\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Kospi Ends +2.7%, Tech Shares Soar;1680-1850 Range\b0\par\par\uc2 1,953 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2011\par 08:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0643 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi ends 2.7% higher at 1769.29 after tech shares staged a rebound on strong interest from local institutions as their valuation merits shine following heavy losses, say analysts. Growing expectations that Germany will approve the expansion of the euro-zone's rescue fund later in the global day also boosted sentiment, though some analysts are skeptical that the possible outcome will provide longer-term relief. "Despite lingering external risks, local institutions have started to look to the cheap valuations of tech stocks and amid a weaker local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says \uc2 Daishin Securities\uc2  analyst Choi Jai-sic; the Kospi will likely trade in a 1680-1850 band near-term. The robust gains also come ahead of quarterly window-dressing by managers who have to report their investment results to clients, adds Choi. \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  (066570.SE) rises 11.2% to KRW68,800, while \uc2 Hana Financial\uc2  (086790.SE) climbs 6.7% to KRW34,900 on higher expectations its bid to buy \uc2 Korea Exchange Bank\uc2  (004940.SE) will be approved by Korea's financial authorities. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT [Dow Jones] While a more extensive Greek debt restructuring, the significant expansion and aggrandizement of the role of the EFSF and additional non-standard policy support from the ECB could go some way towards stabilizing the eurozone crisis, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  says "the fact remains that if it is to survive and flourish, the eurozone needs to take on more of the characteristics of an optimal \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  zone." The house says "Labor and capital mobility and wage and price flexibility need to be greatly enhanced via deep-seated structural reforms. But most importantly of all, there must be a well developed system of fiscal policy oversight and transfers -- essentially the collectivization of Euro fiscal policy under a single arbiter -- backed by the issuance of joint and severally guaranteed 'Eurobonds'." The house says the challenging politics and legalities of this process need to be overcome and suggests that "an honest depiction of the potential horrors of a Euro break up could help to concentrate minds." (john.phillips@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0640 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds are unchanged despite a rise in the weekly food inflation rate as traders are cautious ahead of the federal government's meeting to finalize the October-March borrowing calendar later in the day, says a dealer with a privately-run bank. The benchmark 7.80% 2021 bond is at 96.43 unchanged from before data and down from 96.47 at Wednesday's close. "We will see sharp downside if overseas borrowing increases previous estimates," she adds. The dealer tips the yield on the 10-year paper to stay in a 8.33%-8.37% band until the announcement; it's trading at 8.35%. India's federal government is likely to borrow INR1.67 trillion in the October-March period. In the week to Sept. 17, the food articles index rose 0.8% to 197.3 from 195.7 the previous week. The on-year inflation for food articles accelerated to 9.13% from 8.84% the previous week. (sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0634 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is steady at 76.55 from 76.60 late Wednesday in New York. as fears remain among investors that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may conduct JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the pair gets closer to breaching below 76.00, says a senior dealer at a major bank in Tokyo. "Flows by speculators are very thin lately as they are cautious over possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities," he adds. What's driving USD/JPY moves recently is orders by Japanese exporters and importers for month-end settlements. For now, he tips the pair in a 76.30-77.00 range.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0628 GMT [Dow Jones] December bunds open in negative territory ahead of a sizeable Italian BTP auction--around EUR8 billion of new 2014s and existing 2021s and 2022s--and the vote in Germany's Bundestag on expanding the EFSF. It is a reasonably busy data day, with German unemployment figures at 0755 GMT and euro-zone confidence indicators at 0900 GMT as the main focus of attention. The December bund contract is 0.10 lower at 135.56 and the 2s/5s/10s are quoted at 0.563%/1.257%/1.980%, respectively. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0620 GMT [Dow Jones] Given the constraints to growth and the enduring risks of deflation, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  says "official interest rates are likely to remain historically low for the foreseeable future and central banks will probably have to continue to supplement these super low policy rates with unorthodox initiatives." The house expects further quantitative easing measures in the U.S., U.K. and Japan, and thinks "the ECB's resistance to this kind of approach will necessarily weaken as the Eurozone crisis rumbles on." In the meantime, it says formal \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  independence and narrow inflation targeting are likely to be diluted amid the search for macro policy traction, adding "price level or nominal GDP targeting may result." \uc2 Westpac\uc2  says, "the further blurring of fiscal and monetary policy is likely...ultimately, we would not be surprised were some governments and central banks to be forced into what might be described as 'radical Keynesianism,' where government spending programmes or tax cuts were directly underwritten by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  financing." (john.phillips@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0615 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional equities are mixed with tentative buying pushing some markets higher ahead of Germany's EFSF vote. The Nikkei rises 0.5%, the S&P/ASX loses 1.0%, the Kospi rises 2.6%, the Sensex falls 0.4% and the Shanghai Composite Index falls 0.6%. Markets in Hong Kong are shut due to a typhoon. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the euro rises against the dollar and yen as some investors are optimistic German lawmakers will approve an expansion of the euro-zone's rescue fund. The EUR/USD is at 1.3613 from 1.3545 late Wednesday in New York, the EUR/JPY is at 104.18 from 103.77, and the USD/JPY is at 76.49 from 76.60. The CNY hit the lowest level of its daily trading band against the USD for the second consecutive session this week, boosted by the USD's international strength and continued month-end USD-demand by Chinese importers. China's NDRC Chairman Zhang Ping sees little grounds to worry about a hard landing for China's economy, and expects the country's 2011 economic growth to be similar to 2010's. Japan posts a merchandise trade surplus of Y188.52 billion in the first 10 days of September, more than double the Y86.32 billion surplus in the year-earlier period. Australian job vacancies rise 3.2% seasonally-adjusted on-quarter during the three-month period ended in August. Korea's August current-account surplus is at $401.3 million vs July's $3.77 billion surplus. The BOK said the country will be able to meet its 2011 target surplus of $15.5 billion. Spot gold is at $1,619.80/oz, up $10.10 from New York Wednesday. November Nymex crude oil futures rise seven cents to $81.28/bbl on Globex. (wei-zhe.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0556 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares rise 0.4% to 934.82 at midday in choppy trade. Some investors are accumulating recently-battered stocks but lingering worries over the eurozone debt crisis will likely cap near-term upside. "I think the market will stay in a tight 930-940 range for the rest of the day. All eyes are now on any progress in resolving the European debt problems," says Bualuang Securities strategist Pongrat Ratanatawanananda. Among the most actively traded stocks, PTT (PTT.TH) is down 0.4% at THB270, Asia Precision (APCS.TH) is up 6.4% on its debut at THB5.80 vs its IPO price of THB5.45, Banpu (BANPU.TH) is down 1.4% at THB558, \uc2 Advanced Info Service (ADVANC.TH) \uc2 is up 2.5% at THB125, and Bangkok Bank (BBL.TH) is up 0.7% at THB146. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0551 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 December futures are +0.3% at 8670, recovering from their intraday low of 8500 as the euro strengthens against the yen (EUR/JPY now at 104.15). But the market remains cautious ahead of a crucial vote by German lawmakers later in the global day on whether to put more money into Europe's bailout fund, says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager of investment strategy and research at Daiwa Securities. Even though many investors expect a favorable vote, the outcome is far from certain, he says, as some lawmakers even in Angela Merkel's own party are expected to vote against the measure. Takahashi pegs futures' range at 8500-8800 for the rest of the week. Germany's vote will be a crucial event to set the market tone ahead of next week's heavy dosage of economic indicators, including the Tankan in Japan and ISM data in the U.S, he adds. (judy.lam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0547 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end 0.5% higher at 7182.61 in choppy trade, but off its intraday low of 7061.01 as investors turn more hopeful that Germany will soon approve of an expansion of the eurozone rescue fund, says Grand Cathay Securities analyst Mars Hsu. He tips the Taiex to trade in a 7100-7300 range Friday. Apple (AAPL)-related shares rise ahead of an expected launch of a new-generation iPhone: Catcher (2474.TW) rises 3.7% to NT$181.50, Largan (3008.TW) rises 2.4% to NT$690.00, Hon Hai (2317.TW) is up 1.5% at NT$70.00. Upstream techs are weak: TSMC (2330.TW) ends flat at NT$70.30, MStar (3697.TW) fell 1.3% to NT$152.00. Financials reverse their earlier losses, with Fubon Financial (2881.TW) ending 0.8% higher at NT$32.00. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 29 Sep 2011 02:43 EDT DJ  MARKET TALK: Kospi Ends +2.7%, Tech Shares -2-\par \par \uc2 0541 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR/USD is at 1.3619, up from 1.3545 late Wednesday in New York as some investors are optimistic German lawmakers will approve an expansion of the euro-zone's rescue fund later in the day, says a senior dealer at a major bank in Tokyo. He adds, these investors are also encouraged by a Wall Street Journal report saying German Chancellor Merkel is racing to convince the public that their continued prosperity rests on preserving the EUR, and a large majority of Germans continue to feel a strong, historical commitment to Europe, with a common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as its anchor. He tipped the pair's resistance at 1.3650 for now.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0529 GMT [Dow Jones] A bounce Thursday in the AUD/USD on better-than-expected job vacancies figures for the last quarter won't last, says Christopher Gore, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with Go Markets in Melbourne. "We still think the Aussie is on a downward trajectory with so much more to go through in Europe. The Aussie in on its way to at least 0.9500," says Gore. The AUD/USD is trading at 0.9790, up from 0.9700 before the release of job vacancies data. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 29, 2011 02:43 ET (06:43 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110929e79t001kn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
