\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  FOCUS: Japanese  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Loses Its Attraction\b0\par\par\uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2011\par 12:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --New administration appears less interested in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 --Points to benefits of strong JPY for M&A\par \par \uc2 --Safe haven flows into JPY will likely intensify now\par \par \uc2 By Nicholas Hastings\par \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN\par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is losing its attraction--at least for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For months, the market has been on tenterhooks.\par \par \uc2 Will they, won't they march in and stop the yen from rising against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The uncertainty over what Japan will do became even more acute after Prime Minister Naoto Kan stepped down and was replaced by his finance minister, Yoshihiko Noda, at the end of last month.\par \par \uc2 Speculation over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was so strong that the dollar's rate against the yen spent most of this month in a narrow range between 76 and 78 with many players expecting the new administration to provide Japanese exporters with relief at the end of their fiscal half-year.\par \par \uc2 The assumption was that the Bank of Japan would be asked to wade into the market and push the yen lower against the dollar as the month of September draws to a close.\par \par \uc2 But, this has not happened.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday was the last day for regular spot settlement before the half-year ends and there has been no sign of Bank of Japan activity.\par \par \uc2 On the contrary, the new prime minister appeared to be more interested in extolling the virtues of a strong yen, including the advantages in brings for cross border mergers and acquisitions.\par \par \uc2 In other words, if Japanese companies are having difficulties at home, they should go shopping abroad.\par \par \uc2 At least part of this apparent switch in Japanese policy could arise from the strength of the new prime minister's approval rating.\par \par \uc2 According to Todd Elmer at Citibank, with a rating up at 60%, compared with the 20% Kan had, Noda is more immune to political pressure from the exporting community and less likely to resort to extreme measures such as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Of course, there is probably an element of accepting reality in all this too.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese authorities are well aware of the high risks associated with unilateral market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . With the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  already adopting an ultra-easy monetary policy stance there is little else the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can do to make the yen less attractive.\par \par \uc2 Also, there is little sign that the safe haven flows that have been supporting the yen are about to subside.\par \par \uc2 If anything, these flows are likely to get larger.\par \par \uc2 The euro-zone debt problem continues to lurch from one crisis to the next and even hopes over the U.S. Federal Reserve's latest attempts to revive the U.S. economy, \b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Twist, are starting to fade.\par \par \uc2 All this suggests that apart from brief episodes of denial, as we saw earlier this week, market sentiment in general will continue to deteriorate as investors turn away from risk.\par \par \uc2 The level of this shift in sentiment was reflected in results from \uc2 Man Group PLC\uc2 , the world's largest listed hedge fund group, which reported a $6 billion decline to its asset base since June, partly because of investor redemptions.\par \par \uc2 With the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  carefully managing the strength of that other major safe haven, the Swiss franc, and hinting that it will even raise the floor at which the euro can trade against the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the franc will hardly pose as the alternative to the yen it once was.\par \par \uc2 This means that the yen will become even more popular, especially if the new fiscal half-year starts with still no sign of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  and investors start pushing the dollar down under Y76.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg TNI FRX POV\par \par \uc2 Reuters USD/DJ\par \par \uc2 Thomson P/1066 or P/1074\par \par \uc2 (Nicholas Hastings is a Senior Correspondent in London for Dow Jones Newswires who has written about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more than 20 years. He previously covered a variety of markets, including equities, fixed income, commodities and energy. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com) [ 28-09-11 1034GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1066   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110928e79s000uq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
