\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Euro Sinks As No End To Debt Crisis In Sight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kosaku Narioka and Tatsuo Ito   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 970 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2011\par 08:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The euro led Asian regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  broadly lower on Monday as the markets saw little to cheer from weekend discussions at the G-20 on the euro-zone crisis and as stock market sell-offs around the region undermined confidence.\par \par \uc2 China, meanwhile, set the yuan's reference rate against the U.S. dollar at a record high Monday, a surprise move that market participants said was aimed at sending a message of stability to jittery investors.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei Stock Average was down 2.2% in late trading while the Seoul's KOSPI was off 2.3%. Thai shares fell more than 8.0% to a 13-month low.\par \par \uc2 "Nothing concrete came out of the G-20 meeting over the weekend," said Tomohiro Nishida, senior dealer at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking. "Uncertainty will likely persist," he added.\par \par \uc2 While various officials meeting in Washington over the weekend offered new steps, such as leveraging the European Financial Stability Facility through borrowing, there seemed no clear consensus, with German officials remaining cautious.\par \par \uc2 German Deputy Finance Minister Joerg Asmussen was quoted as saying in Washington on Sunday that extra funding for Greece was unlikely to be granted at an Oct. 3 meeting, countering previous expectations that the money could come quickly after recent talks between Athens and the "troika" of the IMF, ECB and EU commission.\par \par \uc2 "It's up to the Germans," said Dai Sato, senior vice president at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . "Sentiment could turn for the better if Germany makes a resolve to save the euro in a spirit of self-sacrifice," he added. The situation could otherwise continue to get worse with no imminent reversal in sight, Sato said, adding the euro may fall as low as $1.3200 this week.\par \par \uc2 At 0500 GMT, the euro was at $1.3407 down from $1.3499 in New York late Friday. It briefly fell as low as $1.3390.\par \par \uc2 The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also fell to Y102.18, a ten-year low, before recovering some ground to Y102.46, from Y103.70 in New York Friday.\par \par \uc2 Traders also took remarks by a senior official at U.S. rating agency \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  as a green light to dump the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The official said efforts to increase the size of the European Financial Stability Facility could have "potential credit implications in different ways," according to Reuters.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the yuan at a record high Monday, despite the weaker euro and the recent rout in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  fixed a central parity for the dollar at a record low CNY6.3735, down from Friday's over-the-counter close of CNY6.3889.\par \par \uc2 Calling the fixing "totally unexpected," a trader at a Beijing-based bank said the action indicates that the PBOC "is sending a strong signal to the market that it won't let the yuan depreciate despite unfolding sovereign-debt woes overseas."\par \par \uc2 Other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were lower, in line with falling stock markets and the euro, but the region's central banks weren't spotted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as they did aggressively last week.\par \par \uc2 The Korean won was sharply lower against the dollar although it came off its worst levels as traders remain cautious about dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Bank of Korea was spotted multiple times last week taking action to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar was at KRW1,185.30, up from KRW1,166.00 late Friday.\par \par \uc2 Portfolio outflows pushed the Thai baht lower. The dollar was at THB31.08 from THB30.85 in late Asian trade Friday.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar fell throughout the day and was at $0.9694, although a Macquarie strategist said that the unit could draw buying interest from exporters and real money names if markets start to stabilise. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still ahead of its low of $0.9669 hit on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also rose sharply against its Canadian counterpart, climbing to C$1.0377, its highest in a year.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the dollar/yen remained locked in its recent range. Dollar selling by Japanese exporters ahead of the end of fiscal half-year is likely to continue weighing this week, while hopes for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance ministry is likely to prevent any sharp falls in the pair, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was at Y76.40 from Y76.64 in New York. The dollar was at CHF0.9107 from CHF0.9088. The British pound was at $1.5458 from $1.5469, while the ICE Dollar Index was at 78.665 from 78.302.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 00:50 EDT / 0450 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY Japan            76.40-42       76.44-50  -0.08    76.76    76.40  -5.81 \par EUR/USD Euro            1.3407-10      1.3516-26  -0.83   1.3550   1.3392  +0.13 \par GBP/USD U.K.            1.5451-56      1.5474-90  -0.18   1.5494   1.5438  -0.99 \par USD/CHF Switzerland     0.9105-10      0.9019-56  +0.78   0.9117   0.9036  -2.59 \par USD/CAD Canada          1.0349-54      1.0292-96  +0.56   1.0356   1.0270  +4.06 \par AUD/USD Australia       0.9720-24      0.9765-70  -0.47   0.9836   0.9716  -5.00 \par NZD/USD New Zealand    0.7699-710      0.7744-50  -0.55   0.7773   0.7696  -1.18 \par  \par Euro Rate \par EUR/JPY Japan           102.44-48      103.21-70  -0.96   103.73   102.34  -5.68 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Kosaku Narioka and Tatsuo Ito, Dow Jones Newswires; 81-3-6269-2784; kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com, tatsuo.ito@dowjones.com [ 09-26-11 0221ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1033 1034 1863 28038 4162   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020110926e79q0000y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
