\par\par \b \uc2 Japan can't act like Swiss on  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  rise-MOF\b0\par\par\uc2 By Leika Kihara   \par \uc2 357 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2011\par 02:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Sept 23 (Reuters) - Japan cannot curb rises in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  like Switzerland has done by setting a cap on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pledging to defend it with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as that would effectively mean abandoning monetary policy independence, a senior Japanese finance ministry official said on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Deputy vice finance minister Masatsugu Asakawa also said the country's priority should be to beat deflation which is keeping upward pressure on the yen.\par \par \uc2 "Logically speaking, as long as deflation goes on, there will be continuous pressure for the yen to rise," Asakawa said in a seminar hosted by the International Monetary Fund.\par \par \uc2 He declined to comment on the appropriate level of the yen and shrugged off the view Japan should be as bold in countering \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rises as Switzerland, which like Japan saw its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate on safe haven demand from risk-shy investors.\par \par \uc2 "Switzerland decided to abandon its independence on monetary policy. In Japan, that is simply not an option to take," he said.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this month decided to set an exchange rate cap on the soaring franc and promised it would defend it with unlimited \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month to stem sharp rises in the yen, but just for one day spending roughly 4.5 trillion yen.\par \par \uc2 That has not prevented the yen from hitting a record high against the dollar and multi-year highs against the euro, moves policy-makers fret could hurt the export-reliant economy.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo has repeatedly threatened to step into the market again to counter yen rises. But as a G7 member committed to market-determined exchange rates and with an economy much bigger than Switzerland's, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  feels it cannot \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  unlimitedly even if rises in the yen were a concern. [ID:nL3E7KD46X]\par \par \uc2 Asakawa also warned against complacency on Japan's huge public debt, saying it would be dangerous to assume that bond yields will stay low as long as Japanese government bonds (JGBs) are held mostly by domestic investors.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Chizu Nomiyama)\par \par \uc2 IMF/JAPAN-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110927e79o0000v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
