\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Japan PM Noda: Japan Open To Buying More EFSF Bonds To Help Stablize European Mkts, Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 527 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2011\par 11:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 20 Sep 2011 05:39 EDT *DJ  Japan PM Noda: Believe G7 Understands Japan's Concerns Over Strong Yen\par \par \uc2 20 Sep 2011 05:39 EDT *DJ  Japan PM Noda: No Change In Policy To Take Decisive Steps Vs Disorderly Or Excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Moves\par \par \uc2 20 Sep 2011 05:39 EDT *DJ  Japan PM Noda: Hope BOJ Will Take Timely And Appropriate Steps To Support Economy\par \par \uc2 20 Sep 2011 05:39 EDT *DJ  Japan PM Noda: Govt And Boj Share View Over Strong Yen, Deflation\par \par \uc2 20 Sep 2011 05:39 EDT *DJ  Japan PM Noda: Hope To Restart Idled Reactors From Next Spring\par \par \uc2 20 Sep 2011 05:39 EDT *DJ  Japan PM Noda: "Impossible" To Get By Next Summer Without Idled Reactors\par \par \uc2 20 Sep 2011 05:39 EDT *DJ  Japan PM Noda: Temporary Tax Hike To Finance Reconstruction Should Be Over 10 Yrs\par \par \uc2 20 Sep 2011 05:39 EDT *DJ  Japan PM Noda Was Speaking In Exclusive Interview With Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 20 Sep 2011 05:39 EDT DJ  INTERVIEW: Japan PM Noda: Japan Open To Buying More EFSF Bonds\par \par \uc2 By George Nishiyama\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda said on Tuesday that Tokyo is open to buying more bonds issued by a European bailout fund to alleviate concerns over the sovereign debt crisis in Europe.\par \par \uc2 Noda said in an exclusive interview with Dow Jones Newswires and the Wall Street Journal that the European Union must first reach a consensus on how to deal with the crisis, but said Japan was ready to do its part to help contain the euro-zone debt turmoil.\par \par \uc2 "To stabilize the European financial markets and the economy, Japan needs to make a certain contribution going forward, including purchases of EFSF bonds," Noda said, referring to debt issued by the European Financial Stability Facility.\par \par \uc2 Using its $1.2 trillion foreign reserves, Japan has already bought about EUR2.7 billion--or a little more than 20%--of the EFSF bonds since January to help Ireland and Portugal.\par \par \uc2 Noda said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the yen's moves became excessive, adding that the Group of Seven nations understand Tokyo's stance.\par \par \uc2 "There is no change in our policy to take decisive steps against disorderly or excessive moves," Noda said.\par \par \uc2 Noda ordered yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August when he was the finance minister to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, but doubts over G-7 cooperation led the yen to hit a post-war high of Y75.94 to the dollar on Aug. 19.\par \par \uc2 "I believe the (G-7) countries understand Japan's position," the prime minister said.\par \par \uc2 He also said the Bank of Japan shared the government's concerns of the yen's persistent strength, adding that he hoped the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take appropriate steps to support the economy.\par \par \uc2 -By George Nishiyama, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2818; george.nishiyama@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -Toko Sekiguchi and Yuka Hayashi contributed to this article\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 20, 2011 05:39 ET (09:39 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110920e79k00287\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
