\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: INTERVIEW: Japan PM Noda: Japan Open To Buying More EFSF Bonds\b0\par\par\uc2 841 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2011\par 14:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    --Japan PM Noda says open to buying more EFSF bonds \par    --Noda says ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market if yen moves are excessive or disorderly \par    --Noda says temporary tax hikes to fund reconstruction should be over 10 years \par (Adds more comments from Noda and background throughout) \par  \par    By George Nishiyama and Toko Sekiguchi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda said Tokyo is open to buying more bonds issued by a European bailout fund to alleviate concerns over the region's sovereign debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 "To stabilize the European financial markets and the economy, Japan needs to make a certain contribution going forward, including purchases of EFSF bonds," Noda said in an interview with Dow Jones Newswires and the Wall Street Journal on Tuesday, referring to debt issued by the European Financial Stability Facility.\par \par \uc2 Noda's comments come as the Group of 20 nations gear up for their meeting later this week, where Europe's debt problems will be one of the top items on the agenda.\par \par \uc2 He said that the European Union must first reach a consensus on how to deal with the crisis, including support for Greece, before turning to other countries for help.\par \par \uc2 European nations have increasingly been looking outside of Europe, including emerging economies such as China and Brazil, to meet their financing needs and prevent them from being ensnared by the Greek debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 Using its $1.2 trillion foreign reserves, Japan has already bought about EUR2.7 billion--or a little more than 20%--of the EFSF bonds since January to help Ireland and Portugal.\par \par \uc2 Noda's commitment for further support is a reflection of concerns that the European crisis is a major factor driving the yen to historically high levels, as investors turn to it as a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The prime minister said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the yen's moves became excessive, adding that the Group of Seven nations understand Tokyo's stance.\par \par \uc2 "There is no change in our policy to take decisive steps against disorderly or excessive moves," Noda said.\par \par \uc2 Noda ordered a yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August when he was the finance minister to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise, but doubts over G-7 cooperation led the yen to hit a post-war high of Y75.94 to the dollar on Aug. 19.\par \par \uc2 "I believe the (G-7) countries understand Japan's position," the prime minister said.\par \par \uc2 He also said the Bank of Japan shared the government's concerns of the yen's persistent strength, adding that he hoped the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take appropriate steps to support the economy, but did not elaborate on specific measures.\par \par \uc2 Noda ruled out both views that Tokyo had shifted its emphasis on mitigating the impact of the strong yen and that it had acknowledged that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength would be here to stay.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Tuesday, the government proposed a series of measures aimed at softening the impact of the soaring yen, including subsidies and loans to prevent businesses from moving overseas.\par \par \uc2 Noda, who was speaking just hours before heading to New York to address the United Nations and also hold talks with U.S. President Barack Obama, said both Japan and U.S. shared a common challenge of seeking economic growth while fixing its finances.\par \par \uc2 Japan is prepared to spend Y13 trillion over the next five years for reconstruction from the March 11 earthquake, and the government has proposed temporary tax hikes to finance the bulk of it.\par \par \uc2 Japan's outstanding debt is twice the size of its economy, and the largest among the G-7 nations.\par \par \uc2 Noda said the increases will center on corporate and income taxes which should be over a period of 10 years, adding that it was premature to discuss sales of government-held shares in \uc2 Japan Post\uc2  as additional revenue to lower the dependency on taxes to fund reconstruction.\par \par \uc2 Some ruling party lawmakers have called for the sales of \uc2 Japan Post\uc2  shares, which are worth Y9.6 trillion in book value, but a law revision is required to open the way for government sales.\par \par \uc2 Political hurdles for such legislative measures are high given that the opposition, which controls one of the two chambers of parliament, are against it.\par \par \uc2 On the issue of restarting idled nuclear reactors which face growing concerns over atomic energy after the March disasters, Noda said he hoped to get them back online once their safety is confirmed and local governments give the green light.\par \par \uc2 He said without the resumption of the idled reactors, Japan may face power shortages next summer. Asked about if Japan would be able to get by next summer if they weren't restarted, he said it would be "impossible."\par \par \uc2 -By George Nishiyama and Toko Sekiguchi, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2818; george.nishiyama@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 -Yuka Hayashi contributed to this article\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 20, 