\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Struggles Ahead Of Fed Meeting\b0\par\par\uc2 534 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2011\par 13:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Dollar weakness prevails as traders remain cautious ahead of FOMC decision Wednesday\par \par \uc2 -- Traders shrug off Italy debt downgrade\par \par \uc2 -- Decent Greek T-Bill auction, better than expected German survey data help euro\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jessica Mead \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON -(Dow Jones)- The dollar slid against other major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in European trading Tuesday as an upcoming Federal Reserve policy decision gave the euro some breathing space and allowed investors to shrug off a raft of negative euro-zone news.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are preparing themselves for further stimulus from the Federal Open Market Committee Wednesday, which may weigh heavily on the greenback, and are wary of heading into the FOMC meeting overly positive on the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves we are seeing are position-driven. Those \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that have been hit hardest will recover the most today," said Ian Stannard, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stannard said this partly explained sharp rallies in the Australian dollar and the pound, two \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  which have suffered in the past week or so.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such positioning for the Federal Reserve meeting also allowed traders to shrug off an overnight debt downgrade of Italy by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . and news that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of China Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (3988.HK) had suspended trading of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swaps with several European banks, including \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  (BNP.FR) and UBS AG (\f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 ).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But there was also some mildly supportive news for the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A decent Greek T-Bill auction helped to quell investors' fears about an imminent Greek default while a survey showed German economic expectations deteriorated by less than expected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere, Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda told Dow Jones Newswires that Tokyo is open to buying more bonds issued by a European bailout fund to alleviate concerns over the sovereign debt crisis in Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "To stabilize the European financial markets and the economy, Japan needs to make a certain contribution going forward, including purchases of EFSF bonds," Noda said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda also warned that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market if the yen's moves became excessive.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Looking ahead, traders are likely to focus on U.S. housing data at 1230 GMT, which is expected to reveal that housing starts declined in August by 2.3% on the month. Greece is also scheduled to resume talks with the troika of the European Union, the International Monetary Fund and the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at around 1700 GMT.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1043 GMT, the euro was trading at $1.3692 against the dollar, compared with $1.3685 late Monday in New York, according to trading system EBS. The dollar was at Y76.54 against the yen, compared with Y76.59, while the euro was at Y104.78 compared with Y104.89. Meanwhile, the pound was trading at $1.5706 against the dollar, compared with $1.5699 late Monday in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 77.046 compared with 76.599 late Monday in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Jessica Mead, Dow Jones Newswires, +44 (0) 20 7842 9256;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 jessica.mead@dowjones.com [ 09-20-11 0718ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1033 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020110920e79k0002z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
