\par\par \b \uc2 INDIAN MORNING BRIEFING: Asia Rattled By Renewed Concerns Of Greek Default\b0\par\par\uc2 1,236 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2011\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Energy Service\par \uc2 NRG\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (South Korean markets are closed Monday for THE Harvest Festival, while Chinese and Taiwanese markets are closed for the Mid-Autumn Festival)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA          10992.13 -303.68  -2.69% \par Nasdaq        2467.99  -61.15   -2.42% \par S&P 500       1154.23  -31.67   -2.67% \par FTSE 100      5214.65  -125.73  -2.35% \par Nikkei Stock  8562.86  -230.26  -2.62% \par Hang Seng     19298.18 -568.45  -2.86% \par Kospi         1812.93  -33.71   -1.83% \par SGX Nifty     4958.50  -84.50   -1.68% \par Sept contract \par  \par USD/JPY    77.41-44   -0.41% \par Range      77.55-77.20 \par EUR/USD    1.3586-94  -0.48% \par Range      1.3660-1.3574 \par  \par CBOT Wheat Sept   $7.010   per bushel \par Comex Gold        $1,859.50    +$2.00 \par Comex Gold Range  $1,825.50-$1,889.10 \par Nymex Crude (NY)  $87.24       -$1.81 \par  \par  \par US STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks closed sharply lower due to fresh worries of a Greek default, the surprise resignation of a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  executive board member and concerns about President Barack Obama's jobs plan.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average tumbled 303.68 points, or 2.7%, to 10992.13, its fifth drop out of the last six sessions. All 30 Dow components closed in the red.\par \par \uc2 The increasing possibility of a Greek default sent the Dow tumbling as much as 360 points before it recovered some losses in the final hour of trading. Investors are worried that a default is becoming a more-likely scenario, with the fear of how markets and the global economy would react to such a scenario weighing on investor sentiment.\par \par \uc2 "People are definitely spooked because you're never quite sure what the secondary effects are going to be," said Ben Halliburton, chief investment officer at Tradition Capital Management. "It's impossible to say until it actually happens."\par \par \uc2 The euro tumbled to a seven-month low against the dollar. Rumors also circulated about how Germany is planning to recapitalize German banks that would suffer from a Greek default.\par \par \uc2 "Germany is saying 'we're going to prepare if there is a default'," said Peter Boockvar, managing director and equity strategist at Miller Tabak & Co. "Markets are betting and people are preparing that this could be an inevitability."\par \par \uc2 The steep declines came as finance ministers and central bankers gathered in Marseille, France, for a Group of Seven meeting.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian shares dropped sharply Monday, and the euro slumped to multiyear lows against the yen as investors were rattled by fresh concerns of a Greek default, with the Tokyo stock market skidding to its lowest level in almost six months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wall Street's sharp drop on Friday and the renewed euro-zone woes sent investors scurrying out of riskier stocks into the safe-haven bonds and yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's very clear to us that this situation in Europe is not going to end well and the now plummeting Euro is trying to tell you that some sort of Greek default and subsequent European bank recapitalisation program is imminent," said Bell Potter managing director Charlie Aitken in Sydney.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were down 89 points in screen trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Tokyo, the yen's spike against the euro sent stocks tumbling with exporters bearing the brunt of the selloff. The Nikkei plummeted to its lowest level since March 15 at 8529.06 on the open.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Honda Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  was off 3.6%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sony\f28 \uc2 \f2  dropped 3.1% and industrial concern Fanuc lost 3.0%, as investors worried that the global woes would further hurt Japan's recovery from the devastating March 11 natural disasters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cyclical and growth-sensitive stocks were sold off heavily in Sydney and Wellington.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian market slipped to a four-week low, with major banks down 2.9%-3.4%, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 BHP Billiton\f28 \uc2 \f2  off 3.0%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Wellington, Auckland Airport lost 1.5% and Telecom Corp. gave up 1.4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amid the global uncertainty, investors jumped into safe-haven bond investments, with December Japanese government bond futures up 0.20 at 142.71 points.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro remained under pressure in Asia after falling sharply on Friday, amid the Greek default worries and apparent divisions in the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The surprise resignation Friday of ECB executive committee member Juergen Stark, who had opposed the bank's move to buy the bonds of struggling euro-zone countries, also hurt market sentiment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit Agricole\f28 \uc2 \f2  said the euro is to encounter more selling in the near term, and added that "with a Greek default only a matter of 'when,' not 'if' in the view of many, investors' sentiment is unlikely to improve for now."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tapped a fresh 10-year low against the yen at Y104.90 and a seven-month low of $1.3550 against the U.S. dollar in early Asian trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In recent trade, the euro was fetching Y105.40, from Y105.94 late in New York Friday, and at $1.3598, from $1.3648. The dollar was at Y77.50, compared with Y77.74.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold was at $1,847.60 a troy ounce, down $11 from New York Friday\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nymex crude for October delivery was down $1.04 at $86.20 per barrel on Globex.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES \par EU's Rehn Sees Greek Bailout Review Concluding By End September \par Greek PM Warns Against Euro Zone Exit, Rules Out Early Polls \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  CEO: French Banks Can Handle Greece Problems \par French Minister: Won't Lend To Greece If Efforts Insufficient \par German Govt Nominates Asmussen To Succeed Stark At ECB \par Japan Asks U.S. Geithner To Share Worries Over Yen \par Greece To Introduce New Property Tax To Meet Budget Goals \par G-8: International Financial Institutions Pledged To Raise Help For Arab Spring To USD38 Bln \par German Econ Min: Can't Rule Out Orderly Greek Insolvency- Report \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  May Cut Ratings Of France's Top Three Banks - Sources \par Italy's Tremonti Reviewing Economic Growth Measures - Reports \par Japan Trade Minister's Resignation Rocks New Cabinet \par China End-August M2 Up 13.5% On Year; Expected Up 14.5% \par Ex-PBOC Adviser: China's Economy Headed For Soft Landing--Report \par China August Trade Surplus Narrows As Imports Surge \par Japan Finance Minister Azumi: G-7 Shares View Strong Yen Could Hurt Japan Recovery \par UK's Osborne Says New EU Treaty Likely In Next Two Years \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOF Official: Don't Think Solo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Precluded As Policy Tool \par IMF Likely To Re-Activate $580B Resource Pool Amid Europe Crisis-Sources \par China August Crude-Oil Imports Rise To 21.04 Mln Tons \par Alibaba Chairman:Taobao Targeting CNY1 Tln In Transactions In 2012 \par Japan Releases New IAEA Report To Win Back Intl Confidence \par NWS Holdings: Unit To Raise Stake In China Road Operator For US$280 Mln \par Scotiabank To Buy 19.99% Stake In Bank of Guangzhou For US$727 Mln; Paves Way For Listing \par US Citizens Suspected In Possible 9/11 Terror Plot \par ECB's Stark To Leave Post By End Of Year \par Mexican Finance Minister Quits To Run For Presidency \par Obama Asks Congress For $447B In Tax Cuts, Spending \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 TNK-BP\f28 \uc2 \f2  Corruption Program Leads To 37 Criminal Cases \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo\f28 \uc2 \f2  Weighs Bid For KBC Private Banking Unit \par Greece Rejects Default Talk \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lufthansa\f28 \uc2 \f2  Formally Starts BMI Sale Process -- Source \par NYSE CEO: Crisis Supports Deal With Deutsche Boerse \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 09-11-11 2300ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 62500   \par \par \uc2 Dokument NRG0000020110912e79c0000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
