\f28 \uc2 \f2 Sony\f28 \uc2 \f2  dropped 3.1% and industrial concern Fanuc lost 3.0%, as investors worried that the global woes would further hurt Japan's recovery from the devastating March 11 natural disasters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cyclical and growth-sensitive stocks were sold off heavily in Sydney and Wellington.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Australian market slipped to a four-week low, with major banks down 2.9%-3.4%, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 BHP Billiton\f28 \uc2 \f2  off 3.0%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Wellington, Auckland Airport lost 1.5% and Telecom Corp. gave up 1.4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amid the global uncertainty, investors jumped into safe-haven bond investments, with December Japanese government bond futures up 0.20 at 142.71 points.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro remained under pressure in Asia after falling sharply on Friday, amid the Greek default worries and apparent divisions in the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The surprise resignation Friday of ECB executive committee member Juergen Stark, who had opposed the bank's move to buy the bonds of struggling euro-zone countries, also hurt market sentiment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit Agricole\f28 \uc2 \f2  said the euro is to encounter more selling in the near term, and added that "with a Greek default only a matter of 'when,' not 'if' in the view of many, investors' sentiment is unlikely to improve for now."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tapped a fresh 10-year low against the yen at Y104.90 and a seven-month low of $1.3550 against the U.S. dollar in early Asian trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In recent trade, the euro was fetching Y105.40, from Y105.94 late in New York Friday, and at $1.3598, from $1.3648. The dollar was at Y77.50, compared with Y77.74.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \p