\par\par \b \uc2 Euro seen under pressure on lack of G7 support\b0\par\par\uc2 877 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2011\par 16:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Euro can fall to $1.35 vs dollar, 105 yen\par \par \uc2 * Lack of consensus from G7 will hurt risk appetite\par \par \uc2 * Dollar, core govt bonds seen major beneficiaries\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  watch\par \par \uc2 By Anirban Nag\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 11 (Reuters) - The euro and growth-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may fall on Monday, hit by a lack of concrete measures from Group of Seven finance chiefs to address either faltering growth, the escalating euro zone debt crisis, or exchange rate volatility.\par \par \uc2 The dollar, yen and, to a lesser extent, Swiss franc are set to advance with more investors seeking safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the back of rising financial market stress.\par \par \uc2 That will raise the risk of more solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese and Swiss authorities.\par \par \uc2 The flight to safety should drive core government bonds like German Bunds and British gilts higher, leading to wider spreads over euro zone peripheral debt, while European banking shares may ease on mounting worries about contagion engulfing bigger economies like Italy and Spain.\par \par \uc2 Finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven industrialised nations pledged to respond in a concerted matter to a global slowdown. However, they offered no specific steps and differed in emphasis on Europe's debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That will likely offer little solace to investors who had expected some sort of coordinated policy response from G7 policymakers at a time when stock markets have been falling and global growth in showing increasing signs of stalling.\par \par \uc2 "As this falls short of any commitment to undertake co-ordinated action in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, investors are likely to react with disappointment when trading resumes on Monday," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 UBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He expected \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stay on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  watch.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister, Jun Azumi, said he met with little resistance to further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the G7 meeting. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Aug. 4 to topple the yen from a record high against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "We expect Japan's authorities will act again unilaterally if dollar/yen tests its post-war lows of 75.95 yen. As a result we think investors should instead keep favouring the dollar now when they seek safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," \uc2 UBS\uc2 's Mohi-uddin said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index , which measures its performance against a basket of six \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which includes the euro, yen\par \par \uc2 and sterling , rose to its highest in six months at 77.276 on Friday.\par \par \uc2 In a bullish signal, it closed above its 55-week moving average at 77.01. Resistance was seen at the base of the weekly Ichimoku cloud around 78.05, while strong resistance was at the 38.2 percent retracement of the index's fall from a high of 88.71 on June 7, 2010 to a low of 72.696 on May 4, 2011 which comes in at 78.80.\par \par \uc2 The dollar is set to make strong gains against the euro\par \par \uc2 , which last week fell to its lowest in six months, at around $1.3627. The euro posted its biggest weekly fall since mid-August last year, with many looking for it to test $1.35 in the near term.\par \par \uc2 EURO ON THE WAY DOWN\par \par \uc2 The euro also fell sharply against the safe-haven Japanese yen on Friday, dropping to its lowest in nearly a decade. It ended the week at 105.85 yen, and a break below the psychologically key 105.00 level could see it drop towards 100 yen in coming weeks, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Howard Wheeldon, a strategist at BCG Capital Partners, said the weekend's developments provided little confidence to investors in the euro zone, and the coming week will see increased volatility in stock markets.\par \par \uc2 That could hurt the euro more in coming days.\par \par \uc2 The euro was sold off last week after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet shifted the monetary stance from a hawkish bias to a more neutral one.\par \par \uc2 The shock resignation of ECB board member Juergen Stark, which highlighted sharp divisions within the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  over purchases of government bonds in the secondary market and concerns that Greece may not secure its latest aid tranche from the IMF/European Union, also added to the euro's woes.\par \par \uc2 Investors will also likely be unsettled by a weekend report from Der Speigel magazine that the German finance ministry was looking at scenarios that included Greece abandoning the euro.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, latest data from the Commodity Futures Trading Commission showed speculators added to their bearish bets against the euro in the week to Sept. 6.\par \par \uc2 "With $1.40 going last week, I think the euro could fall to $1.35 in the next few days," said Michael Derks, chief strategist at FXPRO. "The dollar be will the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that will gain from safe-haven inflows given the risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the yen and the line in the sand that has been drawn on the Swiss franc by the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 On the charts, near term support was seen at $1.3426, a low hit on Feb. 14 and from where the euro started its move to a 17-month high at $1.4939 struck on May 4.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Anirban Nag; Editing by Dan Lalor)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EURO   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110911e79b000f8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
