\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan's  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Action Plan Draws No G-7 Ire\b0\par\par\uc2 670 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2011\par 04:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Japan's Finance Minister Jun Azumi says he told G-7 of his intention to take "decisive steps" against strong yen if necessary\par \par \uc2 -- Azumi says G-7 counterparts didn't question his \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy\par \par \uc2 -- Azumi adds that he didn't ask for coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by G-7\par \par \uc2 -- Azumi says G-7 shared view that strong yen could hurt Japan's economic recovery\par \par \uc2 -- Analysts doubt G-7 silence means go-ahead for Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 (Recasts lead, adds comments from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister in 2nd, 10th and 12th paragraphs, comments from senior Japanese MOF official in 3rd paragraph, remarks from \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst in 6th, 7th paragraphs)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MARSEILLE, France (Dow Jones)--Japan stressed Friday that its pledge to take "decisive steps" against the yen's relentless rise drew no objections from finance ministers of the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations, but it is not clear whether their silence is tantamount to an endorsement of further Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I sought to gain their understanding about our views on the yen's strength and our stance to take decisive steps when it is deemed necessary," Finance Minister Jun Azumi said at a news conference after a one-day meeting of G-7 finance ministers and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chiefs. "And actually no country asked for any further comments from me regarding those points."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A senior official from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also said "there was no objection to Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy" during the G-7 talks. He added that "although prior consultations (with relevant authorities) are necessary, we don't think solo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is precluded" as a policy tool.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The high-profile gathering came at a crucial time for Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After hitting a post-war high of Y75.94 to the dollar on Aug. 19, the yen has continued to trade around that level partly on speculation that opposition from the U.S. and Europe may make it hard for Tokyo to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen. Though only about a week into his new job and with little prior experience in economic policy making, Azumi headed into the meeting with a challenging mission to enlist U.S. and European support for measures to fight the strong yen, which is threatening Japan's economic rebound from the March 11 earthquake.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That G-7 nations didn't object to Japan's action plan may be good news for Tokyo. But that is not likely enough to convince the market that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has official G-7 permission to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , said Koji Fukaya, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Given the U.S. and Europe's own economic struggles, "it is hard to imagine them saying to Japan, 'Go ahead. Please do it. You're in trouble,'" he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some observers also said the group might have been too focused on Europe's debt crisis--the main agenda item---to spend much time discussing exchange rates. Indeed, a senior U.S. official said the G-7 had no formal talks on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Doubts over G-7 cooperation on the yen have grown after Japan let the yen hit its new high without a fight---after the U.S. government and the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  expressed concern about Tokyo's solo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Aug. 4.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Azumi said he didn't ask the G-7 to jointly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen, as they did on March 18.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In its wrap-up statement, the group continued to warn against "excess" exchange-rate volatility, but again stressed the importance of rates being "market-determined." Analysts interpret this phrase as a general objection to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Azumi said he had told the G-7 he was "very concerned" about the yen and that the G-7 likely understood the damage it could do to Japan's economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 10-09-11 0238GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110910e79a00009\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
