\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: BOJ Chief To Urge Europe To Tackle Debt To Ease Yen's Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2011\par 12:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --BOJ Gov. Shirakawa plans to emphasize the importance of Europe firmly tackling its debt problems at the G-7\par \par \uc2 --Shirakawa says Europe's debt problems are a reason behind yen rises\par \par \uc2 --Shirakawa says he will seek the G-7's understanding for Japan's yen problems and policy response\par \par \uc2 (Recasts lead, adds Shirakawa comments in 4th and final paragraphs, background) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MARSEILLE, France (Dow Jones)--The head of Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Friday he will use the coming Group of Seven gathering to urge Europe to work to fix its debt crisis, which he cites as a major reason behind the yen's relentless climb.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa also vowed to "gain understanding" from other G-7 leading industrialized countries for Japan's efforts to defend its still-fragile economic recovery from the yen's rise, which makes exports less competitive.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shirakawa's comments suggest Japan feels the U.S. and Europe should allow it to take steps--such as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --to curb the yen's climb, since it is due mainly to the economic problems of these trade partners.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Growing uncertainty over the world's economy has lured money into the yen, which is seen as a relatively safe \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and has caused the yen to appreciate," Shirakawa told reporters ahead of a one-day meeting of G-7 finance ministers and central bankers later in the day in Marseille, France.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Surely, there are various reasons behind this uncertainty over the global economy. But one factor is Europe's debt problems. I'd like to state that for Europe, it is more important than anything to firmly deal with the continent's debt problems and that would indirectly have a positive impact on Japan's economy."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shirakawa added that he would like to gain understanding about "various problems facing Japan's economy, particularly the negative impact of sharp yen rises, which have come in the middle of reconstruction efforts after the earthquake, as well as Japan's policy response."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The G-7 meeting offers Japan a rare opportunity to directly make its case to an international forum. It comes as Japanese manufacturers and politicians have been pressuring the government and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to do more to curb the strong yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors may pay close attention to Europe's response, as its staunch objection to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is seen as one reason for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's lack of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since Aug. 4. After prolonged negotiations with the U.S. and Europe, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unilaterally \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that day to weaken the yen, but later got complaints from both.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Thursday, Japan's new finance minister Jun Azumi said he will clarify at the G-7 talks his intention to resort to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if needed. He also aims to convince other group members that the strong yen could undermine Japan's recovery from the March 11 earthquake and tsunami, and eventually hurt global growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But with the U.S. and Europe both facing a slowdown in their own economies, it may be difficult for Azumi to win support for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , some Japanese officials recently told Dow Jones Newswires.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shirakawa added that the U.S. Federal Reserve, the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the BOJ are already offering "a sense of assurance" to the banking sector through programs to supply dollar funds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 09, 2011 06:27 ET (10:27 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110909e799002uv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
