\par\par \b \uc2 G7 Meeting Sets Stage For Widening  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Woes\b0\par\par\uc2 720 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2011\par 22:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Weak global economy triggering new fears of a '\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war'\par \par \uc2 --Japan snipes at S. Korea; Norway chides Switzerland\par \par \uc2 --Everybody is mad at Europe and the U.S.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Javier E. David \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--It's appropriate the Group of Seven is meeting in France. It should make it easier for some G-7 officials to partake of some cheese with their whine.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Europe's debt crisis is roiling markets worldwide, prompting traders to jettison the euro. The squall buffeting the global economy has sent investors in desperate search for safe harbors. The dollar's no longer the automatic destination for worriers, thanks to a weak U.S. economy and ultra-accomodative monetary policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ensuing hunt for the next best refuge is making the G-7 meeting in Marseille an uncomfortable affair. Canada and Norway are both faulting Switzerland's dramatic move to bring the franc, the safe haven of choice until the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  imposed a ceiling on Tuesday, to heel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In an audacious bout of the pot calling the kettle black, Japan is demanding South Korea stop deliberately weakening the won--mere weeks after the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to undercut the yen's record strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And just about everyone is mad at the euro zone, with leaders struggling to prevent Greece's financial conflagration from scorching everything around it.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Friday, Canadian Finance Minister Jim Flaherty took an uncharacteristic swipe at the embattled country. He told Canada's Globe & Mail that Greece would probably be ejected from the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if its government failed to undertake needed reforms.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speculation has intensified that Germany might let Greece default or force it from the euro. But that could trigger a massive devaluation, which could lead to more social instability and unrest, said Krishna Memani, portfolio manager and director of fixed income at OppenheimerFunds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "You can't spit it out, and you can't swallow it," Memani said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Canada and Japan will participate in the G-7 confab, although some of the targets won't be present. However, some analysts wonder if the bickering might put pressure on the G-7 to forge a policy response that will both settle markets and prevent unwanted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nervous investors are selling euros and latching onto seemingly safer assets, wherever they can be found. In normal times, the dollar would be the recipient of safe-haven flows because of its status as the world's largest reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But low Treasury yields and loose monetary policy--which may get even looser after the Federal Reserve meets in late September--has dramatically diminished the greenback's allure. Partisan rancor surrounding the U.S.'s efforts to curb its deficit and revive the economy have broadened the list of reasons why the greenback has fallen steeply since 2008. The dollar's weakness has infuriated many countries that have seen their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  surge as a result.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Norwegian krone has jumped since the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's move. That has drawn the ire of Norwegian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Gov. Oystein Olsen, who warned that a swift policy response, which could include lower interest rates or a move similar to the SNB's, could be in the making.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All this talk has touched off what Brazil warned was a brewing "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war" in which competitive devaluations spark trade disputes between major economies. Canada's Flaherty also echoed this fear on Friday as he, too, chided the SNB's new policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are also viewed by analysts as acceptable safe-haven assets. However, the export-reliant economies in the region mean that officials there are notoriously prickly about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That idea was driven home by Japan's top trade negotiator, Hideichi Okada. He took aim at South Korea's efforts to devalue the won, saying the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's relative weakness against the yen posed a "serious challenge" to Japanese industries and that it was inappropriate for Seoul to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, except in extraordinary circumstances.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is especially sensitive about a strong yen, as it seeks to recover from a devastating earthquake that shook the economy to its foundations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Javier E. David, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4564; javier.david@dowjones.com [ 09-09-11 2039GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110909e799001bn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
