\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 942 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2011\par 10:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OBAMA URGES CONGRESS TO IMMEDIATELY PASS $447B JOBS PLAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President Barack Obama on Thursday proposed a $447 billion plan to revive the U.S. economy, saying his plan will provide an instant boost to the economy if Congress immediately passes it. (published 2340 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA INFLATION MODERATES IN AUGUST; POLICY LIKELY ON HOLD \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's inflation moderated in August, raising market hopes the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is done with tightening policy, although inflation remains at a relatively high level. (published 0214 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GREEK DEBT ROLLOVER PROCESS "SATISFACTORY" \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ATHENS --A debt rollover plan vital to Greece's economic survival is making "satisfactory progress," a Greek government official said Friday as a deadline for euro-zone banks and funds to express interest expired. (published 0907 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMAN AUG FINAL CPI REVISED UP TO 0.0% MM, +2.4% YY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German final consumer prices rose at a slightly faster pace on the year in August than preliminary data showed, the Federal Statistics Office, or Destatis, said Friday. (published 0612 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FRENCH FINANCE MINISTER: G-7 MAINLY FOCUSED ON GROWTH - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Officials from the Group of Seven leading countries meeting in Marseilles Friday are mainly focusing on growth, French Finance Minister Francois Baroin says in an interview with French daily Le Figaro Friday. (published 0606 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ECB STARK: PREPARED TO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AS LONG AS LOW INTEREST RATES NOT TRANSLATED INTO LENDING RATES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Jurgen Stark, Germany's representative on the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's executive board, said the ECB remains ready to step into bond markets and its measures will remain in place as long as they need to be. (published 0327 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FRENCH BUDGET GAP END-JULY EUR86.6B VS EUR93.1B END-JULY '10 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 France's budget deficit narrowed during the first seven months of 2011 from a year earlier, due to lower public spending while revenue was marginally higher, the French budget ministry said Friday. (published 0805 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TURKEY JULY INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT RISES AHEAD OF FORECASTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Turkish industrial production rose 6.9% on the year in July, comfortably topping market forecasts and suggesting that Turkey's red-hot growth isn't cooling as rapidly as economists had expected. (published 0824 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par IMF LAGARDE: GROWTH RISKS HIGHER, COUNTIES MUST ACT NOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Downside risks to growth across the world have increased and countries must act now, boldly and together, Christine Lagarde, managing director of the International Monetary Fund, said Friday. (published 0846 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA JAN-AUG NON-RURAL FAI +25.0% ON YEAR; MARKET EXPECTED +25.2% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's fixed-asset investment in non-rural areas, a closely watched indicator of construction activity, rose 25.0% in the January-August period compared with the same period a year earlier, data from the National Bureau of Statistics showed Friday. (published 0537 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA AUG INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT +13.5% ON YEAR; MARKET EXPECTED +13.7% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's value-added industrial output rose 13.5% in August from a year earlier, slowing from July's 14.0% increase, data from the National Bureau of Statistics showed Friday. (published 0536 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN 2Q GDP SHRINKS MORE THAN INITIALLY REPORTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese economy contracted more severely than previously reported in the April-June period, data released Friday showed, with firms cutting back on capital spending at a faster pace in the wake of the March 11 earthquake and tsunami. (published 0309 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FUJIMURA: CABINET TO WORK WITH OPPOSITION TO AID COS COPING WITH STRONG YEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's top government spokesman said Friday that the government must gain opposition cooperation to promptly tackle the effect of the high yen on Japanese businesses. (published 0618 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN ECON MIN FURUKAWA: NEED TO PAY CLOSE ATTENTION TO OVERSEAS ECON, YEN RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's economy minister said Friday that while the economy has picked up as supply-side constraints have eased, it is necessary to keep a careful watch on overseas economies and the yen's rise. (published 0151 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  PLANS TO INFORM G-7 OF ITS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  POLICY \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister said Thursday he will inform top finance officials from the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations about his intention to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market if he spots "excessive" speculation bolstering the yen. (published 0010 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA FINANCE MINISTER SIGNALS NO NEW CAPITAL FLOW STEPS IN NEAR TERM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan on Friday signaled that Asia's fourth-largest economy won't take any new action on managing foreign capital flows in the near term, emphasizing the need to preserve systemic stability. (published 0417 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BERNANKE SAYS FED STANDS READY TO ACT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke said Thursday the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has the tools to provide more support to the sluggish U.S. economy, but declined to say whether it is prepared to use them in less than two weeks. (published 2039 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SENATE VOTE MEANS THAT US BORROWING LIMIT IS IMMEDIATELY HIKED BY $500B \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. Senate defeated an attempt by Republicans to slow down a $500 billion increase in the federal government's borrowing limit in a vote that means the borrowing limit is immediately hiked to $15.19 trillion. (published 0039 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: DOLLAR REMAINS POINTING HIGHER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: the RSI is capped by a declining trend line, the pair stands below its new resistance and remains under pressure. Suggests short positions below 1.3970, with 1.3875 and 1.3815 in sight. The upside breakout of 1.3970 will open the way to 1.4020 and 1.4110. (published 0711 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 09-09-11 0853GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110909e799000pb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
