\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 875 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2011\par 08:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN AUG FINAL CPI REVISED UP TO 0.0% MM, +2.4% YY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German final consumer prices rose at a slightly faster pace on the year in August than preliminary data showed, the Federal Statistics Office, or Destatis, said Friday. (published 0612 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FRENCH FINANCE MINISTER: G-7 MAINLY FOCUSED ON GROWTH - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Officials from the Group of Seven leading countries meeting in Marseilles Friday are mainly focusing on growth, French Finance Minister Francois Baroin says in an interview with French daily Le Figaro Friday. (published 0606 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ECB STARK: PREPARED TO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AS LONG AS LOW INTEREST RATES NOT TRANSLATED INTO LENDING RATES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Jurgen Stark, Germany's representative on the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's executive board, said the ECB remains ready to step into bond markets and its measures will remain in place as long as they need to be. (published 0327 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA INFLATION MODERATES IN AUGUST; POLICY LIKELY ON HOLD \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's inflation moderated in August, raising market hopes the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is done with tightening policy, although inflation remains at a relatively high level. (published 0214 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA JAN-AUG NON-RURAL FAI +25.0% ON YEAR; MARKET EXPECTED +25.2% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's fixed-asset investment in non-rural areas, a closely watched indicator of construction activity, rose 25.0% in the January-August period compared with the same period a year earlier, data from the National Bureau of Statistics showed Friday. (published 0537 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA AUG INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT +13.5% ON YEAR; MARKET EXPECTED +13.7% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's value-added industrial output rose 13.5% in August from a year earlier, slowing from July's 14.0% increase, data from the National Bureau of Statistics showed Friday. (published 0536 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN 2Q GDP SHRINKS MORE THAN INITIALLY REPORTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese economy contracted more severely than previously reported in the April-June period, data released Friday showed, with firms cutting back on capital spending at a faster pace in the wake of the March 11 earthquake and tsunami. (published 0309 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FUJIMURA: CABINET TO WORK WITH OPPOSITION TO AID COS COPING WITH STRONG YEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's top government spokesman said Friday that the government must gain opposition cooperation to promptly tackle the effect of the high yen on Japanese businesses. (published 0618 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN ECON MIN FURUKAWA: NEED TO PAY CLOSE ATTENTION TO OVERSEAS ECON, YEN RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's economy minister said Friday that while the economy has picked up as supply-side constraints have eased, it is necessary to keep a careful watch on overseas economies and the yen's rise. (published 0151 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  PLANS TO INFORM G-7 OF ITS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  POLICY \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister said Thursday he will inform top finance officials from the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations about his intention to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market if he spots "excessive" speculation bolstering the yen. (published 0010 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA FINANCE MINISTER SIGNALS NO NEW CAPITAL FLOW STEPS IN NEAR TERM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan on Friday signaled that Asia's fourth-largest economy won't take any new action on managing foreign capital flows in the near term, emphasizing the need to preserve systemic stability. (published 0417 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BERNANKE SAYS FED STANDS READY TO ACT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke said Thursday the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has the tools to provide more support to the sluggish U.S. economy, but declined to say whether it is prepared to use them in less than two weeks. (published 2039 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par OBAMA URGES CONGRESS TO IMMEDIATELY PASS $447B JOBS PLAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President Barack Obama on Thursday proposed a $447 billion plan to revive the U.S. economy, saying his plan will provide an instant boost to the economy if Congress immediately passes it. (published 2340 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SENATE VOTE MEANS THAT US BORROWING LIMIT IS IMMEDIATELY HIKED BY $500B \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. Senate defeated an attempt by Republicans to slow down a $500 billion increase in the federal government's borrowing limit in a vote that means the borrowing limit is immediately hiked to $15.19 trillion. (published 0039 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TAIWAN TSAI PICKS EX-INTERIOR MINISTER AS VICE-PRESIDENTIAL RUNNING MATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tsai Ing-wen, the presidential candidate for Taiwan's opposition Democratic Progressive Party, said Friday she had picked Su Jia-chyuan, a former interior minister, as her vice-presidential running mate for the election in January. (published 0612 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA MAY LAUNCH PROGRAM FOR INVESTING OFFSHORE YUAN THIS MONTH - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China may launch the so-called "yuan-denominated foreign direct investment" program this month, the China Securities Journal reported Friday, citing Chinese Commerce Ministry spokesman Shen Danyang. (published 2334 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MALAYSIA JULY INDUSTRIAL OUTPUT SHRINKS UNEXPECTEDLY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's industrial output unexpectedly contracted in July, as the export-reliant nation's key manufacturing sector slowed on weak global demand while mining output continued to shrink. (published 0606 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA TRADE SECRETARY: AUGUST EXPORTS UP 44% ON YEAR AT $24.3 BLN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's merchandise exports grew 44.2% in August from a year earlier, totaling $24.3 billion, sharply slowing from the previous month's pace, Commerce Secretary Rahul Khullar said Friday. (published 0748 GMT) [ 09-09-11 0657GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110909e799000jb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
