\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 5-Japan machinery orders slump, cast doubt on recovery\b0\par\par\uc2 797 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2011\par 10:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * July core machinery orders fall 8.2 pct vs forecast of 4.1 pct fall\par \par \uc2 * Follows disappointing output, exports, capex figures\par \par \uc2 * Strong yen, potential delay in reconstruction are risks\par \par \uc2 * Economics Minister Furukawa calls for BOJ action\par \par \uc2 * Many analysts expect BOJ to ease in October - poll\par \par \uc2 (Adds economics minister comment, BOJ poll)\par \par \uc2 By Stanley White and Rie Ishiguro\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 8 (Reuters) - Japan's core machinery orders tumbled twice as much as expected in July in a sign that companies are delaying investment due to worries about the strong yen, faltering global growth and slow progress in rebuilding from the March earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 The data is the latest in a series of disappointing figures, including July exports and output, that cast doubt on the strength of Japan's recovery from its post-quake slump.\par \par \uc2 The report could put pressure on the government and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  for action to prevent additional yen rises by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and further easing monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Many market players expect the BOJ to ease monetary policy next month with the most likely trigger a renewed spike in the yen that would hurt the export-reliant economy.\par \par \uc2 The brisk pace at which Japanese companies restored supply chains and production that had been disrupted by the March 11 disaster convinced economists that the world's third-largest economy would resume growth this quarter, expanding at the fastest rate among major developed nations.\par \par \uc2 Yet the latest statistics suggest the yen's recent strength, concerns about Europe's sovereign debt crisis and fears that the world economy could slip back into recession are beginning to bite.\par \par \uc2 "Monthly data suggest the economy has already seen a V-shaped recovery and is likely to see only flat growth or even deceleration from the autumn and there is an emerging risk of a contraction in the fourth quarter," said Taro Saito, senior economist at NLI Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 Core machinery orders, a leading indicator for corporate capital spending, fell 8.2 percent in July from the previous month, Cabinet Office data showed on Thursday. That compared with a median market forecast for a 4.1 percent decline and follows a 7.7 percent rise in June.\par \par \uc2 Separate data showed the current account surplus fell more than expected in the year to July as exports weakened. A service sector sentiment index, also published on Thursday, fell for the first time in five months in another sign that the yen's strength and recession fears were sapping business confidence.\par \par \uc2 GLOBAL SLOWDOWN WEIGHS\par \par \uc2 Sumco Corp\uc2  , the world's No.2 supplier of silicon wafers used to make chips, on Wednesday slashed its annual operating profit forecast by 37 percent on weak PC demand and slower-than-expected growth in smartphones and tablet PCs, and said the fragile economy could dampen demand further.\par \par \uc2 Adding to pressure on exporters, the yen has been attracting safe-haven demand from investors unsettled by Europe's debt crisis and the U.S. economic slowdown, even as Japan struggles with its own debt burden and its new government faces a battle to win a consensus over how to fund its biggest rebuilding effort since the years after the World War Two.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stabilised in the past few days below record highs hit in mid-August, allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its policy on hold on Wednesday after it eased in August.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ stuck to its view that the economy would resume moderate growth from the next quarter, but highlighted risks posed by the yen and growing uncertainty about the health of the European and U.S. economies.\par \par \uc2 Economics Minister Motohisa Furukawa said the yen had risen to levels much higher than assumed by companies, potentially hurting corporate profits and prompting companies to relocate overseas.\par \par \uc2 "I expect the BOJ to take appropriate and bold steps to support the economy through monetary policy," he told reporters, although he stopped short of specifying what action it should take.\par \par \uc2 Many economists still think the momentum of upswing in output in the months after the quake will be enough to produce robust overall growth in July-September after three quarters of contraction.\par \par \uc2 But they have started questioning the assumption that foreign demand and reconstruction spending will carry the economy forward in the final quarter of this year and early in 2012.\par \par \uc2 "Full-fledged reconstruction demand is unlikely to emerge throughout this year given the delay in the third supplementary budget," NLI's Saito said.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda's new government formed last week is due to compile the main reconstruction budget in mid-October at the earliest.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 77.325 Japanese Yen)\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Tomasz Janowski and Edmund Klamann)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 5)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110911e7980007e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
