\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: US Treasury Urges Europe To Take 'Decisive' Actions\b0\par\par\uc2 997 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2011\par 22:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --Europe needs to take more forceful, swift action to stem its sovereign debt crisis, Washington warns\par \par \uc2 --A stronger European firewall is critical, while banks need to recapitalize to protect against rising risks\par \par \uc2 --The U.S., Japan and China should focus on policies to boost global growth\par \par \uc2 (Adds Treasury comments, background and details beginning in the 10th paragraph.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Ian Talley and Michael Crittenden \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--Europe must take "decisive' actions to solve its growing sovereign-debt crisis, including recapitalizing its banks and assuring ailing economies that it will provide affordable financing as needed, a senior U.S. Treasury official said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As concerns mount over another possible global recession, U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner will fly late Thursday to southern France for a Group of Seven finance ministers' meeting, and he will deliver a clear message that Europe must act swiftly to douse the growing sovereign debt crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Europe's most important contribution will be to take decisive actions to build a stronger firewall and prevent the further spread of the crisis," the senior Treasury official said in a briefing with reporters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Europe needs to have the capacity to mobilize financing rapidly, efficiently and on a scale commensurate to the challenges," the official said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The warnings come amid ongoing reluctance from some members in the euro zone, in particular Germany and Finland, to do what Washington believes is necessary to stem the financial crisis. Finland, for example, wants Greece to post collateral as a condition for putting up additional funds--a requirement that some economists say adds to the cost of lending to a country on the razor's edge of a hard default on its debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In July, European Union leaders agreed to bolster its EUR500 billion bailout facility by giving it the flexibility to buy sovereign debt in the secondary market, lend to member countries pre-emptively and recapitalize banks. But parliaments, most importantly Germany's, have yet to ratify the agreement. The pace of European lawmakers has been far too sluggish for markets that are increasingly betting on sovereign debt defaults.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Europe needs to ensure the support can be deployed rapidly and without cumbersome additional national approval mechanisms that could undermine" rescue efforts, the Treasury official said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists have been pressing Europe to move towards fiscal federalism, which would allow for a more rapid response to crises. Brussels could then use a euro-zone bond to help countries in need. Washington supports the idea of more centralized economic policy-making in Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In the coming week, European leaders must implement those summit commitments in a way that shows markets that Europe will do what it takes...to ensure that Spain, Italy and other vulnerable euro-area sovereigns can borrow at sustainable rates," the person added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As part of those efforts, it's "vitally important" that European officials provide liquidity to its financial sector and recapitalize its banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Treasury official's comments come on the heels of a call by the International Monetary Fund for Europe to require mandatory recapitalization of its banks given their substantial exposure to failing sovereigns in the euro zone. The IMF and European officials have been bickering over how to value and account for European bank exposure to rotten sovereign debt on the balance sheets. Based on market valuations of debt and data released in Europe's latest stress tests, some estimates have put that figure around EUR200 billion. But, European officials have argued that figure doesn't account for gains from other sovereigns, such as German bunds, that could offset those potential losses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, the rising risk of a debt default, particularly by Athens--where financing talks between European, IMF and Greek officials broke down last week--and the potential for international contagion have bolstered arguments for recapitalization of European banks seen as short of funding.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The rising cost of funding for European banks also suggests that European authorities and European banks need to take steps to strengthen confidence," the Treasury official said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The governments of vulnerable economies need to continue delivering difficult and painful reforms to restore growth and convince markets they are on a sustainable path, the official added. Amid a worsening economic environment, Greece has failed to meet some of its rudimentary financing-program targets. And Italian authorities are already back-tracking from fiscal restructuring policies that analysts said were only the first step of a needed larger program.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Given the risk the European crisis will pull the U.S. and the rest of the global economy into another recession, the sovereign debt woes will be the prime agenda at the G-7 meeting. But Washington would much rather be focusing on growth. Obama on Thursday will announce his plan to fight stubbornly high unemployment in the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan and China can contribute to boosting global output, the Treasury official said. Tokyo can accelerate its infrastructure investment following the devastating earthquake and tsunami earlier this year. Meanwhile, Beijing "has the capacity to unlock broader adjustments in emerging markets by allowing its exchange rate to adjust more rapidly," the official said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury declined to comment on Switzerland's moves to rein in the franc. Switzerland's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Tuesday set a cap on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's value. The Treasury official noted, however, the country had borne a "disproportionate" burden of the sovereign debt costs as markets bought the safe-haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and there were certainly disorderly movements in the Swiss franc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asked about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the official said, "The G-7 in the past has said it would be vigilant on excessive volatility, disorderly movements in exchange rates," the official said. "My anticipation is that the position with regard to support for market-based exchange rates will continue to be a central thrust in our discussions."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ian Talley and Michael Crittenden, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-631-5794; ian.talley@dowjones.com [ 09-07-11 1657ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110907e797000u3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
