\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 3-BOJ keeps policy on hold as yen stabilises\b0\par\par\uc2 654 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2011\par 05:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 7 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan kept monetary policy unchanged, saving its limited policy options to support the fragile economy for later with the yen off its record highs.\par \par \uc2 **************************************************************\par \par \uc2  KEY POINTS:\par \par \uc2  -- As widely expected, the BOJ maintained its key interest rate at a range of zero to 0.1 percent by a unanimous vote and held off on loosening policy further.\par \par \uc2  -- The BOJ stuck to its view that Japan's economy was picking up steadily on increases in output and exports. It also maintained its view that Japan's economy will resume a moderate recovery from October.\par \par \uc2  -- In its statement after the rate decision, the BOJ also said it would carefully examine how uncertainty on overseas economies and the ensuing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and market moves could affect Japan's economy.\par \par \uc2  -- BOJ Governor Masaaki Shirakawa will hold an embargoed news conference with his comments expected to come out sometime after 4:15 p.m. (0715 GMT).\par \par \uc2  COMMENTARY:\par \par \uc2  HIROAKI MUTO, SENIOR ECONOMIST, SUMITOMO MITSUI ASSET MANAGEMENT CO, TOKYO\par \par \uc2  "The BOJ will have to steadily increase its asset purchases in the future in order to avoid getting caught up in a debate about whether it should directly underwrite JGBs.\par \par \uc2  "Japan can't go the same route as the Swiss, because when you consider the size of Japan's economy it would take some type of Plaza Accord agreement among G7 countries. Right now everyone wants a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so there's no incentive for the G7 to reach such an agreement."    \par \par \uc2  TAKESHI MINAMI, CHIEF ECONOMIST, NORINCHUKIN RESEARCH INSTITUTE, TOKYO\par \par \uc2  "The BOJ probably opted to wait until they see how this month's FOMC affects markets and until the new government sets the tone on what it wants from the BOJ.\par \par \uc2  "But the bank could prove to be too late in its action as the yen is prone to resume rises after the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's decisions.\par \par \uc2  "The BOJ is likely to ease further sometime later this year. It could try to affect two-year bond yields, which are said to affect the dollar-yen rate, by lowering the interest on banks' reserve deposits at the BOJ or the interest on its fixed-rate market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."    \par \par \uc2  JUNKO NISHIOKA, CHIEF ECONOMIST, RBS SECURITIES\par \par \uc2  "As the BOJ clearly sees the economy picking up steadily, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't see a need to take immediate action when the economy is on a V-shaped recovery from the damage caused by the disaster.\par \par \uc2  "The yen has risen slightly after weakening following the SNB's (\uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 ) move yesterday. Some in the market may have speculated that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if safe-haven inflows drive the yen higher.\par \par \uc2  "If the yen sharply rises, there is a possibility that the BOJ will implement easing steps but as long as the yen is trading around 77 yen against the dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to do so immediately."\par \par \uc2  MARKET REACTION:\par \par \uc2  -- For yen updates click ; for prices \par \par \uc2  -- For JGB updates, click , for prices <2JGBv1>\par \par \uc2  -- For stocks click , for Nikkei average \par \par \uc2  LINKS: \par \par \uc2  -- For an English version of the BOJ's statement, go to:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.boj.or.jp/en/index.htm/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.boj.or.jp/en/index.htm/}}}\par \par \uc2  BACKGROUND:\par \par \uc2  -- The BOJ eased policy just last month by topping up its asset buying programme to assist the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at curbing sharp rises in the yen.\par \par \uc2  -- The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has signalled its readiness to act again if sharp yen rises accompanied by a tumble in Tokyo stocks severely hurt business sentiment.\par \par \uc2  -- The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  shocked markets on Tuesday by setting an exchange rate cap on the soaring franc to stave off a recession. That prompted speculation on how the BOJ would respond as the Swiss move is expected to divert inflows of safe-haven money to the yen. \par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Leika Kihara; Editing by Joseph Radford)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ (INSTANT VIEW 3)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110910e79700027\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
