\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves hit record high after  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2011\par 03:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Y4.5 trln \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushes up \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves\par \par \uc2 * Gains in U.S. Treasury and gold prices also contribute\par \par \uc2 * New finmin says will tell G7 that yen rise harms world economy\par \par \uc2 (For more stories on the Japanese economy click )\par \par \uc2 By Kaori Kaneko\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 7 (Reuters) - Japan's foreign reserves surged to an all-time high in August after it spent a record amount to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, aiming to curb a sharp yen rise that could derail the economy's recovery from the March earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 Newly appointed finance minister Jun Azumi said on Monday that he would tell attendees at his first Group of Seven (G7) financial leaders meeting later this week that the yen's strength was bad both for Japan and for the world economy.\par \par \uc2 Azumi also said he was ready to step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to counter speculative moves, although \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely struggle to gain G7 support for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Growing fears of a tip back into global recession are piling pressure on G7 finance chiefs meeting in France on Friday to moderate the austerity drives in some rich economies and to unleash a new round of monetary stimulus.\par \par \uc2 "There is no change in Japan's stance to take action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if needed. And Japan will probably try to get understanding from G7 members that the yen's rise has been not only rapid but also driven by speculative moves since the disaster, as the authorities have been saying," said Michiyoshi Kato, senior vice president for \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Although European nations are unlikely to go along with joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they may keep quiet if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducts solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves rose to a record $1.22 trillion at the end of August compared with $1.15 trillion at the end of July, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities spent a record 4.5129 trillion yen ($58.5 billion) on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August, previous finance ministry data showed, reflecting a move into the market on Aug. 4. That far exceeded the 2.125 trillion yen it sold on Sept. 15 of last year, the previous daily record.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Aug. 4 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave the dollar a brief boost above 80 yen but it quickly lost ground and hit a record low of 75.941 yen two weeks later.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was trading around 77.50 yen on Wednesday after the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  shocked markets the previous day by setting an exchange rate cap on the soaring franc.\par \par \uc2 The bold Swiss move may force Tokyo's hand and prompt \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen if it diverts more safe-haven flows into the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In March, Japan spent 692.5 billion yen when the Group of Seven (G7) countries conducted its first joint \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in more than a decade, after the yen shot up against the dollar in the wake of the March 11 natural disaster.\par \par \uc2 The rise in Japan's foreign reserves also reflected declines in U.S. Treasury yields that pushed up Treasury prices, with the benchmark 10-year yield falling to 2.225 percent at the end of August from 2.798 percent a month earlier.\par \par \uc2 Gains in gold prices also inflated the value of the government's holdings, the ministry said. Gold was at $1,813.50 per ounce at the end of August, up from $1,628.50 at the end of July.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 77.115 Japanese Yen)\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Edmund Klamann)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY-RESERVES/UPDATE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110907e7970001f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
