\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Profiles of Bank of Japan policy board members\b0\par\par\uc2 1,125 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2011\par 00:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 7 (Reuters) - Following are profiles of the Bank of Japan's policy board members:\par \par \uc2 MASAAKI SHIRAKAWA, 61, governor\par \par \uc2 A career central banker who took the helm in 2008, Shirakawa has led the implementation of unconventional steps including corporate debt buying during the global financial crisis, and guided the BOJ out of the programme as the recession ended.\par \par \uc2 He led the board in reaching surprise decisions last year to set up an asset buying programme to pump more money into an economy long mired in deflation and to peg rates virtually at zero until price stability is in sight.\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa's recent stance has been to act pre-emptively against risks, especially against market volatility that could hurt business and household sentiment. One such case was in March, when the BOJ boosted asset purchases just after the earthquake. Another was in August, when it again topped up its asset buying programme in the wake of sharp yen rises.\par \par \uc2 While Shirakawa has expressed readiness to ease policy further if Japan's recovery prospects are threatened, he has warned of the drawbacks of keeping monetary conditions ultra-loose for too long. He feels that central banks can and should act to prevent bubbles instead of responding after they burst.\par \par \uc2 HIROHIDE YAMAGUCHI, 60, deputy governor\par \par \uc2 A central banker for more than 30 years, Yamaguchi was a BOJ executive director when he was appointed to his current post in October 2008.\par \par \uc2 Yamaguchi, whose views are thought to be close to those of Shirakawa, has mostly toed the BOJ's official line on monetary policy. He is regarded as mainly in charge of communicating the BOJ's view to the government and ruling party lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 Like Shirakawa, Yamaguchi has signalled that yen moves would be key in determining whether the economy needs more support from monetary stimulus.\par \par \uc2 KIYOHIKO NISHIMURA, 58, deputy governor\par \par \uc2 A former professor at the University of Tokyo and a statistics expert, Nishimura joined the board in 2005 and was appointed a deputy governor in March 2008.\par \par \uc2 He has been among the board members more pessimistic about Japan's economic outlook and surprised markets by proposing unsuccessfully in April to boost the BOJ's asset purchases. He did not repeat the proposal in subsequent meetings and has voted with the majority.\par \par \uc2 HIDETOSHI KAMEZAKI, 68\par \par \uc2 Kamezaki, a former senior executive vice president of Japan's biggest trading firm, \uc2 Mitsubishi Corp\uc2  , joined the board in April 2007. He had broad overseas experience at the trading house.\par \par \uc2 Kamezaki said on July 27 the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to be proactive in taking appropriate measures, warning that a default or downgrade of U.S. sovereign debt would severely hurt Japan's financial system.\par \par \uc2 Although Kamezaki at that time said he did not see an immediate need for additional easing steps, the BOJ unanimously decided to add another 5 trillion yen to its asset purchase fund at the Aug. 4 meeting following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In October 2008 he was among four dissenters when the BOJ cut rates to 0.3 percent, calling instead for a cut to 0.25 percent. He has voted with the board and toed the BOJ's official line since then.\par \par \uc2 SEIJI NAKAMURA, 69\par \par \uc2 Nakamura, who joined the board in April 2007, formerly headed a unit of Japanese freight firm \uc2 Mitsui O.S.K\uc2 . Lines and worked mostly in its finance and planning sections.\par \par \uc2 Nakamura was upbeat about the economic outlook in June, when he said global growth would help Japan's recovery accelerate later this year, unfazed by signs of a slowdown in global factory growth.\par \par \uc2 He was cautious about expanding the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's loan scheme targeting growth industries, though he did not dissent when the BOJ decided two weeks later to add a 500 billion yen credit line to the scheme to help banks that extend asset-based lending.\par \par \uc2 He dissented when the BOJ cut rates to 0.3 percent in October 2008, favouring instead a cut to 0.25 percent. He has followed the BOJ's official line since then.\par \par \uc2 RYUZO MIYAO, 47\par \par \uc2 An academic and expert on monetary policy, Miyao joined the board in March last year. He is known for his research on inflation targeting and some analysts say he doubts the effectiveness of such a policy.\par \par \uc2 Miyao is regarded by markets as among board members who are more pessimistic about Japan's recovery prospects. He has voted with the majority since joining the board.\par \par \uc2 Following the March 11 earthquake and tsunami, Miyao said on March 23 that the BOJ would take appropriate policy action if necessary, but offered few clues on what specific action was being considered. He will next speak in public on Sept. 14.\par \par \uc2 YOSHIHISA MORIMOTO, 67\par \par \uc2 Morimoto, a former executive at \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power Co\uc2  , Japan's biggest power utility and now at the centre of the quake-triggered nuclear crisis, joined the board on July 1 last year. He has voted with the majority.\par \par \uc2 Morimoto said in June that global economic risks have heightened somewhat after a slew of weak U.S. data but not enough to affect the outlook for Japan's economy.\par \par \uc2 He also warned that a prolonged suspension of nuclear power plants across Japan would seriously hurt the economy.\par \par \uc2 SAYURI SHIRAI, 48\par \par \uc2 Shirai, a former IMF economist, joined the board on April 1 as its only female member, taking a seat vacated by Miyako Suda, whose term expired in March.\par \par \uc2 In her first media briefing Shirai ruled out the possibility of the BOJ underwriting government bonds to help finance reconstruction after the quake.\par \par \uc2 Shirai, previously a professor of economics and policy at Tokyo's Keio University, is seen as among the more pessimistic board members about Japan's economic outlook, saying that it is very uncertain. She also expressed concern last month over the yen's excessive rise.\par \par \uc2 KOJI ISHIDA, 64\par \par \uc2 Ishida, a veteran banker and a former leasing company head, joined the board on June 30, succeeding Tadao Noda, also a former bank executive.\par \par \uc2 In his first news conference Ishida voiced caution over growing uncertainty surrounding overseas economies, suggesting he will side with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ultra-easy monetary bias to support Japan's fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 He has ruled out the BOJ's direct underwriting of government debts arguing in parliament that such a move would give the impression of lax fiscal discipline and cause rises in bond yields.\par \par \uc2 Little is known of Ishida's views on monetary policy, although analysts do not expect him to rock the boat in debates at rate-setting meetings, at least in the near term.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Rie Ishiguro and Leika Kihara; Editing by Chris Gallagher)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ-PROFILES (FACTBOX)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110906e7960014x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
