\par\par \b \uc2 GBP/JPY's Link To Equity Under Threat -RBS\b0\par\par\uc2 404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2011\par 08:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Historically, yen cross rates and equities have been highly correlated\par \par \uc2 -- But this may be changing as 'Japanese style' low interest rates 'bed in' across major economies\par \par \uc2 -- GBP/JPY's correlation with equity has deteriorated markedly\par \par \uc2 By David Cottle\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The correlation between 'yen crosses' in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and the fortunes of global equity could be under threat.\par \par \uc2 Historically, the tendency of many \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' yen rates to move with equities was largely dependent on the better interest rates, or 'carry', available to Japanese investors overseas, and the tendency of these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain in periods of equity strength.\par \par \uc2 However, now, with low 'Japanese style' interest rates prevailing across the major economies, there is less reason for their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be positively correlated with risky assets like equity. Analysts at the \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  put particular emphasis on the breakdown in the correlation between GBP/JPY and stocks.\par \par \uc2 "We are starting to see the correlation of GBP/JPY with equities (in this case the S&P 500) break down... with the correlation in August at just 0.2% against 0.7% in June and July."\par \par \uc2 On the one hand, RBS blames the lack of carry available in sterling, and the negative 'real carry' in GBP/JPY because of the relatively high level of U.K. inflation.\par \par \uc2 However, some of this breakdown in correlation is also related to Japanese resistance to yen strength, the bank said. The correlation of GBP/JPY and other yen crosses with equities in August would probably have been greater had the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent yen gains on August 4.\par \par \uc2 This combination of low rates in the developed economies and Tokyo on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  patrol, seems likely to endure for some time to come, so the yen crosses' equity link seems set for a winter break, at least.\par \par \uc2 (David Cottle is a Special Writer for Dow Jones Newswires... contributing to the Wall Street Journal. He has reported on corporate credit, the U.K. economy and the sovereign debt markets for Dow Jones Newswires. He has also edited, and written for, the company's Market Talk product in London and Singapore. He can be reached at +44 20 7842 9436 or david.cottle@dowjones.com) [ 06-09-11 0649GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110906e796000ei\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
