\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: As Swiss Act on Franc, Investors Watch Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Cottle   \par \uc2 757 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2011\par 21:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's decision to try to curb the franc by setting a cap on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors worried that Tokyo will seek to further weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 Like Switzerland's, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become a haven amid financial turmoil, and Japanese exporters have been as horrified as their Swiss counterparts at their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise. The dollar has been crumbling against the yen since the financial crisis took hold. In the summer of 2007, a dollar bought more than Y120. Now, it is more like Y77.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 Analysts agree that the SNB's action would make it easier for Japanese authorities to justify any efforts to weaken the yen they might undertake. Indeed the yen slipped against both the dollar and the euro after the SNB said it will buy euros in "unlimited quantities" if the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls below CHF1.20. The latest figures from the Organization of Economic Cooperation and Development show the yen is 30% overvalued given its purchasing power, while the franc is 50% too strong, said David Bloom, \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy.\par \par \uc2 But for the moment, it seems that Tokyo is primarily concerned with talk, not action. On Tuesday, Jun Azumi, Japan's new finance minister, said he will try hard to convince his counterparts from the Group of Seven leading industrialized countries about the danger posed by the strong yen. The ministers meet Friday in Marseille, France.\par \par \uc2 "I will clearly tell them that we have strong concerns over excessive rises in the yen and also have great interest in any speculative movement [in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market], and I will work hard so they will share a common view," he said at a regular news conference.\par \par \uc2 Analysts believe there is virtually no chance that the Japanese will try to draw a Swiss-style line in the sand under the yen exchange rate with any particular \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Japan's dynamics are different to Switzerland's. It already has vast foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," said Marc Ostwald, a strategist at Monument Securities in London, suggesting this should limit Japan's appetite to accumulate still more by selling the yen.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities have said its previous episodes of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were efforts to smooth out what they call disorderly market adjustments. Partly because it has always sought to head off accusations of outright \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation, it has avoided setting an outright target that it would have to defend. Tokyo also has a healthy respect for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 's limits.\par \par \uc2 Events in August were just the latest reminder. Japan sold Y4.51 trillion ($58.65 billion) in August, a statement from the Finance Ministry showed last week, the largest monthly amount since March 2004. Despite this, the yen posted a meager 0.1% gain versus the greenback last month. The dollar reached a postwar record low of Y75.94 on Aug. 19.\par \par \uc2 Other attempts have proven \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be expensive and only temporarily successful. In 2003, the Bank of Japan sold the yen when the dollar approached Y115, in an apparent effort to keep the dollar above that level. That September, despite the reported selling of Y13 trillion by Japanese authorities, the dollar slipped below that mark as investors worried over the U.S. invasion of Iraq.\par \par \uc2 On balance, the Bank of Japan seems likely to continue efforts to smooth its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's path, rather than try to block the road as the SNB has done.\par \par \uc2 What \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have achieved, though, is to weaken the case for the yen as a haven for investors. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's August \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  effectively shut the door against haven flows into the yen, channeling more money into the franc, helping to force the SNB's hand, said \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's Mr. Bloom.\par \par \uc2 With its new cap on the franc, the SNB has put itself alongside Japan, leaving just gold, the Norwegian krone and the Singapore dollar as the last real haven assets, he said.\par \par \uc2 Gold seems to be the only such asset not in the care of a concerned \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "What would you buy if the Swissie and the yen are no longer your options as safe-haven assets due to constant \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?" said Andrey Kryuchenkov, an analyst at VTB Capital.\par \par \uc2 -By David Cottle, Dow Jones Newswires; david.cottle@dowjones.com [ 09-06-11 1554ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110906e796000nd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
