\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK - Majors\b0\par\par\uc2 1,926 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2011\par 01:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Following are expected trading ranges and outlooks for nine major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs today:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range   Larger Range \par USD/JPY    76.69-76.99      76.53-77.25 \par EUR/USD   1.4055-1.4122    1.4014-1.4173 \par AUD/USD   1.0508-1.0582    1.0414-1.0606 \par NZD/USD   0.8287-0.8449    0.8250-0.8544 \par GBP/USD   1.6060-1.6160    1.6003-1.6186 \par USD/CHF   0.7822-0.7915    0.7711-0.7961 \par USD/CAD   0.9850-0.9937    0.9753-0.9968 \par EUR/JPY   108.01-108.66    106.50-109.00 \par EUR/GBP   0.8724-0.8769    0.8694-0.8794 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent high and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis - Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to consolidate. Undermined by capital flight to safe-haven JPY amid elevated risk aversion, sharp European stock losses yesterday (German DAX-30 plunged 5.3%, French CAC-40 fell 4.7%, UK FTSE-100 dropped 3.6%) amid fears of global recession, worries over euro-zone unresolved sovereign-debt crisis and lawsuits filed against major banks by top U.S. \uc2 Federal Housing Finance Agency\uc2 . USD/JPY also undermined by Japan exporter sales, ultra-loose U.S. monetary policy, expectations of more accommodative measures from U.S. Federal Reserve. But USD/JPY downside limited by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importer demand, expectations of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Data focus: 1400 GMT U.S. August ISM non-manufacturing PMI, August employment trends index. USD/JPY daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics neutral; 5- & 15-day moving averages meandering sideways. Resistance at 76.99 (yesterday's high), then at 77.25 (Thursday's high); breach would tilt near-term outlook toward positive, targeting 77.51 (Aug. 26 high), then 77.70 (Aug. 25 high), 77.86 (Aug. 9 high), 78.33 (55-day moving average), 78.48 (Aug. 8 high) and 79.42 (Aug. 5 high). Support at 76.69 (yesterday's low), then at 76.53 (Friday's low) and 76.42 (Wednesday's low); breach would be near-term bearish, exposing downside to 75.94 (Aug. 19 record low), then psychological 75.00 and 74.00 levels, then 73.96 (projected base of descending channel formed with April 2009 high of 101.45 and Nov. 2009 low of 84.81 on weekly chart).\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by prospects of euro-zone economic recession as data showed region's final composite PMI fell to 50.7 in August from 51.1 in July; concerns that German Chancellor Merkel's Christian Democratic Union's defeat Sunday in state elections in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern may further hinder efforts to implement changes to European Financial Stability Facility, worries ahead of Germany's constitutional court ruling Wednesday on legality of Greece's latest rescue package; increased fears over Greek default - Friday is deadline for 90% "voluntary" participation target of private sector to roll over their holdings of Greek sovereign bonds in latest Greece bail-out; worries about Italy's finances as concerns grow over Italian government's commitment to austerity and reform; German Chancellor Merkel telling parliamentary colleagues that situation in Greece and Italy is "extremely fragile"; ECB's Draghi saying solvency of states can no longer be a foregone conclusion; comment from \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Josef Ackermann\uc2  that many European banks wouldn't be able to absorb losses from sovereign bonds if the securities in their books were written down to market value. EUR/USD also weighed by increased expectations for neutral ECB monetary stance at its meeting Thursday. But EUR/USD losses tempered by sovereign demand for euro. Data focus: 0900 GMT euro-zone 2Q GDP (2nd estimate), 1000 GMT German July manufacturing orders. EUR/USD daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold; 5-day moving average below 15-day MA and falling. Support at 1.4060-1.4055 band (yesterday's low-Aug. 5 low); breach would expose downside to 1.4014 (July 18 low, matching 200-day moving average), then psychological 1.4000 line and 1.3837 (July 12 reaction low). Resistance at 1.4122 (hourly chart), then at 1.4173 (hourly chart); breach would expose upside to 1.4288 (Friday's high), then 1.4385 (Thursday's high), 1.4470 (Wednesday's high) and 1.4533 (Aug. 30 high).\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to consolidate with risks skewed lower as markets await 0430 GMT RBA interest rate announcement - as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to stay pat at 4.75%, focus on accompanying statement. AUD/USD undermined by elevated investor risk aversion, softer commodity prices; but losses tempered by AUD-USD interest rate differential, sovereign demand for Aussie. Data focus: 0130 GMT Australia 2Q balance of payments, July housing finance approvals. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics falling from overbought. Support at 1.0508 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.0414 (Aug. 26 low), then 1.0382 (Aug. 23 low), 1.0367 (200-day moving average), 1.0357 (Aug. 22 low) and 1.0311 (Aug. 19 reaction low). Resistance at 1.0582 (hourly chart), then at 1.0606 (hourly chart); breach would expose upside to 1.0733 (Friday's high), then 1.0764 (Thursday's high), 1.0785 (Aug. 3 high) and psychological 1.1000 level (just below Aug. 10 high of 1.1007).\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to trade with risks skewed lower. Undermined by increased investor risk aversion, softer commodity prices. But NZD/USD losses tempered by NZD-USD yield gap. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics falling from overbought. Support at 0.8287 (yesterday's low); breach would target 0.8250 (Aug. 25 low), then 0.8229 (100-day moving average), 0.8209 (Aug. 23 low) and 0.8151 (Aug. 22 reaction low). Resistance at 0.8449 (hourly chart); breach would expose upside to 0.8544 (Friday's high), then 0.8571 (Wednesday's high), 0.8678 (Aug. 3 high) and 0.8784 (Aug. 2 high).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by heightened investor risk aversion; drop in UK August CIPS services PMI to 51.1 from 55.4 in July for biggest monthly plunge since April 2001 and well below forecast for 54.0; rising speculation of QE2 from Bank of England. But GBP/USD losses tempered by GBP demand on soft EUR/GBP cross. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold; 5-day moving average below 15-day MA and falling. Support at 1.6060 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.6003 (July 18 reaction low, near psychological 1.6000 line), then 1.5780 (July 12 reaction low). Resistance at 1.6160 (hourty chart), then at 1.6186 (hourly chart); breach would expose upside to 1.6253-1.6257 band (Friday's high-Thursday's high), then 1.6333 (Wednesday's high), 1.6419 (Aug. 30 high) and 1.6455 (Aug. 29 high).\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to consolidate. Undermined by capital flight to safe-haven CHF amid elevated risk aversion as worries over global economic slowdown and euro-zone sovereign debt crisis fester. But USD/CHF losses tempered by expectations of \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Data focus: 0715 GMT Swiss August CPI. Daily chart mixed as MACD bullish, but stochastics in bearish mode; 5-day moving average staging bearish crossover against 15-day MA; inside-day-range pattern completed yesterday. Support at 0.7822 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.7711 (Friday's low), then 0.7654 (50% Fibonacci retracement of 0.7068-0.8240 Aug. 9-Aug. 29 advance), 0.7548 (Aug. 12 low), 0.7516 (61.8% retracement) and 0.7345 (76.4% retracement). Resistance at 0.7915 (yesterday's high), then at 0.7961 (Friday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.8089 (Thursday's high), then 0.8210 (Wednesday's high), 0.8240 (Aug. 29 reaction high) and 0.8279 (July 19 high).\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to trade with risks skewed higher. Underpinned by inflamed investor risk aversion, softer commodity & oil prices. But USD/CAD gains tempered by USD-CAD interest rate differential. Data focus: 1215 GMT Canada August international reserves. USD/CAD daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics in bullish mode; bullish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 0.9871 yesterday. Resistance at 0.9933-0.9937 band (yesterday's high-Aug. 18 high); breach would expose upside to 0.9968 (Aug. 11 high), then 1.0009 (Aug. 9 high), 1.0057 (Jan. 31 reaction high), 1.0110 (Dec. 27 high) and 1.0208 (Dec. 20 reaction high). Support at 0.9850 (hourly chart); breach would expose downside to 0.9753 (Friday's low), then 0.9733 (Thursday's low), 0.9721 (Wednesday's low), 0.9712 (55-day moving average) and 0.9697 (100-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 EUR/JPY - to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by increased investor risk aversion, worries over euro-zone economic slowdown and sovereign debt crisis; Japan exporter sales. But EUR/JPY losses tempered expectations of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importer demand. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold; 5-day moving average below 15-day MA and falling. Support at 108.01 (Aug. 11 low); breach would expose downside to 106.50 (March 17 low) then 105.80 (Sept. 8, 2010 low) and 105.44 (Aug. 24, 2010 low); after which there is no significant support except for psychological round numbered levels until 99.85 (June 1, 2001 low). Resistance at 108.66 (hourly chart), then at 109.00 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 109.87 (Friday's high), then 111.00 (Thursday's high), 111.78 (Aug. 30 high) and 111.94 (Aug. 29 high).\par \par \uc2 EUR/GBP - to trade with risks skewed lower. Undermined by worries over euro-zone economic slowdown and sovereign debt crisis. Daily chart negative-biased as stochastics falling from overbought; MACD staging bearish crossover against its exponential moving average. Support at 0.8724 (Aug. 24 low); breach would target 0.8699-0.8694 band (Aug. 23 low-Aug. 22 low), then 0.8686 (200-day moving average), 0.8652 (Aug. 19 reaction low) and 0.8640 (Aug. 5 reaction low). Resistance at 0.8769 (hourly chart), then at 0.8794 (yesterday's high); breach would target 0.8822 (Friday's high), then 0.8857 (Thursday's high), 0.8884 (Wednesday's high) and 0.9000 (July 8 high).\par \par \uc2 -By Jerry Tan, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415-4046; Jerry.tan@dowjones.com [ 05-09-11 2330GMT ]\par \par \uc2 This is a financial news and information service. It is provided in general terms and does not take account of or address any individual user's position. To the extent that this article includes suggestions as to various possible investment strategies which users might consider, it does so in only general terms without reference to the personal factors which should determine any user's investment decisions. Nothing contained in this service constitutes personalized investment advice. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors shall not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article. This article does not constitute or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security.\par \par \uc2 [ 05-09-11 2330GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1075 4033   \par \par \uc2 