\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks End Lower After Dismal Jobs Report\b0\par\par\uc2 3,424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 2011\par 00:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. markets are closed Monday for Labor Day.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      11240.26 -253.31  -2.20% \par Nasdaq     2480.33  -65.71  -2.58% \par S&P 500    1173.97  -30.45  -2.53% \par FTSE 100   5292.03 -126.62  -2.34% \par Xetra DAX  5538.33 -192.30  -3.36% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  99 27/32   -1/32 0.208%  +2.0BP \par 5Y Tsy 100 19/32   +5/32 0.878%  -3.1BP \par 10Y Tsy 101 2/32 +1 7/32 2.007% -13.7BP \par  \par USD/JPY    76.86-90  -0.50% \par Range      76.88-76.82 \par EUR/USD    1.4157-60  -0.36% \par Range      1.4205-1.4157 \par AUD/USD    1.0604-10  -0.36% \par Range      1.0653-1.0606 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol  9.80/10.10% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol 12.85/13.35% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol 13.80/14.40% \par  \par Comex Gold        $1,876.90   +$47.80 \par Comex Gold Range  $1,826.90-$1,884.60 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $86.45    -$2.48 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 Investors dumped stocks and sent Treasury yields to record lows Friday as a dismal jobs report renewed fears of a recession.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average tumbled 253.31 points, or 2.2%, to 11240.26, falling by triple digits for a second-straight day. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index lost 30.45 points, or 2.53%, to 1173.97, while the Nasdaq Composite slid 65.71 points, or 2.58%, to 2480.33.\par \par \uc2 Financial stocks led the broad retreat, with \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  plunging 8.3% to lead the Dow decliners after The Wall Street Journal reported that U.S. regulators asked the Dow component to show what measures it could take if conditions for the bank worsened. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  is now down 46% this year.\par \par \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  and \uc2 Goldman Sachs Group\uc2  declined 4.6% each after reports that the U.S. \uc2 Federal Housing Finance Agency\uc2 , which oversees \uc2 Fannie Mae\uc2  and \uc2 Freddie Mac\uc2 , is preparing to sue some of the nation's largest banks over soured mortgage bonds in a bid to recoup billions of dollars in losses from the failed investments.\par \par \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  also suffered, falling 5.2% to bring its slide this year to 42%.\par \par \uc2 All 10 sectors of the S&P 500 and all 30 of the Dow components finished in negative territory, with all but seven of the S&P 500 stocks falling. The losses put the Dow and the S&P 500 into negative territory for the week, following a four-day winning streak.\par \par \uc2 At the center of investors' worries on Friday was the struggling U.S. economy, which failed to add jobs for the first time in almost a year as the unemployment rate remained stuck at 9.1%. The dismal August nonfarm payrolls report was the worst result since September 2010, and fell well short of expectations for 80,000 jobs as the government continued to shed jobs.\par \par \uc2 "The jobs number adds weight to the notion that we've been in a recession for four to five months," said Jerry Harris, president and chief investment officer at Sterne Agee Asset Management. "I just can't see financial institutions as an opportunity--I just don't think they're going to have the earnings power for a long time."\par \par \uc2 However, Harris argued that companies, with their strong balance sheets, are "fully braced" for the downturn and could continue to show strength.\par \par \uc2 Kent Engelke, chief economic strategist at Capitol Securities Management, said investors were facing a trio of gloomy scenarios: a discouraging domestic jobs situation, continued uncertainty in Europe and the specter of more regulatory pain in the financial sector.\par \par \uc2 "These are three major negatives that increase uncertainty ahead of a three-day weekend, and it comes after a nice rally--no one wants to take a position going into a long weekend," he said.\par \par \uc2 With the sharp declines, investor attention turned toward the Federal Reserve as well as President Barack Obama, who is set to deliver a new jobs plan Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "We're really seeing fear start to hit the marketplace," said Benjy Schwartz, chief market strategist at retail brokerage Lightspeed Financial. Schwartz said that any hopes in either the Fed or the federal government helping the economy were probably too optimistic.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended lower Friday on worries the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is set to introduce fresh reserve requirement rules from Monday, a move that investors fretted will lead to a liquidity squeeze.\par \par \uc2 Gold State Securities analyst Wu Yu said: "The markets are still subject to a liquidity crunch as we expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue with stringent lending curbs due to inflation concerns, while the reported new rules may also exert pressure on liquidity conditions in months ahead."\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they expect the Shanghai index to have a downward bias in the week, with immediate support at 2500 as investors will tread carefully ahead of the August consumer price index data release.\par \par \uc2 "The market may continue to be rangebound for one more week before it finds clear direction," said Capital Securities analyst Jacky Zhang. He added Beijing is unlikely to ease its tightening efforts for now as a fall in inflation in the coming months may be very limited. China is slated to release its August CPI data Sept. 9.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares ended lower Friday, snapping a four-session winning streak, following declines in Wall Street overnight ahead of the U.S. employment report for August due later in the day.\par \par \uc2 Ben Kwong, chief operating officer at \uc2 KGI Asia\uc2 , said the HSI rebounded to near main resistance at 21,000 Thursday, but then retreated, "showing investors are inclined to take profits and stay on the sidelines at that level."\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks fell Friday ahead of the release of U.S. jobs data, with \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  and major auto shares joining heavyweight chip and PC-related stocks such as Ibiden as selling targets.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures fell 2.8% to $86.45 a barrel in U.S. trading Friday as fresh concerns over a stagnating U.S. economy trumped worries over a tropical storm disrupting oil output in the key U.S. Gulf of Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures climbed to within striking distance of the previous week's record highs in U.S. trading Friday, as a weaker-than-expected reading on the U.S. labor market spurred investors to buy the metal as a store of wealth.\par \par \uc2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange closed in negative territory Friday, although up off their session lows, as a weak U.S. jobs report weighed on sentiment and traders squared up their books ahead of a the Labor Day long weekend there.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Swiss franc surged again against the dollar and euro Friday following a devastating report that the U.S. economy created virtually no jobs in August.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar and the euro both dipped about one percent on the day against the franc, which remains the main destination for investors worried that the U.S. is leading a global economic slowdown.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was at CHF0.7885 from CHF0.7949, while the euro was at CHF1.1210, from CHF1.1343, according to EBS via CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With no jobs created in August--countering expectations for 80,000 employed--the question now is what the Federal Reserve might do to support the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market is operating under the premise that there will be further monetary easing, such as an extending the duration of asset purchases, said Aroop Chatterjee, an interest rate strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York. The Fed has been buying longer-term Treasurys.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most forms of stimulus from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would result in a weaker dollar, though traders have already priced in some new support, Chatterjee said. The Federal Open Market Committee meets in September, though it's unclear whether any new measures will come out of that gathering.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The focus is also shifting to President Obama's speech to Congress, scheduled for Thursday, on how he plans to boost the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The strategy needs to address the housing market, though current speculation of a shift in mortgage-refinance rules will likely fall short of achieving its intended goals," said Adrian Miller, fixed income strategist at Miller Tabal Roberts Securities. Authorization for the Fed to address housing assets would likely prove beneficial in slowing the decline in home prices, which in turn would slow the pace of delinquencies and foreclosures, he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the euro suffered Friday after talks between Greece and a group of visiting international inspectors were suspended. The Greek government disputed the need for more austerity measures, warning these could further worsen the country's deepening recession. However, Finance Minister Evangelos Venizelos said talks with international inspectors had not broken down, and inspectors are expected to return to Athens in 10 days.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the next loan payment from other euro-zone members contingent on Greece meeting its budget targets, the indebted country will run out of money within weeks and will be forced to stop paying state salaries and pensions. It could also default on its loans from bond holders.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The cost of insuring European sovereign debt rose to record levels, and the euro hit its lowest point against the franc since Aug. 12.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Clearly, the tensions around the sovereign debt situation of different countries remain very high," said credit strategists at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a note. "We believe the situation could get worse before it gets better," they added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Friday in New York, the euro was at $1.4205 from $1.4260 late Thursday. The dollar was at Y76.82 from Y76.91, while the euro was at Y109.12 from Y109.72. The U.K. pound was at $1.6218 from $1.6185. The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at about 74.706 from about 74.479.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Jolted by a dismal U.S. jobs report Friday, investors rushed into safe-haven Treasury bonds, sending benchmark yields briefly below 2% for the second time in less than three weeks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The benchmark 10-year Treasury yield fell to as low as 1.987%, just a tad above 1.974% hit Aug. 18, when the yield broke the 2% mark for the first time in at least 50 years. The 30-year bond's yield touched the lowest level since January 2009. [ 04-09-11 2229GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Demand also surged for government bonds in Germany and the U.K., also perceived as safe-haven assets. In Germany, the 10-year yield tumbled to a fresh record low of 1.964%, based on data from CQG, while the yield on the 10-year U.K. gilt yield fell to near an all-time trough made in August. Bond prices move inversely to their prices\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. jobs report showed no increase in nonfarm payrolls in August, the latest evidence that the world's largest economy may falter in coming months. The poor data raised speculation that the Federal Reserve may take more measures aimed at stimulating the economy when it meets to set interest-rate policy later this month.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Everything is on the table" at the Fed's next meeting, said Bob Auwaerter, head of fixed income at Vanguard. The jobs number "was a real game-changer and it shows that there's absolutely no traction in the marketplace in terms of growth," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One option gathering increasing buzz in financial markets is that the Fed could sell short-dated securities and buy long-dated Treasurys, using proceeds from maturing Treasury and mortgage-backed bonds holdings. The thinking is that this could reduce long-term borrowing costs for U.S. consumers and businesses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such hopes have sparked strong buying in long-dated Treasury bonds in recent weeks. On Friday, these maturities again outperformed short-dated notes, and the 30-year bond was the best performer.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That shrank the yield premium of the 10-year Treasury note over the two-year note to about 180 basis points, the smallest in more than two years. Know as a flattening yield curve, it was a sign of heightened fears about a recession in the U.S. and more actions from the Fed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We can very easily envision a decline in the 10-year yield to 1.75% in relatively short order with the two-year/10-year yield spread collapsing further to 150 bps," said Christopher Sullivan, who oversees $1.85 billion as chief investment officer in New York at the United Nations Federal Credit Union.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late Friday afternoon trade in New York, the benchmark 10-year Treasury note rose 1 7/32 to yield 2.007%. The 30-year bond was 3 30/32 higher to yield 3.311%. In contrast, the two-year note was 1/32 lower to yield 0.208%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Treasury market will be shut Monday for the Labor Day holiday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian investors' attention will turn to some key interest-rate decisions and a batch of Chinese data in the week ahead.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  meets Tuesday, and some analysts are now betting the RBA's next move will involve a rate cut rather than another increase.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is more likely to keep rates on hold at the coming meeting, the markets will watch the governor's statement accompanying the decision for clues on Australia's policy intentions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thursday, the Bank of Korea is slated to give its rate decision, but here the focus is more on potential tightening.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan is due to give its policy decision Wednesday and is highly unlikely to change its 0-to-0.1% rate target.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, China will release a host of economic data Friday, with the focus squarely on inflation. Many China-based economists believe three-year high of 6.5% in July marked a peak and that the August inflation reading will show some easing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds ended slightly higher Friday due to weakness in Tokyo shares, while the market didn't react much to the appointment of Japan's new finance minister.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The trend of money flowing into the bond market is unlikely to change, as there haven't appeared any signs of a brighter economic outlook, which would boost stock market sentiment," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  Global Markets Japan senior strategist Maki Shimizu.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Over the weekend, Japanese Finance Minister Jun Azumi said he plans to submit plans for a third supplementary budget as soon as mid-October.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I want to put together a plan for spending as well as (tax hikes to finance quake-reconstruction) based on discussion among the ruling party, the government and the tax commission within the month to get consent with the opposition parties," said Azumi in a Sunday morning TV show on national broadcaster NHK. "I will try to submit the plan some time next month and pass quickly."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Azumi also voiced concern over the strong yen and pledged to design specific policy measures for an extra parliamentary session in the autumn to prevent the industrial "hollowing out" of Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Concerns over the strong yen are spreading across industries as well as local regions" in Japan, Azumi said. "I was instructed (by Noda) to think about specific measures to prevent the problem of hollowing out, so I'd like (to prepare) specific countermeasures for an extraordinary parliamentary session this autumn and, if necessary, will work on legislative changes."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Later in the day, Azumi met with economic and fiscal policy minister Motohisa Furukawa, trade minister Yoshio Hachiro and reconstruction minister Tatsuo Hirano at a hotel, the Yomiuri Shimbun reported.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While their talks centered on taking "quick" steps to deal with the yen's surge to historic levels, the paper said that it was in the context of preparing a third extra budget as opposed to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended higher Friday amid improving liquidity conditions in the money market following the PBOC's move to inject a net CNY25 billion into the banking system via the routine open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , a Beijing-based analyst with a local insurer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He expects the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to inject more liquidity into the market in the coming week, when CNY176 billion worth of bills and repos will be due.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The interbank seven-day weighted average repo, a benchmark gauge of short-term funding costs, is likely to test support at 4.00% in the week ahead.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among data due Monday, Indonesia's inflation is expected to be slightly higher in August due to the Muslim festivities late in the month, but to have remained manageable, which will allow Bank Indonesia to continue keeping its policy rate at 6.75% when it meets Thursday. The median forecast of nine regional economists polled by Dow Jones is for the consumer price index to have risen 4.70% on the year in August, picking up from 4.61% in July.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's trade surplus likely narrowed in July to $2.64 billion from $3.33 billion in June, according to eight economists surveyed by Dow Jones. They estimate a faster rise in imports because of higher purchases of fuel products and consumer goods may have weighed on the surplus, while export volume likely decreased.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taiwan's August CPI likely rose 1.55% on the year against a 1.32% rise in July, according to a Dow Jones poll of 10 economists.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par ECB's Trichet Urges Italy To Speed Up Approval Of Austerity Measures \par Trichet: ECB Cannot Solve Debt Woes By Itself -Sources \par German EFSF Contribution Could Reach EUR250B - Report \par Merkel's Ruling Party Loses Support In Local Elections \par Japan Fin Min Azumi: To Submit 3rd Extra Budget No Sooner Than Mid-Oct \par Italy's Foreign Affairs Min Rules Out Stop In ECB Italian, Spanish Bond Purchases \par Greek Central Banker Urges Faster Reforms, Confirms Liquidity Fund Use \par IMF's Lagarde: Must Take Measures Against Downward Spiral-Report \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Mins Discussion Not Related To Immediate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Mkt \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - Report \par Shell, Anadarko Begin Remanning Some US Gulf Platforms \par 60.2% Of Oil, 44.3% Of US Gulf Gas Output Shut By Storm Lee \par EU Implements Oil Embargo, Fresh Sanctions Against Syria \par Bank Of China Hong Kong Unit May Sell Up To US$15B In Bonds Next 12 Months \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Mins Discussion Not Related To Immediate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Mkt \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - Report \par PBOC Deputy Gov:To Stay 'Highly Vigilant' Of Future Prices:Sina \par Sources: Chinese Group Bought Part Of CCB Stake From BofA - Report \par Support Rate For New Cabinet Of Japan PM Noda Comes To 62.8%- Kyodo Poll \par Hong Kong Regulators Delay Launch Of Huttenlocher Hedge Fund Amid Probe \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 LG Electronics\f28 \uc2 \f2 : Aim To Be Global 3D TV Market Leader By 2012 \par US Jobs Market Stalls In August \par FHFA Sues Big Banks Over Mortgage Bond Losses \par Greece, Troika Talks Suspended As Govt Resists Fresh Measures \par Judge OKs Lehman Deal To Cut Canary Wharf Claim \par Bank Of America Under Fresh Scrutiny \par Bond Supply To Slow Next Week In Euro Zone \par Italy Urged To Pin Down Austerity Package Fast \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telecom Italia\f28 \uc2 \f2  To Invest At Least EUR1B In 4G Mobile \par Europe Default Insurance Costs Rise As Debt Fears Linger \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2315  Japan        Aug Japan Services PMI \par 2330  Australia    Aug Australian PSI \par 0100  Australia    Aug \f28 \uc2 \f2 TD Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  Monthly Inflation Gauge \par 0130  Australia    2Q  Business Indicators \par 0130  Australia    Aug ANZ Job Ads \par 0200  Australia    Aug VFACTS vehicle sales \par 0230  China        Aug China Services PMI \par 0230  Hong Kong    Aug Hong Kong Whole Economy PMI \par 0400  Indonesia    Aug CPI \par 0400  Indonesia    Jul Trade Balance \par 0500  India        Aug India Services PMI \par 0750  France       Aug France Services PMI \par 0755  Germany      Aug Germany Services PMI \par 0800  EU         