\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYST VIEW 4-Japan's Okada to be new finance minister -media\b0\par\par\uc2 890 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2011\par 12:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 1 (Reuters) - The finance minister in Japan's new cabinet will be Katsuya Okada, a fiscal conservative like Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda, Japanese media reported.\par \par \uc2 Okada takes charge of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fiscal policies at a time when the world's third-biggest economy is grappling with the yen's sharp appreciation and a public debt that is twice the size of its $5 trillion economy. ************************************************************\par \par \uc2 KEY POINTS\par \par \uc2 -- The lineup of the new cabinet is expected to be announced on Friday by Yoshihiko Noda, who was voted in by parliament as prime minister this week.\par \par \uc2 -- Noda, 54, the nation's sixth leader in five years, was himself finance minister in the previous administration.\par \par \uc2 -- Noda, regarded as a fiscal hawk, was the preferred candidate of financial markets to be premier and has consistently called for Japan to face painful reforms to rein in its ballooning fiscal deficit.\par \par \uc2 -- As finance minister since June 2010, Noda has backed a government proposal to double the 5 percent sales tax by the middle of the decade to fund bulging social security costs and curb massive public debt, which is double the size of the $5 trillion economy.\par \par \uc2 COMMENTARY\par \par \uc2 JUNKO NISHIOKA, CHIEF ECONOMIST, RBS SECURITIES TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "Okada is likely to maintain Noda's fiscal reform drive including the plan to raise the sales tax.\par \par \uc2 "But the question of who will be picked for other ministerial posts and the question of whether Noda's government will be able to build good relations with the opposition are more important for fiscal consolidation than who fills the finance minister post.\par \par \uc2 "Okada's appointment also indicates there would be no change in the policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as necessary. Depending of market moves, the authorities are likely to act again."\par \par \uc2 TAKAHIRO SEKIDO, CHIEF JAPAN ECONOMIST, CREDIT AGRICOLE CIB, TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "He was a bureaucrat of the METI so he knows the bureaucrats minds and now the DPJ has to have good relationship with bureaucrats at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . We already have two stimulus packages. The third one will be decided in September. The new administration has to implement and execute the stimulus packages and that is the most significant issue for the DPJ.\par \par \uc2 TAKUJI OKUBO, CHIEF JAPAN ECONOMIST, \uc2 SOCIETE GENERALE\uc2  CORPORATE & INVESTMENT BANKING, TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "There were two leading candidates for the job, Okada and Sengoku and I think Okada is the best choice of the two and other potential candidates.\par \par \uc2 "Noda was looking for two attributes for the new minister. One is loyalty. Noda is a fiscal hawk - he needs a finance minister who's able to implement a fiscally hawkish agenda. The second quality Noda needed was the political ability to negotiate those policies within the DPJ as well as with the opposition.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy will change. I don't think Okada has a particular preference here, he's in a sense very Japanese and will likely do what's required of him to fulfill his duties well. He will be loyal to the PM and he will come up with policy consistent with Noda's."\par \par \uc2 MARI IWASHITA, CHIEF MARKET ECONOMIST AT SMBC NIKKO SECURITIES INC, TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "I expect Okada to maintain the policies pursued by his predecessor, Noda, so there shouldn't be a major shift in Japan's stance on key issues.\par \par \uc2 "He should be more reasonable, unlike Maehara or Kaieda who have expressed extreme views on policies with regards to the BOJ."\par \par \uc2 KATSUTOSHI INADOME, FIXED INCOME STRATEGIST, MITSUBISHI UFJ MORGAN STANLEY SECURITIES, TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "Okada was probably the best choice available. He fits the bill for a finance minister - he is well known, knows his financial policies and has the confidence of Noda.\par \par \uc2 "He is likely to reinforce Noda's tight fiscal policy stance and this may not have been the case if someone else had been appointed. It is difficult to tell his stance on the yen. We will have to see his views on the economy to gauge how he handles the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market."\par \par \uc2 LINKS\par \par \uc2 For more stories on Japan politics\par \par \uc2 BACKGROUND\par \par \uc2 -- Noda has said Japan will need to raise taxes temporarily to help pay for rebuilding from the devastating March 11 tsunami. But he has grown more cautious about the timing of any rises, saying economic growth and fiscal reform are both vital.\par \par \uc2 -- Noda has promised firm steps including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against excessive and rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves and wants to work closely with the Bank of Japan.\par \par \uc2 -- The new administration will have to cope with a resurgent yen that threatens exports, forge a new energy policy while ending the worst nuclear crisis since Chernobyl.\par \par \uc2 -- The Democrats swept to power two years ago promising to change how Japan is ruled. But the party quickly lost momentum, dogged by internal divisions and a hung parliament as its novice team confronted the global financial crisis. The March disaster left 20,000 dead or missing and has pushed the economy back into recession.\par \par \uc2 -- Moody's Investors Service cut Japan's sovereign credit rating on Aug. 24 citing a buildup of public debt and a lack of leadership and a long-term strategy to cope with its fiscal challenges.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tokyo newsroom; Editing by Edwina Gibbs)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-POLITICS/FINMIN (ANALYST VIEW 4)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110904e7910007y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
