\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  MOF Sold Less Than Y5 Trillion In Aug  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2011\par 12:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The Japanese government sold Y4.513 trillion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from July 28 to Aug. 29, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday, a figure suggesting it stepped into the market only on Aug. 4 during the period.\par \par \uc2 The finding is almost in line with a range between Y4.46 trillion and Y4.66 trillion that market participants expected the MOF sold in its officially announced \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Aug. 4.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday's outcome suggests \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only on Aug. 4 because the figure would have exceeded Y5 trillion if Japan had sold the yen on two days or more.\par \par \uc2 The outcome therefore discounts speculation that Japan was behind a number of sudden jumps in the greenback by more than 50 points in the days following the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Japanese officials hadn't ruled out the possibility of repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The MOF said it will release detailed data, including daily figures and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  breakdowns, in the first half of November. It will announce a specific release date in October.\par \par \uc2 The MOF pushed up the dollar to Y80.25 from Y77.10 with the Y4.513 trillion campaign, the biggest single-day \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's history. But the effects were quickly lost and the dollar touched a record-low of Y75.94 on Aug. 19 and was at Y76.52 as of 1010 GMT Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 With pessimism toward the U.S. economy growing and speculation increasing that the Federal Reserve will keep U.S. Treasury yields low for a prolonged period, investors think the dollar will likely weaken further.\par \par \uc2 Nomura Trust and Banking's senior dealer Hideki Amikura, for example, predicts the greenback will drop below Y70.0 by October.\par \par \uc2 Market participants are largely divided over whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will carry out another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this time. But Amikura said it will if the greenback falls below the psychologically important level of Y75.0 because pressure from exporters, the key sector for the nation's economic growth, will intensify.\par \par \uc2 Junya Tanase, chief strategist at \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  in Tokyo, said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is difficult since gaining a green light to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's European and U.S. counterparts is tough. Others said the record-high August \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not successful therefore the authorities may be unwilling to enter the market again.\par \par \uc2 Japan's outgoing Finance Minister and Prime Minister-elect Yoshihiko Noda hasn't been ruling out the chance of another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The government, though, has already announced some measures to cope with, rather than fight against, the strong yen, possibly suggesting a change in Japan's stance toward the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Mochizuki, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2782; takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com [ 08-31-11 0627ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020110831e78v000