\par\par \b \uc2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Daily Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 3,287 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2011\par 19:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 HEARD ON THE STREET: European Stocks Still Not Cheap Enough\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Andrew Peaple \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Bargain hunters beware. European stocks may look cheap following a 15% fall in the STOXX 600 index of leading companies in the last five weeks; price-to-earnings multiples are now not far off their November 2008 lows. But stocks could catch another bout of the blues, once lower growth expectations start to feed into earnings forecasts.\par \par \uc2 Sure, European corporate balance sheets are stronger than they were in 2008, and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has room to loosen policy. But government austerity policies in response to the sovereign debt crisis could knock one quarter of a percentage point off growth next year alone, \uc2 UBS\uc2  estimates.\par \par \uc2 Even on a relatively benign assumption that the euro zone economy grows between 0% and 1% in 2012, corporate earnings are likely to be 5% lower than current consensus forecasts, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  estimates. That would leave the STOXX 600 trading at 9.2 times forward earnings, compared with 8.8 times currently. But if Europe slips into a mild recession, contracting by up to 1% next year, then earnings could be 15.5% lower than current forecasts. On that basis, stocks would now be trading at 10.2 times 2012 earnings.\par \par \uc2 True, either scenario would leave European stocks looking cheap relative to their long-run average forward price earnings ratio since 1988 of 13.3 times. But history suggests markets are likely to be unnerved if stocks suffer persistent earnings downgrades.\par \par \uc2 European stocks still trade at 11 times their 10-year average earnings adjusted for inflation - the so-called Shiller P/E ratio. A bear market doesn't usually end until that ratio is in high single figures, implying stocks could fall a further 10% from here at least.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  expects European companies will experience an effective global economic growth rate of 2% next year, weighted for the spread of their revenues around the world. On the five occasions growth has fallen below that level since the early 1970s, corporate earnings have dropped by at least 30% from peak to trough, over an average of 22 months, with European equities falling by 39% from their peak each time.\par \par \uc2 Investors may be suffering from rational despondency.\par \par \uc2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Foxconn's Margin For Improvement\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Tom Orlik \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hon Hai Precision Industry Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (2317.TW), better known as Foxconn, is back in fashion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A week ago, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Apple Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s (AAPL) main supplier was about as sought after as a first generation iPod. But second-quarter results, which included an expansion in the operating margin from 1.7% to 2%, revived its credibility. With revenue also up 23%, the stock traded up to its 7% daily limit Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors' keen focus on operating margins is easy to understand in a high volume business like electronics manufacturing. A combination of rising input costs, fierce competition and diminishing returns to scale have seen margins at Hon Hai slide from 5.9% in 2006 to their current low levels.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The uptick in the second quarter is the first sign that might be turning around. And there could be more to come. A move into western China intended to capture benefits of lower cost labor, land and utilities is close to completion. The payback should start to show in the second half.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There are also benefits from a shift in Hon Hai's product mix. Alberto Moel, an analyst at Sanford C. Bernstein, says that Hon Hai's focus on tablets, handsets and TVs will give it exposure to the fastest growing sections of the market. Consumer electronics will account for around 40% of Hon Hai's revenue in 2011, up from 16% in 2008. PC's share shrinks from 51% to 42% over the same period, according to Mr. Moel's calculations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As important, these are the products where Hon Hai has best control over costs and the strongest chance of expanding their margin back toward 3%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consider: The cost of the processing chip, display and other inputs means Hon Hai controls just 20% of the cost of producing a personal computer. When it comes to making a handset, production of covers, connectors and other parts in house means that number goes up to 40%-50%. Faster growth in sections of the market where Hon Hai controls more of the costs should mean an opportunity to achieve a modest expansion in margins.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There are risks to the outlook. Apple, Hon Hai's biggest customer, has a new leader at the helm. A downturn in the U.S. and Europe would hit end user demand for many of their products. But none of that justifies the current low valuation. For bargain hunters, Hon Hai deserves a second look.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HEARD ON THE STREET: China's Yurun Seeks Investor Goodwill\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Duncan Mavin \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Five years of "negative goodwill" sounds like a run of seriously bad luck. For China Yurun Foods Group (1068.HK), the opposite is true.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since 2006, China Yurun Foods Group has generated about 9% of its profits from negative goodwill, an accounting quirk that allows the company to mark up the value of the pig slaughterhouses it buys. That should be a red flag for investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yurun, a Hong Kong-listed pork processor with a market capitalization of about $4.2 billion, has bought eight slaughterhouses at knock-down prices over the past five years and booked 587 million Hong Kong dollars (US$75.3 million) in gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To generate profits this way from negative goodwill several years running and across multiple transactions is highly unusual, says Prof. Gary Biddle, chairman of accounting at the University of Hong Kong.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Negative goodwill is rare because sellers don't usually part with their assets at bargain prices. The other concern is that it's the buyer who determines how much it underpaid for the assets and what they are worth now.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The company and its auditors KPMG declined to comment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yurun is one of a number of Chinese businesses under fire from short sellers targeting alleged accounting problems. The company's shares have already lost a third of their value since mid-June, and several areas in Yurun's accounts are under scrutiny from investors. Another area of concern: High levels of government subsidies, which are above industry norms, and margins that appear out-of-whack with those of its peers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 None of this is conclusive, of course. But in the midst of a run of negative sentiment, Yurun's management has work to do to generate positive investor goodwill.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Private Premium For Angie's List\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Rolfe Winkler \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For social networks trying to defend their turf, a good barrier to entry may just be a barrier to membership.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That is the idea behind Angie's List, a members-only site for business reviews that filed for an initial public offering Thursday. More popular review sites like Yelp and TripAdvisor accept reviews from anyone and post those reviews for all to see. This leaves them vulnerable to merchants potentially paying Web surfers a small fee for positive reviews or even just submitting their own.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The downside to falling for bogus restaurant or hotel reviews may just be eating a chewy filet or sleeping on dirty sheets. But the consequence of hiring a bad roofer can be more problematic. So Angie's List focuses on "high cost of failure" segments plumbers, contractors, doctors and the like where trusting reviews matters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That Angie's List charges membership fees necessarily limits the size of its audience. In June, Yelp claimed 53 million visitors, compared with Angie's reported membership of 820,000. But Angie's List has multiple revenue streams: Besides member fees, it gets advertising revenue from service providers ranked highly by users. Sales grew 29% to $59 million in 2010 and rose 40% in the first half of this year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Like Groupon, which also relies on building a large network of users to expand its business, Angie's List is splurging on marketing costs to acquire users. It lost $26 million in the first half of the year as it expanded to more U.S. cities. Yet the investments look prudent: Average revenue for the 10 markets in which the company has been operating for at least eight years was $2.5 million last year, far above the average marketing cost in those markets of $500,000.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Groupon also argues that it earns a positive return on its marketing dollars, yet the cost to acquire subscribers has increased quickly this year. For Angie's List, it has fallen. The bigger risk to Groupon may be the quality of its content. It is easy for rivals to replicate Groupon's deals; it isn't so easy for Angie's rivals to replicate its database of reviews compiled over 16 years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Angie's List shares won't come cheaply. The target valuation for the IPO hasn't been disclosed, but it is likely above the roughly $450 million level at which \f28 \uc2 \f2 T. Rowe Price\f28 \uc2 \f2  invested earlier this year. A $600 million valuation would work out to about seven times this year's revenue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, besides the promise of tidy cash flows when Angie's List reaches maturity, the value of its reviews database could also tempt an acquirer looking to make a splash in local commerce. So long as the cost of entry isn't too outlandish, this is a list investors should try to get on.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HEARD ON THE STREET: For Markets, Did Summer End In July?\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Liam Denning \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July's personal-income data, released Monday, left investors elated. Every sector of the S&P 500 posted strong gains. But the underlying story could deflate such hopes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market was clearly focused on the 0.8% gain in consumer spending, the strongest since February. In real terms, spending rose 0.5%, the first increase since March. Even allowing for seasonally high energy outlays, spending on services and durable goods was strong. It all indicates a good start to the third quarter. [ 30-08-11 1750GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The question is how sustainable July's performance will prove. It came just ahead of August's market rout. Before the weekend, the University of Michigan said its consumer-sentiment index had dived to its lowest point since the dark days of November 2008. Fully two-thirds of respondents felt the economy was deteriorating.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumers are being squeezed by persistent unemployment and rising, if still low, inflation. Disposable personal income rose 0.3% in nominal terms in July. That means the big jump in spending was financed using the old American stand-by of running down savings, with the savings rate hitting its lowest level since March, at 5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet real disposable income actually fell 0.1%, its first drop since September, as inflation accelerated. Core inflation's annualized rate rose from 1.4% in June to 1.6% in July.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This could provide the sucker punch for markets. Friday's bland speech from Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke will have kept residual hopes of further monetary easing alive. But core inflation nudging closer to 2% will provide more ammunition for restless hawks when the Fed meets next month. Barring solid gains in payrolls and vehicle-sales data over the next week, markets may well end the summer with a whimper.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Moving On From Greece?\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Richard Barley \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Will what happens in Greece, finally stay in Greece?\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A host of doubts still surround the second bailout for the country. The economy is in tatters, there is doubt over the bond swap at the heart of the deal, and a row over collateral for Finnish loans is continuing. But while Greek bond yields have surged to fresh highs, with the yield to maturity on two-year notes at 46%, the rest of the euro-zone government-bond market hasn't taken fright. That marks a step forward in the crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Finnish collateral row is another example of the euro zone's challenge in sorting out the detail for its crisis response and bringing all 17 member states together. It may yet become another deeply divisive problem, even though officials are working on a solution and Finland has said it can be flexible on the form of collateral.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the news from Greece itself has been bad. The Finance Ministry now projects the economy will shrink 4.5%-5.3% in 2011, versus a previous forecast of 3.9%. The deficit may fall only to 8%-8.5% of GDP versus a target of 7.6%. And it isn't clear that the latest bailout will stave off an ultimate default, given that it doesn't reduce debt materially.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Previously, this might have sent the market into a tailspin, with fears about Greece extrapolated to other sovereigns. But Portuguese 10-year yields are 2.5 percentage points below their recent peak, while Irish 10-year yields are more than five points lower and are below 9% for the first time since April. Ireland, in particular, has benefited from the reduction in interest rates on its bailout loans and has boosted competitiveness.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's resumption of bond purchases, albeit reluctant, has disrupted the contagion route to Spain and Italy, where 10-year yields are now locked around 5%. That support is crucial.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Governments elsewhere also haven't been idle: Spain, Italy and France are moving to introduce binding rules on deficits like Germany's debt brake, as well as fresh fiscal measures to hit debt targets. Euro-area economic governance is being tightened up. And banks have started reflecting Greek bond write-downs in their results. Some pain is finally being taken.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The crisis hasn't gone away. With euro-zone growth slowing to a crawl, markets could yet become concerned about debt sustainability in other sovereigns. Politics remains a threat. The Finnish row is a reminder of the clear tensions between national and euro-zone interests. An unexpected ratings downgrade could spark new market turmoil.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But for now, at least, Greece may have lost its capacity to shock. That can only be a good thing for the euro zone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HEARD ON THE STREET: From A Position Of Weakness Comes Yen's Strength\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     By Andrew Monahan and James Simms \par     A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hapless and a bit powerless.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That is how Japan appears as it tries to handle the soaring yen, now still close to a record high it hit recently against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo misses the point in blaming speculators for the strength of the yen, despite Japan's weak economic fundamentals. It is precisely these features that make the yen appealing in turbulent times. Sluggish imports help keep the current-account surplus high, ensuring steady demand for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Price falls Japan has been battling deflation since the 1990s extend its buying power.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 True, when global stock indexes plunge, hedge funds pile into the yen. The huge dollar market offers safety in liquidity, but the yen wins out because Japan's current-account surplus offers additional security. There are always Japanese exporters and institutional investors with foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to sell for the yen, in order to repatriate funds. Because of this, the yen tends to rise more sharply than it falls, burnishing its image as a safe harbor.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo is right that it isn't a rush into Japanese stocks or bonds that has sent the yen to record highs against the dollar recently. But the yen is winning in the least-ugly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  contest. Sure, Japan has vast government debt, a political three-ring circus and its credit rating has dropped. But it has weathered these pressures for many years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other countries have newer problems. Europe has failed to take control of its debt crisis, and the U.S. faces its own budgetary problems, tarnishing the appeal of euro and dollar assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The response to yen strength has been unconvincing. The most recent unilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Aug. 4, which Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data suggest was the largest ever in a single day at around Y4.6 trillion, gave only brief respite. Then there is the Finance Ministry's idea for $100 billion of cheap loans to fund foreign mergers and acquisitions, and hence sales of yen, by Japanese companies. But companies are flush with cash, commercial financing is dirt cheap, and the last thing Tokyo needs is to promote poorly thought-out deals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the long run, it might even be counterproductive by pushing up the already high overseas income surplus by increasing income-producing foreign assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is all enough to leave speculators feeling a little emboldened. Indeed, hedge funds and other noncommercial investors have increased bets on the yen by 12% after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , according to data from the Chicago Mercantile Exchange.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Given the forces arrayed against them, the Japanese authorities seem unlikely to stem yen strength alone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HEARD ON THE STREET: China's Collateral Damage For Copper\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Chuin-Wei Yap and Tom Orlik \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has kicked away a key support to copper prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The red metal took a pounding in the recent commodities sell off, with prices dropping from a high near $4.50/lb at the end of July to a low of $3.97/lb in the second week of August. The factor preventing them from falling further: Continued strong growth from China. The world's second largest economy accounts for about 40% of global demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But China's purchases of copper, as well as soybeans, steel and other commodities have been fueled by more than just fundamental demand. Since late 2010, local commodities traders have used imports as a way to beat the government's credit curbs. Traders use what little funds they have as collateral to obtain a letter of credit from a bank that they use to finance imports of copper. That copper is then sold or itself used to obtain yuan loans, with the trader enjoying use of the funds until the letter of credit has to be repaid a few months down the line.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is the need for credit, not the demand for copper, that drives the transaction. The funds are used for, say, developments in real estate. The metal piles up in warehouses in Shanghai, where inventory rose to record levels in the opening months of the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Now, Beijing has apparently had enough. \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  moved on Friday to require banks to place a part of the original collateral held against letters of credit in low yielding reserve accounts, instead of allowing them to use it to make more loans. That means it's going to become more expensive to get and give out the letters of credit that fund the whole escapade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The move isn't likely to crater metal markets, and copper prices were pretty much flat on Monday. That might be because Beijing - mindful of the need for an orderly transition - will implement the new policy in stages through mid-February. It also helps that the move comes just ahead of a traditionally strong season for the construction sector.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But some corrosion of demand is inevitable. Henry Liu, metals analyst at Mirae Asset Securities, estimates some 50% of copper trades and 60% of steel use such letters of credit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Copper prices, already skidding on euro-zone debt concerns and a lackluster U.S. economy, will struggle to recover their poise in the months ahead. [ 30-08-11 1750GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4257   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110830e78u0014z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
