\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: Japan's Next PM Plans Steps To Deal With Yen After Forming Cabinet\b0\par\par\uc2 569 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2011\par 06:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Japan's incoming prime minister Yoshihiko Noda plans to announce steps to weaken the yen after forming his Cabinet\par \par \uc2 -- Noda suggests the steps will include measures to help small firms with financing needs\par \par \uc2 -- Noda says Japan will continue to actively involve itself in G-7 and G-20 efforts to stabilize global markets\par \par \uc2 -- Some analysts expect more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while others doubt Noda will be able to rein in the yen\par \par \uc2 (Adds analyst comments in quotes in 7th and 10th paragraphs.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The man virtually assured of becoming Japan's next prime minister said Tuesday he would announce steps to deal with the strong yen soon after forming his Cabinet, highlighting the priority he places on defending the nation's fragile economy from the effects of the yen's rises.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "After forming a new structure, I'd like to quickly put together and announce my thinking," current finance minister and soon-to-be prime minister Yoshihiko Noda said when asked about how he would handle the strong yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda, who is expected to be formally chosen as prime minister by parliament later in the day, said measures to help small companies with financing needs could be central to fresh anti-strong yen steps.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He suggested he is considering tapping into an emergency yen fund in the national budget to fund such measures. He is also thinking of including steps to deal with the yen in a planned new package to rebuild areas damaged by the March 11 earthquake and tsunami.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese policymakers have been hard-pressed to come up with ways to protect the nation's still-weak economic recovery from yen rises, which make exports less competitive abroad.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Early this month, Noda ordered solo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to push the yen lower, even though there were indications the U.S. and European countries opposed such action. The Japanese government last week unveiled steps to indirectly influence the exchange rates as well. These included measures to encourage companies to sell yen for foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy overseas firms or natural resources.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda's rise to the top government post has made some investors brace for more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the future, with Takuji Aida, a senior economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 , saying that "further monetary easing and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are likely to underpin \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's domestic demand recovery and soften deflationary pressure."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Noda suggested Tuesday he isn't overly eager to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Since I became the finance minister, I have conducted three \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market," he said. "Those were valuable experiences, but it would be better if this kind of thing didn't happen."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After various, unsuccessful attempts by Japan's government and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials over past months to rein in the yen, market participants doubt that a change of leadership alone could reverse the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's upward trend.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Investors are assuming that there are only a limited amount of new steps that could be taken, and any further focus on helping businesses likely won't have a major near-term impact on the yen," said Yuzo Sakai, manager of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  business promotion at Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  & Ueda Harlow.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 30-08-11 0446GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110830e78u0008q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
