\par\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \b \uc2 Yuan Takes Heat Off Prospects For Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 Tania Chen  \par \uc2 DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 693 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 2011\par 22:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top News & Commentary\par \uc2 DJCOMM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Given the importance of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trading relationship with China, the appreciating yuan is taking the heat off the prospects of another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen versus the dollar.\par \par \uc2 As the yen hit new highs against the U.S. dollar this month, Japanese exporters and other manufacturers complained about a loss of competitiveness, which put pressure on their government to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But that view misses the importance of their competition and trading with China, whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has appreciated against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 As the two largest economic powerhouses in Asia, China and Japan share key export and import markets--particularly the U.S. and Europe--and do a formidable amount of trading with each other. Consequently, when one country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthens, the other country's exporters benefit as their goods become cheaper in that counterpart's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and vice versa.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy allows its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to peg the yuan within a preset range, which it can vary daily. And while it has continued to use that tool to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  undervalued, it gradually has set it on an appreciating path, and more recently at a faster rate than expected. Since June 2010, when the People's Bank of China stopped rigidly pegging the yuan to the dollar at a fixed level, the yuan has risen 6.7% against the U.S. dollar. Because the dollar's depreciation against the yen is greater than its depreciation against the yuan, China's move is not exactly a full panacea for Japan's exporters but it does take some of the edge off of their loss of competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 That is best illustrated by what happened on Aug. 19, when the dollar dropped to a new record low against the yen of Y75.94. Given that this was below the Y76.25 low that prompted the Group of Seven nations to join Japan in selling the yen after the earthquake on March 18, it put markets on edge over the prospect that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again. But when measured against their Chinese trading partners, the country's exporters' competitiveness had not fallen at all. Over the same period, the yen had fallen by 2% against the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Notably, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  did \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own on Aug. 4, but it did so alone and was effectively rebuked by other G7 nations for doing so--a lack of support that severely limited its effectiveness. And one reason why those countries might have been reticent to support it is that Japan's terms of trade actually had improved against China.\par \par \uc2 On a trade-weighted basis, China occupies a bigger position in Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's basket than do either the U.S. or Europe, said Greg Anderson, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 . As such, the impact on Japanese exporters from yen strength is countered by China's stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With both the yuan and yen appreciating against the dollar, any moves of the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus Japan's are going to be more "muted" than when compared to the dollar, said Nick Chamie, the global head of emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  in Toronto. "They don't have nearly as much to complain about as the dollar," he said of Japan's exporters.\par \par \uc2 But Japanese exporters habitually focus on the dollar, not the yuan, and in that way they are like their peers in most economies. As such, many analysts believe the dollar-yen cross rate will continue to be the sole determinant of what policy the authorities choose--regardless of what happens to the yuan.\par \par \uc2 A reason for that narrow focus is that there is no way to trade the yuan itself, so it escapes both exporters' and government officials' attention.\par \par \uc2 "Apples and Oranges. The Japanese yen is just not comparable to the Chinese yuan, which is highly regulated and not convertible," said Michael Woolfolk, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York Mellon.\par \par \uc2 -By Tania Chen, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2814; tania.chen@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCOMM1120110829e78t001l4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
