\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-INTERVIEW-Ex-BOJ Muto calls for QE expansion to weaken yen\b0\par\par\uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 2011\par 02:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats item that first ran late on Friday)\par \par \uc2 * Ex-BOJ deputy governor: BOJ has room to buy more assets\par \par \uc2 * BOJ can buy JGBs with longer durations, risk assets\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot sustainably weaken yen\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 26 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan should consider boosting asset purchases further to weaken the yen as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has only a temporary effect in stemming yen rises, former BOJ Deputy Governor Toshiro Muto said.\par \par \uc2 Muto, who was a top bureaucrat at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  before joining the BOJ, also urged the government to maintain fiscal reform efforts regardless of who succeeds outgoing premier Naoto Kan, warning that delaying tax hikes for too long could trigger a severe market backlash.\par \par \uc2 "If the government weakens efforts to restore fiscal discipline, Japan may face a market attack through a sharp bond sell-off. While that may not happen immediately, the risk is quite high," Muto, now chairman of private think-tank \uc2 Daiwa Institute of Research\uc2 , told Reuters in an interview on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Muto, who still has influence on and contact with key policymakers, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this month was justified as a measure to stem an overshooting of the yen, and likely had informal consent from the United States and Europe.\par \par \uc2 But given that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone cannot sustainably weaken the yen, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should buy more government debt to help stem yen gains by narrowing interest rate differentials between Japan and the United States, said Muto, who served as deputy BOJ governor for five years until 2008.\par \par \uc2 "Japan is in a very tough situation with (short-term) interest rates stuck at zero. The option left is quantitative easing," Muto said.\par \par \uc2 "Giving the impression that nothing more can be done by the BOJ would be a disappointment to markets."\par \par \uc2 The BOJ has room to further expand the size of its 15 trillion yen ($194 billion) asset buying programme, and to buy more risk assets and government bonds with longer durations, he said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should even not hesitate to boost its buying of long-term government bonds from the current 21.6 trillion yen per year, but should hold off on directly underwriting debt and stick to purchasing bonds from the market, he said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and eased monetary policy by boosting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's asset buying programme on Aug. 4, but the measures have had only a limited effect in stemming yen rises that hurt the country's export-reliant economy.\par \par \uc2 Under the asset buying scheme, the BOJ buys Japanese government bonds (JGBs) with up to two years until maturity as well as private debt such as corporate bonds and trust funds investing in shares and real estate.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts have called on the BOJ to buy under its asset buying scheme JGBs with longer periods until maturity, or boost outright purchases of long-term JGBs from the market.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ is ready to boost asset purchases if a renewed yen spike triggers a stock market sell-off severe enough to damage business sentiment, but doubts whether it can influence the long end of the yield curve with its bond purchases.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Kan on Friday confirmed his intention to step down at a gathering of ruling party lawmakers, clearing the way for them to vote next week on a new leader who would become prime minister due to its majority in parliament's lower house.\par \par \uc2 The new premier will face huge challenges, including a strong yen seen as a threat to the economy, rebuilding from the devastation of the March earthquake and curbing huge public debt while funding the bulging social welfare costs of an ageing society. ($1 = 77.450 Japanese Yen)\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Yoshifumi Takemoto; Editing by Michael Watson)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ (INTERVIEW) (REPEAT)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110901e78t00002\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
