\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Notch Third Straight Gain; Focus On Fed\b0\par\par\uc2 2,665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2011\par 00:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      11320.71 +143.95  +1.29% \par Nasdaq     2467.69  +21.63  +0.88% \par S&P 500    1177.60  +15.25  +1.31% \par FTSE 100   5205.85  +76.43  +1.49% \par Xetra DAX  5681.08 +148.70  +2.69% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  99 25/32    -0/32 0.231%  +0.7BP \par 5Y Tsy 102 10/32   -13/32 1.018%  +8.1BP \par 10Y Tsy 98 16/32 -1 12/32 2.294% +15.9BP \par  \par USD/JPY    76.98-7.00  +0.42% \par Range      77.07-76.48 \par EUR/USD    1.4414-16  -0.18% \par Range      1.4480-1.4384 \par AUD/USD    1.0469-74  -0.51% \par Range      1.0530-1.0450 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol 10.80/11.20% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol 12.90/13.40% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol 15.05/16.05% \par  \par Comex Gold         $1,757.30 -$104 \par Nymex Crude (NY)      $85.16   -$0.28 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks notched a third straight day of gains Wednesday as an upbeat durable goods report set a positive tone while investors await Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke's highly anticipated speech later this week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average gained 143.95 points, or 1.29%, to 11320.71. All but one of the Dow's 30 components closed higher. The index has surged nearly 5% this week and has risen in seven of the past 10 trading days.\par \par \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  led blue-chip gains, surging 69 cents, or 11%, to $6.99. The stock had been battered in recent weeks amid worries over mortgage-litigation woes and the possibility of more capital raises. But analysts say the bank is much sounder now than it was before the financial crisis. Shares are still down 28% this month.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index gained 15.25 points, or 1.31%, to 1177.60. Financial and utility stocks registered the biggest gains in a broad rally. All 10 of the S&P 500's sectors rose. The technology-heavy Nasdaq Composite rose 21.63 points, or 0.88%, to 2467.69.\par \par \uc2 The recent gains come as investors hope Bernanke's much-awaited speech Friday will offer clues on how the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  prepares to combat the slowing economy. That optimism may be fleeting, however, as the Fed hasn't given signs that it is ready to enact additional stimulative measures.\par \par \uc2 "There will be a lot of nervousness between now and Friday," said Peter Tuz, president at Chase Investment Counsel. "I'm not expecting very much from the Fed. We're kind of trapped. We can't stimulate the economy anymore as effectively as we're used to."\par \par \uc2 Stocks got a jolt early Wednesday as demand for long-lasting manufactured goods bounced back in July, giving the fragile economy a much-needed boost.\par \par \uc2 Orders for factory goods rose 4.0% from the prior month to $201.45 billion, according to the Commerce Department. However, orders for nondefense capital goods excluding aircraft, which economists use to gauge business spending on new equipment, dropped by 1.5%.\par \par \uc2 "It's almost as if negative news is being priced in as something positive because it underscores the argument that the Fed needs to do something," said Abigail Huffman, director of research for \uc2 Russell Investments\uc2 . "People are hedging their bets. They're hoping for the best and positioning for the worst."\par \par \uc2 At the same time, investors are unclear as to what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have in store for the struggling economy. Some say Bernanke will likely use the speech to elaborate on the Fed's economic outlook rather than break new ground and announce a third round of quantitative easing, or QE3.\par \par \uc2 "If what he outlines is underwhelming, you could get a selloff," Gary Flam, portfolio manager at Los Angeles-based Bel Air Investment Advisors. "The risk is to the downside. There's more of a chance that he disappoints than provides a coherent strategy for how the Fed can manage the weak economy."\par \par \uc2 China's shares were lower Wednesday as caution reigned amid tight liquidity and investors took quick profit after Tuesday's gains.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index may continue to consolidate between 2500 and 2600 as investors await a clear signal of any move from U.S. or Chinese monetary authorities.\par \par \uc2 Hongyuan Securities analyst Tang Yonggang said the Shanghai index will continue consolidating as liquidity is tight "and it won't improve until the PBOC shows a willingness to ease its monetary policies."\par \par \uc2 Chinese government think tank researchers have been debating whether China should continue monetary tightening amid the ongoing U.S. debt crisis or whether the authorities should focus on stimulating growth and job creation in the face of a possible economic hard landing.\par \par \uc2 "There are concerns that China may hike interest rates again in September and the government will further tighten the property sector," said Guan Yewen.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares ended lower Wednesday, tracking falls in regional markets after \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Investors Services downgraded Japan's sovereign debt rating.\par \par \uc2 Analysts' opinion differ on whether the Hong Kong market has bottomed, after the HSI's 14.6% slump from an Aug. 1 high of 22,808.\par \par \uc2 "I'm much more optimistic compared to two weeks ago," said William Lo, a buy-side analyst at Ample Capital. He said the sharp selloff Monday in many second- and third-line Hong Kong stocks, with many tumbling more than 10%, was evidence that some long funds relentlessly cut their positions, and was probably "the worst during this round of market selloff."\par \par \uc2 Still, "much hinges on what (U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben) Bernanke will have to say Friday. If he doesn't offer anything exciting, we may have to re-test the 19,000 support," said Daniel So, analyst at SHK Financial.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks fell Wednesday as \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgrade of Japan's sovereign debt rating weighed on major banks such as \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group\uc2 , while an attempt by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise fell flat, hurting major exporters.\par \par \uc2 Oil futures settled lower in U.S. trading Wednesday after an abrupt late-day sell-off, as traders took profits off the table following gains earlier in the session.\par \par \uc2 Futures spent most of the day in positive territory after the government said U.S. oil stockpiles fell last week. But prices suddenly turned lower during the last 15 minutes of open outcry trading, and the selling intensified toward the close.\par \par \uc2 Carl Larry, director of energy derivatives and research at Blue Ocean Brokerage in New York, said some automatic sell orders may have been behind the pull-back, likely exacerbated by thin trading volumes.\par \par \uc2 Gold plunged below $1,800 in U.S. trade Wednesday, a dramatic reversal from the recent record-setting run. A rising stock market and sudden doubts about whether the Federal Reserve would, in fact, signal any fresh stimulus measures on Friday were the principal drivers behind Wednesday's selloff, analysts noted.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed largely flat on the London Metal Exchange Wednesday, supported by encouraging U.S. durable goods data yet still seemingly inextricably tied to the gyrations of the global stock markets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A new $100 billion fund unveiled by Japan weakened the yen Wednesday, as traders mulled the longer-term implications of efforts to curb the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's persistent strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the wake of a downgrade by Moody's Investors Service of Japan's sovereign credit rating to Aa3, the government announced new measures to discourage further gains in the yen, which just last week surged to a record high against the dollar below Y76.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition to strengthening oversight of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, the finance ministry will establish a $100 billion facility to encourage companies to exchange yen for foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -denominated assets. The new fund will use some of the country's massive stockpile of $1.15 trillion in foreign reserves.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's announcement works at cross purposes. It's a tacit acknowledgement that the world's third-largest economy is reluctant to fight investors who use the yen as a safe-haven as they flee global turmoil.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But some analysts saw the moves as a sign that direct yen-selling from the Bank of Japan won't occur anytime soon, which could embolden traders to eventually probe the yen's upside anew.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The new fund "reduces speculation that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again at these levels, and it allows companies to deal with a stronger yen rather than the BoJ weakening the yen," said John Doyle, a trader at Tempus Consulting in Washington. "Traders see the risk of them coming into the market around Y74 or Y73," if at all, he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Wednesday, the euro was at $1.4415 from $1.4441 late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y76.97 from Y76.65, while the euro was at Y110.94 from Y110.71. The dollar was at CHF0.7960 from CHF0.7925. The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 74.031 from about 73.894.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Much of the market's focus remained on Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke's upcoming speech in Jackson Hole, Wyo., on Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "For the most part the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market is trying to stay out of trouble ahead of the Bernanke appearance," said Shaun Osborne, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 TD Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Toronto.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite another Treasurys auction that saw notes sell at a record low yield, market prices tumbled Wednesday as investors increasingly doubt that Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke will commit to more quantitative easing in a speech Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The selloff--albeit in light trading--marked the third-straight session of lower Treasurys prices--a streak that hasn't been seen since the last week of June when curtains closed on the Fed's $600 billion bond-buying program. Since then, the Treasurys market has been in a steep rally.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Well-bid auctions usually spur buying momentum following the sale. But the fact that buying tapered off after Wednesday's and Tuesday's sales underscores the attention that traders are placing on the Jackson Hole, Wyo., summit at which Bernanke will speak, betting against the prospect of some form of new bond-buying initiative.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There will be no fireworks at Jackson Hole," said Ray Remy, head of fixed-income trading at Daiwa Capital Markets America, referring to Bernanke's speech at the annual central-bankers conference in Wyoming. [ 24-08-11 2212GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Troves of tepid economic data in the past few weeks had worked up hopes that the Fed would step in with some sort of disclosure at this weekend's conference. But those expectations have died down since the start of the week as new data show the global economic recovery might not have slowed down as much as some investors thought.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We felt last week that yields might have gone too far [down]," said Brett Rose, head of fixed-income strategy at Citi. "It's not that today's data was that great, but all these real indicators seem to be saying the underlying economy is reasonable, that growth is not zero." He said a lot of the recent negative data have been survey-based, reflecting bad sentiment, not bad growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wednesday morning, a much-better-than-expected jump in total U.S. durable-goods orders in July sparked an early selloff--a price drop that was needed, according to some analysts, to garner interest in Wednesday afternoon's auction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Treasury Department's issue of $35 billion in five-year notes sold at a record low yield of 1.029%. Part of the auction's success came from indirect bidders--a pool of buyers that includes foreign central banks--who scooped up 42.1% of the offering, higher than the 40.3% recent average.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But buying at the auction didn't seep into the market, and prices sank further after the results were reported. Benchmark 10-year notes lost 1 12/32 in price by late-afternoon trading, lifting yields up nearly 16 basis points to 2.294%. The 30-year bond shed 3 10/32 to yield 3.653%, while two-year notes were little changed in price to yield 0.231%. Prices and yields move inversely.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bond futures ended slightly higher Wednesday due to weakness in Tokyo shares, shrugging off a sovereign debt downgrade by Moody's Investors Service and the finance ministry's new measures against the strong yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  downgrade of Japan's sovereign debt rating was best timed for the JGB market as it gives the new ruling party leader an obligation to commit to market players that the government will keep fiscal discipline," said Makoto Noji, a senior strategist at SMBC Nikko Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, "JGBs are likely to remain top-heavy, with the yields on a steepening bias, until a 20-year auction and (the ruling Democratic Party of Japan's) leadership election, but will start to find support after these key events," said Chotaro Morita, chief strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market is now focused on a sale of Y1.1 trillion 20-year JGBs Thursday and the outcome of the DPJ's election Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended higher amid improved money market liquidity, as unsuccessful subscription funds for \f28 \uc2 \f2 GD Power Development Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s convertible bond offering began to flow back to the market, a Shenzhen-based trader with a local insurer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The trader expects the seven-day repo rate to extend losses to 4.0%-4.2% Thursday on the improvement in liquidity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Fresh Fighting Near Gadhafi's Captured Compound \par Greek Banks Seen Taking Eur5 Bln Hit From Swap Deal \par US Bolsters Accusations Against \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  Reaches $500M Settlement Over Online Pharmacy Ads \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Applied Materials\f28 \uc2 \f2  3Q Profit Surges \par France Cuts GDP Forecast, Unveils Austerity Measures \par US Durable Goods Orders Rise 4% In July \par CBO Sees $1.3T US Budget Deficit For Fiscal 2011 \par S Korea Consumers Expect Inflation To Reach 4.2% Over Next 12 Months - BOK Survey \par Tohoku Electric To Fix 70% Of Rain-Damaged Hydro Capacity By Dec -Nikkei \par Daikin Aims To Cut Inventory Turnover Period 20% -Nikkei \par Mizuho Trust Offers Plan To Help Regional Banks Boost Capital-Nikkei \par Uni-Charm To Purchase Vietnam Paper Diaper Maker -Nikkei \par Hitachi Kokusai To Buy Bra