\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Japan New  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Rules Won't Weaken JPY -Strategist\b0\par\par\uc2 206 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2011\par 12:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1000 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's imposition of new rules requiring financial firms to disclose their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings likely won't succeed in weakening the strong JPY, and sends the wrong message to investors already wary of regulation in Japan, says Naomi Fink, head of Japan strategy at Jefferies Japan Limited. "It's clear they are targeting speculators with these new moves, as they do with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but as with the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  the impact should be limited," Fink says. As \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is set to choose a new prime minister, Fink says a key task for any new government will be getting deregulation back on track. The new \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  disclosure rules are a step in the wrong direction, she says. The new rules, the first since 1998, are designed to help authorities monitor what they believe is excessive speculation in the market.\par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said the oversight will make Japan the only major country to be monitoring \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings by major financial firms. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 24-08-11 1000GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1012 1037 1085 5011 55117 75112   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110824e78o000t2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picsc