\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: JGBs May Fall; 20-Year JGB Tender Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,812 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2011\par 01:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2314 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs may fall as investors are unwilling to trade actively ahead of a 20-year JGB auction later in the day and they will likely take cues from U.S. Treasurys' weakness Wednesday, says Mizuho Securities market economist Naoki Tsuchiyama. He tips the September lead JGB futures in a 142.25-142.58 range and the benchmark 10-year yield in a 1.015%-1.040% band for today. They were last at 142.65 and 1.010% respectively. For the longer-term, JGBs remain an investor favorite for their safe-haven status as "the global economic momentum is still slowing," he says. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] Given the waning expectations the Jackson Hole, Wyo., central bankers' summit will result in the Fed announcing further quantitative-easing measures, the USD could soften, say \uc2 BNP Paribas\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategists. But they add, "with the seemingly well-informed media Fed commentators at once downplaying the likelihood of the Fed chairman dropping strong hints of additional policy action on Friday, the bar may now have been set sufficiently low in terms of expectations for risk markets not to come away from Friday's speech too disappointed." The EUR/USD at 1.4412 and the USD/JPY is at 77.00. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2306 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stocks may rise, tracking U.S. markets, which rose for a third straight day Wednesday with an upbeat U.S. durable goods report setting a positive tone as investors await Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke's highly anticipated speech Friday. In \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the yen may continue to weaken as Japan's new $100 billion fund to encourage companies to exchange yen for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated assets pressured it in U.S. trade. But some analysts said it may indicate direct yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't occur anytime soon, which could embolden traders to probe the yen's upside. The USD/JPY is at 77.02 from 76.97 late Wednesday in New York, the EUR/JPY is at 111.00 from 110.94 and the EUR/USD is at 1.4412 from 1.4415. On the data slate, there's Hong Kong external trade at 0830 GMT, Philippines external trade at 0100 GMT and the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's meeting highlights. Chinese President Hu Jintao is set to meet French President Nicolas Sarkozy in Beijing for talks on the global economy. In the eurozone, Germany's GfK consumer climate survey and French unemployment data are due. In the U.S., U.S. weekly jobless claims, the DJ-BTMU U.S. business barometer and the Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City's survey of manufacturing are due. The Jackson, Hole, Wyo., central bankers' summit is set to begin. (leslie.shaffer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2305 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's 2Q retail sales continued to rise, albeit at a slower pace than in 1Q, with the volume of seasonally adjusted real retail sales +0.9% on the quarter. When price effects are included, 2Q retail sales rose 1.7% on the quarter. Statistics New Zealand says that nationally 12 of the 15 industry sectors recorded increased sales values in the June quarter. While the continued growth in sales reinforces the pool of data that shows an improvement in the domestic picture, economists have said global uncertainty is likely to keep the Reserve Bank on the sidelines at September's monetary policy review. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2241 GMT [Dow Jones] Precious metals are mostly lower, with gold under pressure following a sharp selloff overnight as investors started to doubt whether the U.S. Federal Reserve will signal any fresh stimulus Friday. Meanwhile, U.S. exchange operator \f28 \uc2 \f2 CME Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  increased trading margin requirements on gold contracts effective Thursday, the second such hike in August in response to market volatility. Gold, considered a safer bet during economic turmoil, gained rapidly in the past few weeks on global growth uncertainty and euro-zone debt woes, but some analysts and traders had anticipated a sharp correction as they felt that the metal was already in overbought territory. Comex December gold, the most active contract, fell 5.6% to settle at $1,757.30/oz Wednesday. Spot gold is at $1,744.30/oz, down $7, silver is at $39.45/oz, down 24 cents, palladium is at $744/oz, down $1, while platinum is at $1,807/oz, up $4 from New York's informal close, according to kitco.com. (arpan.mukherjee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2219 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian milling wheat prices face mixed leads Thursday after U.S. grain futures stumbled Wednesday amid profit-taking and a lack of fresh supportive news, say traders, who took profits on long positions in wheat. MGEX September wheat fell 24.25 cents, or 2.6%, to US$9.26/bushel, CBOT September wheat lost 8.0 cents to $7.4925 and KCBT wheat dropped 6.0 cents to $8.35. But the AUD/USD is trading around 1.0468, down around 0.50 cent from midnight. On Wednesday, the most active ASX wheat futures deliverable January next year in New South Wales rose A$3.00 to A$271.50/metric ton, while those delivered in Western Australia rose A$2.00 to A$281.00. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2218 GMT [Dow Jones] Given the weakening in Germany's economy and trade links with China, it's likely the AUD and NZD will stay soft in the near term, says NAB \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  srategist Emma Lawson. "The trade connections between Asia and Europe are strong, and while Asia, including China, has managed with a weaker U.S., it may not be as staunch in the face of weaker German growth." The AUD/USD is at 1.0463. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5:49 (Dow Jones) "Businesses likely shut the door on hiring," says Nomura, disclosing just now that they're lowering their preliminary August nonfarm payrolls forecast; the data are due next week. They now expect NFP to show a decline of 5,000, from a previous estimate for a 100,000 job increase. Nomura expects private payrolls to increase by 5,000. Nomura's forecast of a loss of 10,000 manufacturing jobs remains intact. The bank's rationale: Philly Fed's lackluster reading; slowing growth in payroll tax receipts; and the impact of the Verizon strike. (gabriella.stern@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Call us at (212) 416-3100 or email patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2137 GMT [Dow Jones] US SUMMARY: A new $100B fund unveiled by Japan weakened the yen, as traders mulled the longer-term implications of efforts to curb the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's persistent strength. In the wake of a downgrade by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  of Japan's sovereign credit rating to Aa3, the government announced new measures to discourage further gains in the yen, which just last week surged to a record high against the dollar below Y76. The new fund "reduces speculation that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again at these levels, and it allows companies to deal with a stronger yen rather than the BoJ weakening the yen," said John Doyle, a trader at Tempus Consulting in Washington. "Traders see the risk of them coming into the market around Y74 or Y73," if at all, he added. The EUR/USD was at 1.4415 late Wednesday from 1.4441 late Tuesday, the USD/JPY at 76.97 vs 76.65, while the EUR/JPY was at 110.94 vs 110.71 and the USD/CHF was at 0.7960 vs 0.7925. ICE Dollar Index was at 74.031 from about 73.894. Stocks rose as an upbeat durable goods report set a positive tone while investors await Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke's speech later this week. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2  added 11% after being battered in recent weeks amid worries over mortgage litigation woes and the possibility of more capital raises. But analysts say the bank is much sounder now than it was before the financial crisis. The Dow added 1.3% while the Nasdaq added 0.9%. Despite another Treasurys auction that saw notes sell at a record low yield, market prices tumbled as investors increasingly doubt that Bernanke will commit to more quantitative easing Friday; the 5-year yields are +8.1 bps at 1.018% while 10-year yields were up 15.9 bps at 2.294%. Oil futures settled lower after an abrupt late-day sell-off, as traders took profits off the table following gains earlier in the session. October Nymex crude settled down 28 cents at $85.16/bbl. Comex December gold fell $104 to settle at $1,757.30/oz. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2130 GMT [Dow Jones]The NZX-50 is likely to continue to take its lead from offshore, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  broker Peter Sigley; But "it's another big day for reporting both here and Australia so the tone (of the results) will be important." He adds \f28 \uc2 \f2 Air NZ\f28 \uc2 \f2  (AIR.NZ) may get some boost after its FY11 results were "quite decent" relative to expectation. AIR reports net profit for the year to June 30 was NZ$81 million vs NZ$82 million a year earlier. He adds small cap Delegats (DLG.NZ) likely higher after a "very strong operating number and digging below that they have given some guidance for volumes and prices over the next 3-years which always looks strong." He adds the market will also be watching the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fisher & Paykel Appliances\f28 \uc2 \f2  (FPA.NZ) AGM later in the day to see if they give guidance on the company's 1Q activities.(lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5:30 (Dow Jones) More than half of the 82 investors who responded to the bank's survey said they expect Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke to "limit the substance of his remarks to reviewing the rationale underlying the FOMC's intent to keep the Fed funds rate near zero for two more years." Notably however, 18% think the Fed will extend the average maturity of its Treasury holding, akin to the "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Twist" program. (cynthia.lin@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Call us at (212) 416-3100 or email patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 24, 2011 19:14 ET (23:14 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110824e78o007tu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
