\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Swiss July Data Shows Exports Easing -C Suisse\b0\par\par\uc2 1,406 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2011\par 09:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0748 GMT [Dow Jones] The Swiss July trade report confirms the underlying pattern of a moderation in export demand, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "Once again, data show exports to Europe are weakening, while demand from Asia remains firm," says economist Fabian Heller. "The watch and the jewellery industries still registered high demand, while exports of textiles, paper and clothing continued to decline," he adds. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0748 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds slip as asset reallocation talk re-surfaces, with one trader noting a continuation of Monday's selling fixed income/buying equity trade, thought to be worth around EUR3.7B. Another trader adds, "looking at the price action, [asset reallocation] is a possibility. Treasurys were pretty weak in Asia so maybe we're seeing some profit taking at these levels, and we also have bund supply tomorrow." The September bund contract is 28 ticks lower at 134.90, while European equity markets are roughly 1.5% higher across the board. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0745 GMT [Dow Jones] Peripheral euro-zone spreads open unchanged to a fraction tighter as equity markets open higher and bunds drift lower. In the 10-year bucket, Italy and Spain are both 1 bp tighter at 282.6 bps, with their outright yields stuck around 4.975% as ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds their yields below the 5% level. Monday's release of the ECB bond purchase figure of EUR14.3B gave the market "reason for comfort," according to WestLB, being "neither too low to raise doubts as to the ECB's commitment, nor too high as to trigger concerns regarding the sustainability of the ECBs activities." (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0744 GMT [Dow Jones] Shares in mid-cap and junior gold stocks could "double within weeks," says Tom Winnifrith, senior fund manager for SF t1ps Smaller Companies Gold Fund, whose view is that gains are being fundamentally-driven and this is not a bubble. "The party is only just starting" for gold stocks, he says, amid collapsing faith in fiat \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the possibility of a fresh round of QE and the "unsustainable joke" that the euro has become. "Gold should push it well past $2,000 a troy ounce by Christmas...Belatedly investors are starting to see the incredible value in gold equities," Winnifrith adds. Spot gold trades at $1,897.20 a troy ounce after peaking earlier at a high of $1,912.29/oz. (andrea.hotter@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0743 GMT [Dow Jones] E.U. PMIs are seen continuing to decline, RBS says, forecasting a drop in the composite PMI to 49.8 in August from 51.1 in July. "The European economy has been slowing in recent months and we expect the composite to post a below-50 reading for the first time since July 2009, indicating the economy is flirting with contraction in 3Q," RBS says. It sees the E.U. manufacturing PMI number printing at 49.0 from 50.4 in July. The bank expects the services PMI number to post a fall to 50.5 in August, the lowest for two years, from 51.6 in July. EUR/USD currently at the day's high at 1.4410. (eva.szalay@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0742 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNY pares earlier losses on import settlements; the pair is at 6.4004 from an earlier low of 6.3950 and compared with 6.4013 late Monday. A Shanghai-based local bank trader says the pair may trade higher later and 6.4030 may be a near-term resistance. "These large-volume import settlements are typical at the end of the month," she says, adding that trade settlements, rather than PBOC-guided appreciation, are driving the spot market so far this week. The central parity was set at 6.3987 from 6.3997 Monday. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.2800/6.2840 from 6.2815/6.2845 late Monday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0741 GMT [Dow Jones] iTraxx Japan Series 15 widens to 145 bps vs Monday's 140/143 bps amid worsening risk sentiment following widening in the overseas markets, Tokyo CDS traders say. The widening momentum is pronounced particularly in high beta names although there is no fundamental news behind it, says a CDS trader at a major bank in Tokyo. The spreads widened easily "because selling interest was very thin," he says. "Volatile trading will likely persist if a lack of aggressive selling continues." Japan USD-denominated sovereign CDS is traded at 108 bps vs 107 bps on Aug 19. Other CDS deals done today include \uc2 Kobe Steel Ltd\uc2 . (5406.TO) at 127 bps vs 102 bps on Aug 8, \uc2 Nissan Motor Co. Ltd\uc2 . (7201.TO) at 102.5 bps vs 95 bps on June 24, \uc2 Promise Co. Ltd\uc2 . (8574.TO) at 440 bps and 460 bps vs 385 bps on July 4, and \uc2 Orix Corp\uc2 . (8591.TO) at 250 bps vs 160 bps on Feb 21.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0736 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds end higher after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signaled that it has no plans to hike interest rates in the near term by leaving the yield on its one-year bills unchanged at 3.5840% in the regular open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says a Shenzhen-based trader with a local brokerage. He notes that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's auction of CNY30 billion worth of six-month state-fund deposits injects liquidity into the banking system, thus boosting demand for low risk government paper. The Shanghai exchange government bond index ends at 129.30 vs 129.14 at its last close. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0735 GMT [Dow Jones] A 25 bps cut to 5.5% is possible today, even though most market participants expect the CBRT to stay on hold. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has several arguments for another rate cut," says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . Notes, manufacturing activity is slowing, credit growth is starting to lose momentum, and the outlook for inflation appears less worrying than expected. Adds the bank could take more TRY supportive measures, such as cutting the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve requirement ratios. "A rate cut today may however trigger a short-term upward move (for USD/TRY) to 1.8000." USD/TRY trades up at 1.7865, ahead of the rate decision due at 1400 GMT. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0731 GMT [Dow Jones] The KOF barometer of leading Swiss indicators is estimated to have slipped to 1.84 in August, from 2.04 in July, according to the average of forecasts from six economists compiled by DJN. Still, the KOF barometer remains high, pointing to strong Swiss economic momentum in the coming six months. The data are due Friday at 0930 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0726 GMT [Dow Jones] Citi says the yen is still pointing higher and notes that, although more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't be ruled out, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  couldn't dip in repeatedly without "stiff international criticism." Retail accounts taking profits, exporters hedging and general investor nerves on the up all point to a further decline in USD/JPY, the bank says. Spot now at 76.74. (katie.martin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0720 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is up 0.3% at 1476.84 in afternoon trade in line with most regional stock markets; trading remains volatile amid low volume as investors stay cautious in light of Wall Street's flat performance Monday. A local dealer say negative sentiment persists despite an increase in China's August \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI that is driving a rebound in Asian equities. "When it comes to macro data, investors will want to see more clarity before jumping back into the market," says a local dealer. He tips strong resistance at 1500 in the near term. Market breadth is negative at 346 losers and 289 gainers. Among gainers, MISC (3816.KU) is up 2.5% at MYR7.26, Public Bank (1295.KU) is up 0.5% at MYR12.96 and Gamuda (5398.KU) rises 0.6% to MYR3.19. Losers include newly-listed Sunway, down 11.1% at MYR2.49, and Tenaga Nasional (5347.KU) is down 0.7% at MYR5.45. (jason.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 23, 2011 03:48 ET (07:48 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110823e78n002n4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
