\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen touch softer after Japan rating downgrade\b0\par\par\uc2 496 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2011\par 01:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Moody's\uc2  cut Japan's govt debt rating to Aa3\par \par \uc2 * Yen briefly unsettled, but not expected to fall much\par \par \uc2 * Commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hold gains after risk assets rally\par \par \uc2 By Ian Chua\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Aug 24 (Reuters) - The yen came under a bit of pressure on Wednesday after \uc2 Moody's\uc2  cut its rating for Japan's government debt, but commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  held firm having gained after manufacturing data in China and Europe were less grim than feared.\par \par \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgraded Japan by a notch to Aa3, blaming large budget deficits and the build-up of debt since the 2009 global recession. Still, such cuts have had scant effect on yields as the vast bulk of Japanese debt is owned by the Japanese themselves.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Moody's\uc2  rating is now equivalent to what S&P had for Japan's government debt. So it's just a bit of a catch up. The market didn't react too much and I don't expect dollar/yen to rise that much," said Joseph Capurso, strategist at Commonwealth Bank in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the reaction was underwhelming with the dollar creeping up 10 ticks to 76.77 yen , before lapsing back to 76.66. It remains in a slim range between 75.94 and 77.20, with the downside limited by the threat of yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The euro came within a whisker of $1.4500 before losing a bit of steam to trade at $1.4435. That saw the dollar index fall to a low of 73.572, from levels above 74.000.\par \par \uc2 Commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were the star performers, gaining as riskier assets such as U.S. stocks rallied. The Australian dollar was last at $1.0518, having climbed more than a cent to around $1.0535.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, the preliminary purchasing managers indexes (PMIs) out of Europe and China, while better than feared, were anything but upbeat.\par \par \uc2 They showed economic growth stagnating in Europe and cooling slightly in China, keeping alive concerns about the risk of a global slump.\par \par \uc2 "The market is becoming more pessimistic about the economic outlook and is responding by pricing in a greater chance of QE3," said Bricklin Dwyer, economist at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The reality is that we are getting more data confirming a slowdown in manufacturing activity and a dead cat bounce in the (U.S.) housing sector," he wrote in a client note.\par \par \uc2 Some investors are pinning their hopes that Fed Chief Ben Bernanke will use his speech at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's annual symposium in Jackson Hole, Wyoming on Friday to prepare markets for more stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Last year, Bernanke used this occasion to bring up the idea of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's $600 billion bond-buying programme that became known as QE2. That pumped money and confidence into markets.\par \par \uc2 Many Fed watchers, however, think Bernanke will be more circumspect and the market could be disappointed.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Wayne Cole)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110824e78n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
