\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Possible successors to Japan PM Kan\b0\par\par\uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2011\par 06:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 Aug 23 (Reuters) - Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan's days in office are numbered after his economics minister said on Tuesday that he would resign on Aug. 30, setting the stage for Japan's sixth prime minister in five years.\par \par \uc2 Below are key facts about possible successors to Kan, who took office in June 2010.\par \par \uc2 (For more on Japanese politics, click )\par \par \uc2 YOSHIHIKO NODA\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Noda, 54, has backed Kan's push to cut huge public debt through fiscal reforms, including a proposed increase in the 5 percent sales tax.\par \par \uc2 Last September he oversaw \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in six years to stem rapid yen gains. In March, at Noda's request, the Group of Seven conducted joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive earthquake, tsunami and nuclear disaster. He ordered \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  again this month as the yen approached a record high against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Noda has distanced himself from Kan's call for a future free of nuclear energy and said in a magazine interview this month that the best option was to restart reactors that have passed thorough safety checks.\par \par \uc2 Noda recently reiterated his view that Japanese wartime leaders convicted of war crimes by an Allied tribunal after Japan's defeat in World War Two were not "war criminals" under domestic law. He also said in a magazine this month that China's rapidly growing military and its expanding area of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  posed a serious risk to surrounding nations.\par \par \uc2 A fan of combat sports, he is a keen judo practitioner.\par \par \uc2 SUMIO MABUCHI\par \par \uc2 The 51-year-old former transport minister told Reuters in July that Japan should reduce its reliance on nuclear power but that public debate is needed on whether to completely stop using it.\par \par \uc2 Mabuchi has also urged the Bank of Japan to adopt a policy of quantitative easing to escape deflation and underwrite bonds to be issued to pay for rebuilding tsunami-hit northeast Japan.\par \par \uc2 He is nicknamed the "Terminator" for his body-building hobby.\par \par \uc2 BANRI KAIEDA\par \par \uc2 Currently trade minister, Kaieda, 62, oversees Japan's energy policy and played a key role in efforts to bring the crippled Fukushima nuclear plant under control.\par \par \uc2 Kaieda has called for an improvement in nuclear safety, arguing that Kan's call to scrap nuclear power in the future is too soon and more debate is needed.\par \par \uc2 He has warned of the adverse effects of a hasty rise in the sales tax and called on the Bank of Japan to start discussing lifting a self-imposed ceiling on its government bond holdings, suggesting he wants more action from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEIJI MAEHARA\par \par \uc2 The former head of the party was previously seen as a top candidate to replace Kan before a funding scandal in March forced him to resign as foreign minister.\par \par \uc2 Maehara, 49, told Reuters that nuclear power plants should be scrapped by around 2030 and that Japan in the meantime will have to rely on atomic power. .\par \par \uc2 He also said in the interview that Japan should escape from deflation before raising the 5 percent sales tax to curb public debt.\par \par \uc2 An expert on security matters, Maehara has expressed concern about China's military buildup but has also said dialogue with Japan's giant neighbour is essential.\par \par \uc2 His hobbies include taking pictures of steam trains.\par \par \uc2 SAKIHITO OZAWA\par \par \uc2 Ozawa, 57, is the No.2 official of the party's key tax panel and has said he opposes tax hikes to fund the costs of rebuilding after the quake and tsunami in March, saying reconstruction should instead be funded by government borrowing.\par \par \uc2 SHINJI TARUTOKO\par \par \uc2 Tarukoto, 51, ran against Kan in a party leadership race last June but lost badly despite being backed by allies of party powerbroker Ichiro Ozawa. He has served as the party's chief of parliamentary affairs and is wary of tax hikes.\par \par \uc2 MICHIHIKO KANO\par \par \uc2 Agriculture Minister Kano, 69, has been sceptical of the merits of Japan joining the U.S.-led free trade pact called Trans-Pacific Partnership or TPP.\par \par \uc2 Although lacking in popular appeal, Kano, 69, might win the support of Democratic powerbroker Ichiro Ozawa and would probably make cooperation easier with the main opposition Liberal Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Shinichi Saoshiro, Editing by Linda Sieg and Michael Watson)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-POLITICS/SUCCESSORS (FACTBOX)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110823e78n00062\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
