\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: BOT Tipped To Raise Policy Rate At Wed Meeting - TMB\b0\par\par\uc2 1,580 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2011\par 05:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0307 GMT [Dow Jones] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  is likely to hike policy rate by 25 basis points to 3.50% at its meeting on Wednesday, although Thailand's 2Q GDP was weaker than expected. Disappointing GDP in the 2Q was mainly due to Japan's recent natural disaster that disrupted auto production in Thailand, says TMB Bank Vice President Naris Sathapholdeja. "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may consider the weak GDP is temporary, as the auto sector's production has already returned to normalcy; so they're likely to raise the rate," he adds, noting however, that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may pause rate hikes in the next two meetings in 2011 following pressure from the government, which has a pro-growth policy. The National Economic and Social Development Board said earlier that Thailand's 2Q GDP shrunk to 0.2% on-quarter but rose 2.6% on-year.(oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0254 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds are lower amid concerns the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may resume its efforts to drain net funds from the banking system this week via its weekly open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says a Shanghai-based trader with a local brokerage. CNY25 billion worth of repos and bills matures this week via the open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the smallest weekly maturing amount since early January, he notes. The Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index is at 129.13 vs its last close of 129.21; short-term support is likely at 10-day moving average of 129.11. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0253 GMT [Dow Jones] Indian government bonds are likely to open higher tracking US Treasury gains on Friday and may trade in a range with an upward bias, says a fixed income analyst with primary dealership. The benchmark 7.80% 2021 bond may open 97.05-97.10 vs 97.00 at Thursday's close; markets were shut for a local holiday on Friday. "The minutes of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's pre-July policy advisory committee meeting, released last week, showed a large number of people in favour of a dovish RBI stance. The uncertain economic outlook in the advanced countries may help support the view," he says. Minutes of the meeting revealed several members had suggested either a smaller 25 bps hike, or, taking a pause in the rate cycle in July, although the RBI raised rates by 50 basis points. An analyst tips yields on the 10-year paper to trade in a 8.15%-8.30% range vs 8.26% at last close. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0251 GMT [Dow Jones] Three-month copper trades 0.6% higher from its previous settlement on the London Metal Exchange on robust Chinese demand and expectations that stocks will continue to fall near term. Copper is at $8,875 a metric ton and is poised to extend its gains later in Asian trading on better demand from China, traders and analysts say. "The demand for both copper and aluminum is healthy in India and China," says a Tokyo-based analyst, citing falling stocks in Shanghai warehouses as a reason for more bullish sentiment in the market. "Investors and traders are hoping that falling inventories (on the) SHFE could push prices higher," he says. Traders expect arbitrage-buying to help support LME copper prices this week, although they say the contract will hit stubborn resistance at $9,000/ton.(clementine.wallop@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0248 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares are lower as some investors continue to take profit following Wall Street's decline Friday. The benchmark PSEi is down 0.5% at 4318.92 in thin volume. "We're seeing a follow-through selling in the absence of positive news that could drive the market," says Ignacio Soriano, research head with Papa Securities. Soriano adds that many investors are taking a wait-and-see approach before the release of 2Q GDP data later this month; next support is tipped at 4200, while resistance is at 4400. Most active stocks include Boulevard Holdings (BHI.PH), up 38.0% at PHP0.40; Lepanto Mining (LC.PH), up 1.3% at PHP1.56; and Banco De Oro, off 0.1% at PHP58.96. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0240 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi erases its earlier fall on bargain hunting and is last flat at 1744.74 in choppy trade, with utility stocks leading gains while techs also do well following their underperformances late last week, analysts say. They expect volatile trade in local shares to continue. Resistance is pegged near the intraday high so far of 1763.64, while support is at 1700. Analysts say shares of defensive sectors such as utilities will grow increasingly attractive valuation-wise should the outlook for the global economy darken further; Kepco (015760.SE) is +6.0% at KRW22,100 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kogas\f28 \uc2 \f2  (036460.SE) is +5.9% at KRW35,200. Among techs, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung Electronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  (005930.SE) rises 2.2% to KRW695,000 while \f28 \uc2 \f2 LG Electronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  (066570.SE) adds 2.0% to KRW56,100. Large-cap chemicals names are mostly lower, following the recent pullback in crude prices; \f28 \uc2 \f2 SK Innovation\f28 \uc2 \f2  (096770.SE) falls 2.8% to KRW139,000 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Honam Petrochemical\f28 \uc2 \f2  (011170.SE) is down 5.8% at KRW332,500. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0240 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei is up 0.2% at 8735.57 midday, supported by a relatively stable yen and positive performances in most other Asian bourses, says a market analyst at a Japanese securities firm. "Expectations for some type of (monetary policy) action by the U.S. Fed later in the week is fueling the short-covering of select oversold stocks," he says. He notes that while auto makers are underperforming, some technology shares are higher; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nikon\f28 \uc2 \f2  (7731.TO) is up 1.7% at Y1,637. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Toyota Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  (7203.TO) is down 1.6% at Y2,725 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Honda Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  (7267.TO) is down 0.9% at Y2,381, both hitting 2011 lows amid an uncertain global economic outlook and a strong yen. Kobayashi pegs the Nikkei's range at 8600-8800 for the remainder of the day. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fast Retailing\f28 \uc2 \f2  (9983.TO) is up 1.8% at Y15,020 after hitting a new 2011 high on steady business prospects. (judy.lam@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0237 GMT [Dow Jones] The spread between Nymex WTI and ICE Brent crude may narrow by $5-$10/bbl in the next few sessions as rebel forces enter Tripoli, bringing the possibility of an end to hostilities in Libya, Melbourne-based National Australian Bank economist Ben Westmore says; the spread is now around $24/bbl. An end to the civil war would likely result in a gradual restoration of 1.65 million barrels of crude exports from Libya. Nymex September crude gains 65 cents/bbl to $82.91/bbl; October ICE Brent is down $1.62 at $107.00 /bbl. (cheeyew.cheang@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0236 GMT [Dow Jones] The Reserve Bank of New Zealand's inflation expectations data Tuesday followed by trade data Wednesday and retail sales Thursday should confirm that the local economy is on a "sound footing and inflation pressures remain," says BNZ Head of Research Stephen Toplis. He expects reasonable growth in July exports and imports, year-on-year, with a seasonally impacted deficit of NZ$236 million, but enough to hold the annual balance in moderate surplus. However, Toplis expects markets to remain volatile and focused on global factors as the meeting of global central banks at Jackson Hole approaches.(arpan.mukherjee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY jumps briefly to 77.23 from 76.66, followed by an immediate decline to 76.82; the earlier spike was likely due to big non-Japanese banks buying the pair, say several Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers. The pair is now at 76.78; "it was one of those moves that you've seen often lately," one dealer says. Three of the dealers say there's only a low chance of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Monday, because \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  usually \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the early Tokyo hours.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei 225 September futures are up 0.5% at 8750 after reaching an intraday high of 8780 following a spike in USD/JPY to 77.20 from 76.66. But the gains were quickly trimmed as USD/JPY fell below 77 (now 76.77) with 4 dealers telling Dow Jones Newswires that Japanese authorities likely did not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. "It just shows how nervous investors are about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements," says Yutaka Miura, a senior technical analyst at Mizuho Securities, adding that the gains are mostly driven by short-covering by investors who had been selling on Friday. He says the futures index may fall back to its intraday low of 8670 in the afternoon as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  expectations wane. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 21, 2011 23:07 ET (03:07 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110822e78m000gt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
