\par\par \b \uc2 DJ UPDATE: Japan Noda Won't Rule Out Any Possible Measures To Counter Yen Strength\b0\par\par\uc2 423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2011\par 02:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --Finance minister Noda said will not rule out any possible measures\par \par \uc2 --Noda says worried about one-sided moves have strengthened\par \par \uc2 --Noda says will be in close coordination with the BOJ\par \par \uc2 (Adds Noda comments in paragraphs 4 and 6.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Tatsuo Ito and Mitsuru Obe \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's Finance Minister stepped up his rhetoric against the yen's strength Monday, saying the nation will not exclude any possible measures and take decisive steps to counter speculative moves.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I'm worried that recent one-sided yen moves have been strengthening further," Yoshihiko Noda told reporters. "We will not rule out every possible measure and will take decisive steps if needed," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda's remarks hinted at the possibility of another \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after the dollar fell to a new postwar low of Y75.94 at the end of last week amid concerns about the U.S. and European economies. The yen's resurge could threaten the country's fragile economy at a time when it has strived to recover from the March 11 disaster. The greenback stood at Y76.78 at 0023 GMT.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda also said he will closely watch the market to see if there are more speculative moves. The finance minister said Japan is in close contact with other Group of Seven industrial countries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Of course, we will be in close coordination" with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Noda added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In an interview with Dow Jones Newswires Friday, Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, Takehiko Nakao, said that he doesn't think unilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "is precluded as a tool to stabilize the markets," though he emphasized: "Consultation with other nations is required even for solo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many analysts say \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  backed up by further credit easing by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is more effective than \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  without the BOJ's action to stem the yen's sharp gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Given the circumstances, further easing by the BOJ, along with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , is a necessary step to stem the yen`s sharp rise," said Naoki Iizuka, senior economist at Mizuho Securites.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ will hold a two-day policy setting meeting on Sept. 6-7 but there is a possibility that it may bring forward the meeting if the yen strengthens again.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tatsuo Ito and Mitsuru Obe, Dow Jones Newswires; +813-6269-2780; tatsuo.ito@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 21, 2011 20:34 ET (00:34 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110822e78m00070\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
