\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-European stocks fall on debt woes, US fears; gold climbs\b0\par\par\uc2 870 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2011\par 03:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Stocks fall on U.S. recession, Europe debt fears\par \par \uc2 * Leading European stocks down 0.7 percent\par \par \uc2 * S&P futures 0.3 percent lower, Asia ex-Japan off 1.5 pct\par \par \uc2 * Gold contines to set fresh highs on safe-haven bets\par \par \uc2 * Oil falls over 3 pct on hopes for end to Libyan civil war\par \par \uc2 (Updates throughout, adds European market open)\par \par \uc2 By Frederik Richter\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Aug 22 (Reuters) - European stocks extended four weeks of losses on Monday, tracking jittery Asian shares lower, while gold shot to new highs as investors worried about the sluggish U.S. economic outlook and Europe's festering debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 The FTSEurofirst 300 index of top European shares fell 0.7 percent in early trade, tracking Friday's losses on Wall Street on worries that the U.S. economy may slide back into recession.\par \par \uc2 Spot gold prices rose more than 1 percent to a record\par \par \uc2 $1,882.20 per ounce as the shaky global outlook prompted investors to move more money into safe havens, while oil prices tumbled on hopes Libya may resume full output soon as a six-month civil war seemed to be nearing an end.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei 225 index fell 1 percent, with increasing expectations that Tokyo will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the strong yen offsetting growing worries that the U.S. economy may be sliding back into a recession.\par \par \uc2 Shares elsewhere in the region as measured by the MSCI Asia Pacific ex-Japan index fell more than\par \par \uc2 1 percent, unable to hold on to early advances.\par \par \uc2 A similar pattern was seen in S&P 500 futures <ESc1>, which recoiled from early gains to slip 0.4 percent , pointing to more losses in Western markets later in the day.\par \par \uc2 "Given the economic hurdles faced in Europe and the United States, an upward trend is hard to sustain," said Park Yong-myung, a fund manager at Hanhwa Investment Trust Management.\par \par \uc2 Adding to the bearish tone, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Council member Ewald Nowotny told an Austrian magazine he was concerned that euro zone countries will not push through parliamentary approval of changes to their EFSF bailout fund as quickly as planned.\par \par \uc2 Member states have to adopt revisions to the European Financial Stability Facility (EFSF) so that it can take over from the ECB the job of buying debt of struggling members on secondary markets if needed.\par \par \uc2 Khiem Do, head of Asian multi-asset with \uc2 Baring Asset Management\uc2  in Hong Kong, said there would be little visibility on the direction of markets until it was clear whether the United States would slide into recession or not.\par \par \uc2 "The sentiment of markets is very weak at the moment," he said.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's world stock index fell 0.5 percent. The index has slipped into a bear market territory by dropping more than 20 percent from its three-year high in May.\par \par \uc2 BERNANKE IN FOCUS\par \par \uc2 A key event this week will be a speech by Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke on Aug. 26 in Jackson Hole, Wyoming, during which he is expected to provide an economic outlook and hints on how policymakers plan to handle the turmoil in financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Bernanke used the same event last year to suggest the Fed could help growth by buying long-term bonds, but no major announcements are expected this time.(For a preview of Bernanke's speech, see )\par \par \uc2 On Friday, U.S. stocks fell after \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2 's weaker outlook and corporate shakeup added to uncertainty for investors after a month of bad surprises ranging from a U.S. credit rating downgrade to a sharp slowdown in world growth.\par \par \uc2 Markets will also watch data on bond buying by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and debt issuance by European countries such as Italy on Tuesday to see if the euro zone's debt crisis is worsening.\par \par \uc2 Also on Tuesday, a raft of preliminary manufacturing data will shed light on whether economies from China to the euro zone are continuing to lose momentum, adding to global growth fears.\par \par \uc2 Brent oil futures <LCOc1> tumbled more than 3 percent at one point to $105.15 a barrel, weighed down by a firmer U.S. dollar and as the months-long conflict in oil-producing Libya appeared to enter its decisive phase, with rebel fighters streaming into the heart of Tripoli.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index , which tracks the strength of the greenback against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rose 0.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 The dollar surged higher against the yen, but later pared some of its gains, with traders citing talk that the spike in the dollar was triggered by bids by a U.S. bank.\par \par \uc2 The move came as investors were increasingly on edge about the possibility that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb yen strength, in the wake of the dollar's drop down to a record low around 75.95 yen late last week.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was largely flat against the yen at 76.71 yen\par \par \uc2 , having risen to as high as 77.23 yen earlier.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Ayai Tomisawa in Tokyo and Ju-min Park in Seoul; Editing by Kim Coghill and Ramya Venugopal)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-GLOBAL/ (WRAPUP 5)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110822e78m0001c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
