nment relief measures last July, the Census and Statistics Department said Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA RESEARCHER, OFFICIAL ADD TO ECONOMIC POLICY DEBATE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China should take measures to spur the domestic economy instead of cooling it as the ongoing U.S. debt crisis will affect the Asian country, Wang Jian, secretary general of the National Development and Reform Commission's macroeconomic research unit, wrote in an article in the China Securities Journal Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA FINANCE MINISTER FLAGS RISKS TO 2011 GROWTH, INFLATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan on Monday signaled risks to the government's 2011 growth and inflation forecasts, underscoring difficulties faced by Asia's fourth-largest economy amid persistent inflation and heightened external uncertainties.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ:THAI POLITICIAN'S TRIP TO JAPAN STIRS QUESTIONS FOR SISTER'S GOVERNMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ousted Thai leader Thaksin Shinawatra's rush back onto the international stage with a trip to Japan scheduled to begin Monday is raising awkward questions for his sister, recently elected Prime Minister Yingluck Shinawatra, about who really runs the country.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA DEVELOPMENT BANK TO SELL CNY36 BLN BILLS, BONDS THURSDAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Development Bank Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said Monday it plans to auction CNY16 billion worth of one-year bills and CNY20 billion worth of three-year floating-rate bonds Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: SINGAPORE JULY CPI LIKELY 5.0%; PRICES MAY BE FIRMING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's consumer prices likely remained at elevated levels because of a tight local labor market and rising commodity prices, prompting economists to warn that inflation momentum in th