\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 706 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2011\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INTERVIEW: RBA'S KRAEHE: HISTORY SHOWS AUSTRALIAN RISK OF POST-BOOM RECESSION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's economy risks being hollowed out by the country's mining boom and faces the prospect of a downturn once commodity prices fall, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  board member and \f28 \uc2 \f2 BlueScope Steel Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . chairman Graham Kraehe said Monday. (published 0251 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S PM SAYS STRONG A$ PRESSURING EXPORTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The strong Australian dollar is squeezing export-oriented industries and the pressure is likely to continue, Prime Minister Julia Gillard said Monday. (published 0450 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN NODA WON'T RULE OUT ANY POSSIBLE MEASURES TO COUNTER YEN STRENGTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Finance Minister stepped up his rhetoric against the yen's strength Monday, saying the nation will not exclude any possible measures and take decisive steps to counter speculative moves. (published 0034 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  KAIEDA: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  COULD HAVE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENED\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AT TOKYO MARKET OPENING - REPORT \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's trade minister issued fresh warnings against speculators buying the yen, saying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market by now today, the Sankei Shimbun reported Monday. (published 0143 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MOODY'S: EURO-ZONE MEMBERS' RETICENCE PUTS PRESSURE ON FRANCE, GERMANY TO PROTECT EMU \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The reticence of some euro-zone policy makers to act to protect the stability of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bloc puts pressure on France and Germany to take more aggressive steps than their recent proposals, a senior analyst at Moody's Investors Service said Monday. (published 0351 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par 2ND UPDATE: THAI GDP SHRINKS IN 2Q; GOVERNMENT TRIMS 2011 GROWTH FORECAST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Thai economy contracted in the second quarter as supply disruptions in the auto industry caused by the natural disasters in Japan took its toll on Southeast Asia's second-largest economy. (published 0437 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC OFFICIAL: CHINA SHOULD CONSIDER 'ASYMMETRIC' RATE HIKES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should consider asymmetric interest rate hikes to better balance its economy, Yang Ziqiang, head of the Jinan branch of the People's Bank of China, wrote in the Financial News Monday. (published 0325 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA RESEARCHER: SHOULD TAKE MEASURES TO WARM UP ECONOMY - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China should take measures to warm up the domestic economy instead of cooling it as the ongoing U.S. crisis will strongly affect China, Wang Jian, secretary general of the National Development and Reform Commission's macroeconomic research unit, wrote in an article in the China Securities Journal Monday. (published 2338 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN UNIONS SAY MANUFACTURING IN WORST CRISIS SINCE DEPRESSION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's manufacturing industry is in its worst crisis since the Great Depression and Canberra should pressure Beijing to float its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Australian unions said Monday after local steelmakers announced plans to cut 1,400 jobs. (published 2346 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA FINANCE MINISTER FLAGS RISKS TO 2011 GROWTH, INFLATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan on Monday signaled risks to the government's 2011 growth and inflation forecasts, underscoring difficulties faced by Asia's fourth-largest economy amid persistent inflation and heightened external uncertainties. (published 0100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ UPDATE: THAI POLITICIAN'S TRIP TO JAPAN STIRS QUESTIONS FOR SISTER'S GOVERNMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ousted Thai leader Thaksin Shinawatra's rush back onto the international stage with a trip to Japan scheduled to begin Monday is raising awkward questions for his sister, recently elected Prime Minister Yingluck Shinawatra, about who really runs the country. (published 2330 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA DEVELOPMENT BANK TO SELL CNY36 BLN BILLS, BONDS THURSDAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Development Bank Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said Monday it plans to auction CNY16 billion worth of one-year bills and CNY20 billion worth of three-year floating-rate bonds Thursday. (published 0232 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par XINJIANG GOLDWIND PLANS TO SELL CNY4 BLN BONDS IN SEPTEMBER - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese wind-turbine maker Xinjiang Goldwind Science & Technology Co. plans to sell CNY4 billion worth of bonds on the Shenzhen Stock Exchange in September, a person familiar with the situation said Monday. (published 0112 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: SINGAPORE JULY CPI LIKELY 5.0%; PRICES MAY BE FIRMING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's consumer prices likely remained at elevated levels because of 