\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,002 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2011\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INTERVIEW: RBA'S KRAEHE: HISTORY SHOWS AUSTRALIAN RISK OF POST-BOOM RECESSION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's economy risks being hollowed out by the country's mining boom and faces the prospect of a downturn once commodity prices fall, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  board member and \f28 \uc2 \f2 BlueScope Steel Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . chairman Graham Kraehe said Monday. (published 0251 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: JAPAN NODA WON'T RULE OUT ANY POSSIBLE MEASURES TO COUNTER YEN STRENGTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Finance Minister stepped up his rhetoric against the yen's strength Monday, saying the nation will not exclude any possible measures and take decisive steps to counter speculative moves. (published 0034 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MOODY'S: EURO-ZONE MEMBERS' RETICENCE PUTS PRESSURE ON FRANCE, GERMANY TO PROTECT EMU \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The reticence of some euro-zone policy makers to act to protect the stability of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bloc puts pressure on France and Germany to take more aggressive steps than their recent proposals, a senior analyst at Moody's Investors Service said Monday. (published 0351 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: THAI ECONOMY SHRINKS IN 2Q; TRIMS TOP-END OF 2011 GDP FORECAST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Thai economy contracted in the second quarter as supply disruptions in the auto industry caused by the natural disasters in Japan took its toll on Southeast Asia's second-largest economy. (published 0354 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  KAIEDA: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  COULD HAVE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENED\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  AT TOKYO MARKET OPENING - REPORT \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's trade minister issued fresh warnings against speculators buying the yen, saying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market by now today, the Sankei Shimbun reported Monday. (published 0143 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA RESEARCHER: US MAY BE ON ITS WAY TO DEFAULT ON DEBT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. may be on its way to default on its debt despite the U.S. government's ability to print more money, a Chinese think tank researcher said Monday. (published 2357 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PBOC OFFICIAL: CHINA SHOULD CONSIDER 'ASYMMETRIC' RATE HIKES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should consider asymmetric interest rate hikes to better balance its economy, Yang Ziqiang, head of the Jinan branch of the People's Bank of China, wrote in the Financial News Monday. (published 0325 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA RESEARCHER: SHOULD TAKE MEASURES TO WARM UP ECONOMY - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China should take measures to warm up the domestic economy instead of cooling it as the ongoing U.S. crisis will strongly affect China, Wang Jian, secretary general of the National Development and Reform Commission's macroeconomic research unit, wrote in an article in the China Securities Journal Monday. (published 2338 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN UNIONS SAY MANUFACTURING IN WORST CRISIS SINCE DEPRESSION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's manufacturing industry is in its worst crisis since the Great Depression and Canberra should pressure Beijing to float its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Australian unions said Monday after local steelmakers announced plans to cut 1,400 jobs. (published 2346 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA FINANCE MINISTER FLAGS RISKS TO 2011 GROWTH, INFLATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korean Finance Minister Bahk Jae-wan on Monday signaled risks to the government's 2011 growth and inflation forecasts, underscoring difficulties faced by Asia's fourth-largest economy amid persistent inflation and heightened external uncertainties. (published 0100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA 1H DIRECT OVERSEAS INVESTMENT HITS RECORD-HIGH $26.9B \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's direct investment overseas more than doubled to a record-high of $26.9 billion in the first half of the year, led by energy development projects, the Ministry of Strategy and Finance said Monday. (published 0046 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SOUTH KOREA 2Q HOUSEHOLD CREDIT AT RECORD-HIGH KRW876.3T \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's outstanding household credit rose to its highest level on record in the second quarter, posing a dilemma for policymakers in fostering economic growth while keeping interest rates low. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ UPDATE: THAI POLITICIAN'S TRIP TO JAPAN STIRS QUESTIONS FOR SISTER'S GOVERNMENT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ousted Thai leader Thaksin Shinawatra's rush back onto the international stage with a trip to Japan scheduled to begin Monday is raising awkward questions for his sister, recently elected Prime Minister Yingluck Shinawatra, about who really runs the country. (published 2330 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VIETNAM HO CHI MINH CITY AUG CPI RISES 0.68% ON MONTH - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consumer prices in Ho Chi Minh City in August rose 0.68% from the previous month, led by increases in the cost of food, housing and building materials, state media said Monday. (published 0246 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA DEVELOPMENT BANK TO SELL CNY36 BLN BILLS, BONDS THURSDAY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Development Bank Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said Monday it plans to auction CNY16 billion worth of one-year bills and CNY20 billion worth of three-year floating-rate bonds Thursday. (published 0232 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par XINJIANG GOLDWIND PLANS TO SELL CNY4 BLN BONDS IN SEPTEMBER - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese wind-turbine maker Xinjiang Goldwind Science & Technology Co. plans to sell CNY4 billion worth of bonds on the Shenzhen Stock Exchange in September, a person familiar with the situation said Monday. (published 0112 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WHEELOCK & CO PRICES S$350M 10-YEAR BOND ISSUE - TERM SHEET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong-listed Wheelock & Co. Ltd. has priced a S$350 million 10-year bond issue, according to a term-sheet seen by Dow Jones Newswires Monday. (published 0306 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ UPDATE: REBELS SWEEP INTO TRIPOLI \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Libyan rebels poured into Tripoli on Sunday after seizing a nearby military base, as fears of a bloody battle largely gave way to scenes of jubilant opposition fighters surging into the city's center and meeting little resistance from Col. Moammar Gadhafi's defenses. (published 0302 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: STRAUSS-KAHN CASE MAY BE DROPPED - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New York prosecutors are likely to seek dismissal of all criminal sexual assault charges against former International Monetary Fund chief Dominique Strauss-Kahn on Tuesday, a person familiar with the matter said. (published 0400 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: SINGAPORE JULY CPI LIKELY 5.0%; PRICES MAY BE FIRMING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's c